/* Guided-mode focus packs: big idea, why it matters, plain-words gist per part, core/extra flag, things to remember. Guidance only; the lesson text is unchanged. */
window.FOCUS=Object.assign(window.FOCUS||{},{"p10m1":{"big":"Radio is bookkeeping: start with transmit power, add antenna gains, subtract every loss along the path, and compare what arrives with the noise floor.","why":"With a few lines of dB arithmetic you can predict a link on paper before you buy a part, explain a dead zone, and stay within what the law lets you transmit and receive.","parts":[{"t":"Frequency and wavelength","gist":"Wavelength is the length of one wave cycle; in metres it is 300 divided by the frequency in MHz. Antennas are sized to it, so 2.4 GHz uses tiny antennas while 125 kHz tags use a coil.","core":true},{"t":"The one trade-off that explains everything","gist":"Higher frequency carries more data but has shorter range and gets through walls worse. Lower frequency travels farther but carries less. Choosing a band is really choosing a point on this trade-off.","core":true},{"t":"The ISM bands","gist":"Most consumer wireless uses license-free ISM bands, where anyone may transmit within strict power limits but must put up with interference. Crowding in 2.4 GHz is the single biggest cause of slow home Wi-Fi.","core":true},{"t":"dB and dBm","gist":"A decibel is a ratio: +3 dB doubles power and +10 dB is ten times. dBm is power compared with 1 milliwatt, so 20 dBm is 100 mW. Because decibels add, link math becomes simple arithmetic.","core":true},{"t":"Free-space path loss and the link budget","gist":"A link budget follows the signal end to end: transmit power plus antenna gains, minus path loss and walls. Subtract the noise floor from what arrives to get the SNR, the signal-to-noise ratio that decides speed.","core":true},{"t":"Antennas, polarization and the Fresnel zone","gist":"Antenna gain focuses energy into a direction; it doesn't add power. Both antennas need the same polarization, and an outdoor link needs the football-shaped Fresnel zone around the path kept at least 60% clear.","core":false},{"t":"Modulation in one page","gist":"Data rides on a carrier by changing its amplitude, frequency or phase. Packing more bits into each symbol, as 1024-QAM does, needs a cleaner signal, so radios keep trading speed for robustness as conditions change.","core":false},{"t":"The law, which you must know","gist":"Listening is generally legal; transmitting is regulated. In the US the FCC's Part 15 covers unlicensed devices and Part 97 licensed hams. Jamming is illegal nearly everywhere, and intercepting private calls or data is a crime.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Wavelength in metres is 300 divided by frequency in MHz, so 2.4 GHz is about 12.5 cm.","part":0},{"p":"+3 dB doubles power, +10 dB is ten times; 0 dBm is 1 mW, 20 dBm is 100 mW. Two 20 dBm signals make 23 dBm, not 40.","part":3},{"p":"Free-space loss costs about 6 dB every time distance doubles and 20 dB for every factor of 10.","part":4},{"p":"SNR is received signal minus noise floor: -60 dBm against a -94 dBm floor gives 34 dB.","part":4},{"p":"Part 15 devices get no protection from interference, jamming is illegal, and intercepting private communications is a crime even with a receive-only radio.","part":7}]},"p10m2":{"big":"Wi-Fi is a shared radio where only one device talks at a time, so speed depends on airtime, channel planning and placement, not bars or a bigger router.","why":"\"The Wi-Fi is slow\" is nearly always airtime, channel planning or placement, so this lets you fix it with engineering instead of a bigger router, and secure your own network properly.","parts":[{"t":"The generations","gist":"Each Wi-Fi generation, from Wi-Fi 4 to Wi-Fi 7, is faster on paper, but real throughput is a third to a half of the peak. Wi-Fi 7's true ceiling is about 23 Gb/s, not the marketed 46.","core":true},{"t":"The PHY: OFDM and where the rate comes from","gist":"A channel is split into hundreds of narrow subcarriers, and the speed is plain arithmetic: subcarriers times bits per symbol times coding rate, divided by symbol time, times spatial streams. Higher modulation needs more SNR.","core":true},{"t":"The MAC: CSMA/CA and airtime","gist":"Only one device on a channel can talk at a time, so the real shared resource is airtime. One slow or distant client, legacy rates, or too many SSIDs sending beacons can use up most of it.","core":true},{"t":"Channels","gist":"On 2.4 GHz only channels 1, 6 and 11 don't overlap, so run 20 MHz there. 5 and 6 GHz have many more channels, but some 5 GHz ones are shared with radar and must listen for it first (DFS).","core":true},{"t":"Wi-Fi 6E and Wi-Fi 7 in practice","gist":"Wi-Fi 6's OFDMA serves many small devices in one transmission; 6E is the same on 6 GHz. Wi-Fi 7 adds Multi-Link Operation across bands. At home the real wins are clean 6 GHz spectrum and lower latency, not peak rate.","core":false},{"t":"Joining a network","gist":"A device finds a network from its beacons, then authenticates, associates, and finally runs the security handshake that creates the encryption keys. Only after that handshake is traffic protected.","core":false},{"t":"RSSI, SNR, channel planning and roaming","gist":"Design to SNR, not bars: about -67 dBm and 25 dB SNR for voice and video. Same-channel APs share airtime, overlapping channels corrupt each other, and roaming is the client's choice, so plan cell overlap.","core":true},{"t":"Survey your own network","gist":"A site survey checks what is really happening: walk the space and record signal, SNR, how many networks share the channel, and retries. Retries above about 10% or SNR under 20 dB mark trouble.","core":true},{"t":"The security evolution","gist":"WEP and WPA are broken. WPA2-Personal uses AES, but one recorded handshake lets an attacker test passphrase guesses offline. WPA3's SAE stops offline guessing, and Protected Management Frames stop forged disconnects.","core":true},{"t":"802.1X and EAP: per-user Wi-Fi","gist":"802.1X gives each user their own login through a RADIUS server instead of one shared passphrase, so you can lock out one person. EAP-TLS, with certificates on both sides and no passwords, is the gold standard.","core":false},{"t":"Segment and monitor what you cannot trust","gist":"Put trusted devices, IoT and guests on separate VLANs with firewall rules on the router, so IoT can never start connections into your trusted devices. Then watch the logs. Forging disconnects against others' networks is illegal.","core":true},{"t":"Hardening your own Wi-Fi (defensive configuration)","gist":"The practical checklist for a network you own: WPA3, a long random passphrase of 16 or more characters, WPS off, firmware updated, default admin password changed, and guests and IoT on their own SSID.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"PHY rate = data subcarriers × bits per subcarrier × coding rate ÷ symbol time × spatial streams.","part":1},{"p":"Airtime is the shared resource: disable the 1-11 Mb/s rates and keep SSIDs to three or fewer.","part":2},{"p":"In the US only 1, 6 and 11 are clean 2.4 GHz channels; always run 20 MHz there.","part":3},{"p":"Design targets: -67 dBm and 25 dB SNR for voice and video, -70 dBm for general data.","part":6},{"p":"WPA2-Personal passphrases can be guessed offline from one recorded handshake, so length decides everything; WPA3's SAE needs a live exchange for each guess.","part":8}]},"p10m3":{"big":"Bluetooth is two radios under one name: Classic for continuous streaming, and Low Energy for short bursts that let a sensor run for years on a coin cell.","why":"Understanding advertising intervals, GATT and pairing methods is how you read, build and battery-size sensors on your Pi, and how you tell weak pairing from strong before trusting a device.","parts":[{"t":"Classic vs Low Energy","gist":"Bluetooth Classic streams audio and files at 1-3 Mb/s with higher power; BLE sends short bursts and can last years on a coin cell. Both hop between channels in 2.4 GHz to dodge interference.","core":true},{"t":"The link layer: advertising and connections","gist":"A BLE device either advertises, waking to send short packets on channels 37, 38 and 39 and then sleeping, or holds a connection, waking at an agreed interval. These timing numbers decide battery life.","core":true},{"t":"The stack: GAP and GATT","gist":"GAP handles finding and connecting devices; GATT handles data once connected. A device offers services, each holding characteristics, the actual values such as heart rate, which can be read, written or pushed as notifications.","core":true},{"t":"Throughput and battery arithmetic","gist":"Battery life is average current: sleep current plus charge per event times events per second. For one example sensor, advertising every 100 ms instead of once a second turns about 1.5 years on a coin cell into about 62 days.","core":true},{"t":"Pairing and bonding","gist":"Pairing creates keys and bonding stores them so devices reconnect later. Just Works can't detect a man-in-the-middle; Passkey Entry and Numeric Comparison can. A listener who records LE Secure Connections pairing learns nothing.","core":true},{"t":"Range, classes and security","gist":"Most consumer devices are Class 2 and reach about 10 m; Class 1 reaches about 100 m. Security depends on the pairing method, so keep firmware current, prefer strong pairing, and turn Bluetooth off when unused.","core":true},{"t":"What's new: LE Audio, Channel Sounding, and versions","gist":"A tour of recent versions: LE Audio and Auracast broadcast for better, shareable sound, and Bluetooth 6.0's Channel Sounding, which measures distance to tens of centimetres for car keys and item finders.","core":false},{"t":"Coexistence and troubleshooting","gist":"Wi-Fi and Bluetooth often share one chip and antenna, so heavy 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi can make a headset stutter; moving the laptop to 5 or 6 GHz fixes it. USB 3 noise near 2.4 GHz also upsets dongles.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Classic uses 79 channels 1 MHz wide; BLE uses 40 channels 2 MHz wide, with 37, 38 and 39 for advertising.","part":0},{"p":"In GATT, services contain characteristics: the Heart Rate service 0x180D holds the Heart Rate Measurement characteristic 0x2A37.","part":2},{"p":"BLE battery life is sleep current plus charge per event times events per second, so advertising faster multiplies the drain.","part":3},{"p":"Pairing makes keys and bonding stores them; Just Works offers no man-in-the-middle protection, while Passkey Entry and Numeric Comparison do.","part":4},{"p":"Class 2 Bluetooth reaches about 10 m and Class 1 about 100 m.","part":5}]},"p10m4":{"big":"A Raspberry Pi is a full Linux computer built around one chip, and making it reliable comes down to proper power, storage and cooling plus careful service setup.","why":"It is your hands-on lab for every domain and a small server you can deploy for clients; getting power, storage and security right is how it runs for years without corrupting its card or getting compromised.","parts":[{"t":"The model line","gist":"A Pi (4, 5 or Zero) runs full Linux, while a Pico is a microcontroller with no operating system, for direct hardware control. Buy the RAM the job needs: 1-2 GB covers Pi-hole or a VPN.","core":true},{"t":"Inside the SoC: BCM2712 and RP1","gist":"The Pi 5 puts the CPU, GPU and memory controller on one chip, and a second chip, RP1, runs USB, Ethernet and GPIO. That is why old RPi.GPIO code fails on a Pi 5.","core":true},{"t":"The boot chain","gist":"There is no BIOS. A ROM loads a bootloader from a small EEPROM chip on the board, which follows BOOT_ORDER to SD, USB or NVMe, reads config.txt and the device tree, then starts Linux and systemd.","core":true},{"t":"OS images and headless setup","gist":"Raspberry Pi OS is Debian-based. For a headless setup, with no monitor, use Imager to set the hostname, a user, the Wi-Fi country and SSH with your public key, then connect over ssh.","core":true},{"t":"GPIO and the buses","gist":"The 40-pin GPIO header links the Pi to the physical world, with power pins and the I2C, SPI and UART buses for sensors, displays and serial devices. GPIO is 3.3 V, and wrong wiring damages the Pi.","core":true},{"t":"Storage, cooling, power and upgrades","gist":"Power is the classic trap: the Pi 5 wants 5 V at 5 A, and weak supplies or thin cables cause mysterious crashes. NVMe beats microSD for speed and wear, and the Pi throttles around 80-85 °C.","core":true},{"t":"Running services reliably","gist":"A Pi that serves others must survive crashes and power cuts: run each service as a systemd unit that restarts on failure, cap the logs, enable the hardware watchdog, and test-restore your backups.","core":true},{"t":"Securing a Pi you expose on your network","gist":"Treat an exposed Pi as a Linux server: SSH keys only, automatic security updates, a default-deny firewall, and WireGuard or Tailscale instead of forwarding ports from the internet.","core":true},{"t":"What to build (a menu)","gist":"A menu of projects, from Pi-hole and file servers to media servers, sensor loggers and AI vision boxes. Starting with Pi-hole or a file server teaches real Linux administration.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A Pico is a microcontroller with no OS; a Pi 4, 5 or Zero runs full Linux.","part":0},{"p":"On a Pi 5, GPIO sits on the RP1 chip behind PCIe, so use gpiozero or libgpiod, not RPi.GPIO.","part":1},{"p":"BOOT_ORDER is read right to left: 0xf41 tries SD, then USB, then restarts; 0xf416 puts NVMe first.","part":2},{"p":"Pi GPIO is 3.3 V and not 5 V-tolerant, and physical pin numbers differ from GPIO numbers.","part":4},{"p":"vcgencmd get_throttled reports under-voltage and throttling; if those bits are set, fix the power supply before debugging software.","part":5}]},"p10m5":{"big":"RF multitools and SDRs are teachers, not magic keys: rolling codes and encrypted cards resist replay, and the law draws clear lines on transmitting and intercepting.","why":"Knowing precisely what these tools can and can't do, and the law around them, is what separates an RF engineer from someone with an expensive toy and a legal problem.","parts":[{"t":"What's inside","gist":"The Flipper Zero packs a Sub-GHz radio, 125 kHz RFID, 13.56 MHz NFC, infrared, iButton, Bluetooth, GPIO pins and USB modes, each with a legitimate use on devices you own.","core":true},{"t":"RFID vs NFC vs Sub-GHz","gist":"RFID is the broad family of tag technologies across several bands. NFC is a very short-range branch of 13.56 MHz RFID, about 4 cm, behind tap-to-pay. Sub-GHz is where garage remotes and key fobs live.","core":true},{"t":"Why modern systems resist replay","gist":"A fixed-code remote sends the same bits every press, so a recording can be replayed. A rolling code uses a counter and a secret key, so a recorded code is already used up. Current cards use AES.","core":true},{"t":"What it cannot do (the myths)","gist":"The Flipper can't defeat modern rolling codes or encrypted challenge-response systems by replay, can't clone bank cards, and isn't a Wi-Fi radio or spectrum analyzer. Weak legacy systems are now the exception.","core":true},{"t":"The software-defined radio: a receiver made of math","gist":"A software-defined radio turns a slice of spectrum into IQ samples, number pairs carrying amplitude and phase, and does the rest in software. At 2.4 million samples per second you see a 2.4 MHz window.","core":true},{"t":"Legal receive-only projects","gist":"Lawful receive-only projects include broadcast FM, airband voice, NOAA Weather Radio, aircraft ADS-B at 1,090 MHz and Meteor-M weather satellites. ADS-B range is a horizon problem, so antenna height beats gain.","core":false},{"t":"The law, again","gist":"Use these tools only on systems you own or are authorized to test. Transmitting is regulated and interference is illegal. For receivers the line is interception, so private calls and data are off limits.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"All NFC is 13.56 MHz RFID, but most RFID is not NFC.","part":1},{"p":"Rolling codes encrypt an incrementing counter with a shared secret, so a replayed press does nothing; old 125 kHz tags just broadcast a fixed ID.","part":2},{"p":"The Flipper cannot clone EMV bank cards or defeat modern rolling codes by replay.","part":3},{"p":"An 8-bit SDR converter has about 50 dB of dynamic range, so too much gain lets strong nearby signals overload it.","part":4},{"p":"Receiving broadcast, weather, aircraft and amateur signals is lawful; intercepting cellular calls or private data is a crime.","part":6}]},"p10m6":{"big":"A cyberdeck is a portable computer designed around one purpose, and the real craft is the power and heat budget; the box comes last.","why":"Building one makes you use hardware, Linux, networking, power electronics and security together on a real object, and a survey deck turns 'the Wi-Fi is bad' into a measured, billable report.","parts":[{"t":"What makes it a cyberdeck, not a laptop","gist":"A laptop is a general-purpose compromise; a cyberdeck starts from one question: what is this machine for? That answer drives every other choice, and decks are built to be repairable and expandable.","core":true},{"t":"The compute board","gist":"The Pi 5 is the usual choice: fast, with NVMe support, but it draws up to about 12 W and needs cooling and a 5 V/5 A supply. Smaller boards sip power; x86 mini PCs run anything but use more.","core":true},{"t":"Display and input","gist":"Pick the screen and keyboard for the job: 7-inch touch panels are the default, and e-ink is superb outdoors. A bright backlight can be the single largest power draw, so dimming it saves real watts.","core":false},{"t":"Power: the budget that decides the build","gist":"Add up every part's draw and compare it with the battery's energy. A 20,000 mAh bank at 3.7 V holds about 74 Wh, so a 15 W deck runs roughly 4-5 hours after conversion losses.","core":true},{"t":"Power engineering in depth","gist":"Typical USB-C power banks give only 5 V at 3 A, so a Pi 5 limits its USB ports to 600 mA. Serious builds use a fused cell pack with a battery-management system, short thick wires and a fan.","core":true},{"t":"Enclosure, connectivity and the build","gist":"Prototype the electronics on the bench, measure the real power draw and heat with a USB-C meter and a stress test, and only then design the case around the measured parts.","core":true},{"t":"What people actually use them for","gist":"Real uses include field survey kits, SDR radio stations, off-grid messaging, writing and development machines, and data loggers. The hardware changes nothing about the law: only test networks you own or are authorized to test.","core":true},{"t":"Fixed builds: home-lab services and sensor networks","gist":"The same design questions apply to builds that never move, like a Pi on one PoE cable running home-lab services, or a gateway collecting readings from many cheap sensor nodes.","core":false},{"t":"Where to learn and share","gist":"Where the community gathers: cyberdeck.cafe, r/cyberDeck, Hackaday and YouTube build logs. Study ten builds before you start, then write your own build log.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Energy in watt-hours is mAh × cell voltage: 20,000 mAh at 3.7 V is 74 Wh. Subtract 10-20% conversion loss, then divide by measured watts.","part":3},{"p":"USB-PD's standard profiles stop at 3 A at 5 V, so a Pi 5 on a power bank limits its USB ports to 600 mA.","part":4},{"p":"At 5 A every milliohm of wire costs 5 mV, so keep the 5 V run short and thick.","part":4},{"p":"Nearly every watt the deck draws becomes heat inside the case; without airflow the Pi throttles at 80-85 °C.","part":4},{"p":"Build order: prototype on the bench, measure real draw and temperature, then design the case around the measured parts.","part":5}]},"p10m7":{"big":"Parts connect safely only when volts, milliamps and timing match: keep 5 V off 3.3 V pins, limit current, and use the bus that fits the job.","why":"These rules are the difference between a project that works for years and a Pi that smokes on day one, and PoE budgets are everyday access-point installation work.","parts":[{"t":"Logic levels and noise margins","gist":"A pin compares a voltage with thresholds: for 3.3 V logic it reads high above 2.0 V and low below 0.8 V. A 5 V signal exceeds a Pi pin's absolute maximum, so never apply 5 V to GPIO.","core":true},{"t":"Current limits, Ohm's law and the LED resistor","gist":"Each Pi pin should supply at most 16 mA, and all GPIO together about 50 mA. An LED needs a series resistor: R = (Vsupply - Vf) ÷ I, where Vf is the LED's forward voltage.","core":true},{"t":"Driving real loads: transistors, MOSFETs, relays and motors","gist":"Anything needing more than a few milliamps gets a transistor or logic-level MOSFET as a switch between the pin and the load. Relays and motors need a flyback diode, and motors need their own supply and driver.","core":true},{"t":"Pull-ups, open-drain and floating inputs","gist":"An input wired to nothing floats and reads random values, so a pull-up resistor gives it a defined idle state. Open-drain outputs can only pull low, which is how many I2C devices share two wires.","core":true},{"t":"Level shifting","gist":"To read a slow 5 V signal on a Pi, a resistor divider works. Fast one-way signals need a buffer chip, and two-way buses like I2C need a dedicated translator.","core":false},{"t":"Debouncing","gist":"Switch contacts bounce for 1-10 ms, so one press can look like a dozen. Smooth it with a resistor and capacitor, or in software accept a new state only after several identical readings.","core":false},{"t":"PWM","gist":"PWM switches a pin fully on and off quickly, and the share of time it is on, the duty cycle, sets the average. Linux timing jitters, so use hardware PWM or a microcontroller for precise work.","core":false},{"t":"I2C, read as a timing diagram","gist":"I2C shares two pulled-up wires among devices that each have an address. A transaction runs START, address, ACK, data, STOP. Pull-up size and wire capacitance limit speed, and i2cdetect lists who answers.","core":true},{"t":"SPI","gist":"SPI is a fast, full-duplex link with a clock, two data lines and a chip-select per device. It has no addressing or acknowledgement, so a missing device simply reads as all 1s or all 0s.","core":true},{"t":"UART","gist":"UART needs no clock wire: both ends agree on a baud rate, and each byte travels as ten bits. Wire TX to RX, RX to TX and ground to ground; RS-232 voltages will destroy a Pi.","core":true},{"t":"PoE: power over the network cable","gist":"PoE sends power over the Ethernet cable, and the cable loses some on the way. After that loss, 802.3af leaves 12.95 W for the device, 802.3at 25.5 W, and 802.3bt up to 71.3 W.","core":true},{"t":"Reading a datasheet","gist":"Read a datasheet in order: absolute maximums that destroy the part, recommended conditions, then the electrical and timing tables. Design with the min and max values, never the typical ones.","core":true},{"t":"Safe practice","gist":"Power off before rewiring, check polarity with a multimeter, and power new circuits from a bench supply limited to 100-300 mA so a mistake becomes a warning, not a fire. Never work on mains wiring.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"3.3 V logic reads high above 2.0 V and low below 0.8 V; never apply 5 V to a Pi GPIO pin.","part":0},{"p":"LED resistor: R = (Vsupply - Vf) ÷ I. A red LED from a 3.3 V pin uses 180 Ω for about 7.2 mA.","part":1},{"p":"Every relay coil, solenoid or motor needs a flyback diode across it, or the switch-off spike kills the transistor.","part":2},{"p":"UART 8N1 sends ten bits per byte, so 115,200 baud moves 11,520 bytes per second.","part":9},{"p":"After cable loss, 802.3af PoE leaves 12.95 W for the device and 802.3at (PoE+) leaves 25.5 W.","part":10}]},"p10m8":{"big":"Every IoT radio trades range, power and data rate, so you pick one for a job by doing the arithmetic on paper before you buy.","why":"Picking the right radio, with the arithmetic to prove it, is how you design sensor networks for clients that run for years without a battery change or a monthly cloud bill.","parts":[{"t":"The map","gist":"A side-by-side map of the radios: Wi-Fi is fast but short-range and power-hungry, BLE and Zigbee or Thread are very low power, and LoRaWAN reaches kilometres at a few kilobits per second or less.","core":true},{"t":"IEEE 802.15.4: the radio under Zigbee and Thread","gist":"Zigbee and Thread share one slow, low-power radio standard, IEEE 802.15.4, at 250 kb/s. Its channels 15, 20 and 25 fall in the gaps around Wi-Fi channels 1, 6 and 11, so both can share 2.4 GHz.","core":true},{"t":"Zigbee","gist":"Zigbee is a mesh: a coordinator forms the network, mains-powered plugs and bulbs relay traffic, and battery sensors sleep and talk through a parent. Use install codes, because old default join keys could leak the network key.","core":true},{"t":"Thread and Matter","gist":"Thread is an 802.15.4 mesh that carries IPv6 and reaches your LAN through a border router. Matter isn't a radio: it is the application layer over Wi-Fi, Thread or Ethernet, set up over BLE and controlled locally.","core":true},{"t":"LoRa: the physics of long range","gist":"LoRa trades speed for sensitivity: a higher spreading factor reaches farther but stays on air much longer. SF7 gives about 5.5 kb/s and SF12 about 290 b/s, and the law caps time on air.","core":true},{"t":"LoRaWAN: the network around the radio","gist":"LoRaWAN is a star of stars: sensors transmit, any gateway in range forwards to a network server, and the server passes data to your application. Over-the-air activation creates AES-128 session keys; Class A is the low-power default.","core":true},{"t":"Wi-Fi HaLow and Z-Wave","gist":"Wi-Fi HaLow runs Wi-Fi at 902-928 MHz for around a kilometre, with plain IP and WPA3, good for cameras and gates at a property's edge. Z-Wave is a slower but less crowded mesh on 908.42 MHz.","core":false},{"t":"Cellular IoT","gist":"When there is no gateway to build, a SIM is the gateway. LTE-M and NB-IoT use the cell network and sleep with PSM and eDRX. US 3G is gone, so a new module must be LTE-M, NB-IoT or 5G.","core":false},{"t":"Choosing, with arithmetic","gist":"Choose in order: how much data, how far, and what power source. Then eliminate: cameras need Wi-Fi or HaLow, house door sensors Zigbee, Thread or BLE, a property-wide field LoRaWAN, a truck tracker LTE-M.","core":true},{"t":"A secure sensor network on a Pi","gist":"A Pi gateway with a Zigbee or Thread radio, optional LoRaWAN, an MQTT broker and a dashboard. Secure it by configuration: install codes, over-the-air activation, the IoT VLAN, no inbound ports, and backed-up network keys.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"802.15.4 runs at 250 kb/s, and its channels 15, 20 and 25 coexist with a 1/6/11 Wi-Fi plan.","part":1},{"p":"Matter is an application layer over IP on Wi-Fi, Thread or Ethernet; BLE is used only to commission a new device.","part":3},{"p":"LoRa symbol time is 2^SF ÷ bandwidth: a higher SF gains sensitivity but multiplies time on air and battery drain.","part":4},{"p":"US rules cap LoRa dwell time at 400 ms per channel; most of Europe's 868 MHz sub-bands allow a 1% duty cycle.","part":4},{"p":"LoRaWAN Class A devices listen only in two short receive windows after each transmission, the lowest-power default.","part":5}]},"p11m1":{"big":"Reverse engineering studies a compiled program without its source to learn how it behaves, and doing it legitimately means having the right to examine it and handling it safely.","why":"It lets defenders understand suspicious files, find and disclose bugs and make software interoperate. Staying inside the legal and safety lines is what separates a security professional from a pirate.","parts":[{"t":"What it's legitimately for","gist":"Professionals reverse-engineer for defense and knowledge: taking malware apart to build detections, finding and responsibly disclosing bugs, securing firmware, making software talk to undocumented formats, forensics, and CTF practice.","core":true},{"t":"The lines you do not cross","gist":"Analyze only what you may lawfully analyze: your own programs, open-source software, practice challenges and malware you are authorized to study. Never reverse software to defeat its licensing, strip its protections, or clone and resell it.","core":true},{"t":"The professional toolbox","gist":"The standard tools: Ghidra, free with an excellent decompiler and the best place to start; IDA and Binary Ninja; radare2 or rizin; debuggers such as x64dbg and GDB; and file, strings and objdump for quick triage.","core":false},{"t":"Static vs dynamic, and the formats","gist":"Static analysis examines the file at rest; dynamic analysis watches it run under a debugger or sandbox. You'll meet PE, ELF and Mach-O files, and early wins come from strings, imported function names and the control-flow graph.","core":true},{"t":"Studying malware without becoming a victim","gist":"Treat live malware like hazardous material: run it only in an isolated virtual machine with no real network, take a snapshot before and revert after, and turn off shared folders and clipboard.","core":true},{"t":"Professional workflow: define the question before opening the tool","gist":"Start with the decision the analysis must support, record where the file came from and its hash, and keep notes that separate what you saw from what you infer. Confirm written authorization before you begin.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Static analysis studies the file without running it; dynamic analysis watches it run under a debugger or sandbox.","part":3},{"p":"PE is the Windows executable format, ELF is Linux and Mach-O is macOS.","part":3},{"p":"In the US, DMCA section 1201 restricts bypassing a product's technological protections, with periodic exemptions for good-faith security research and interoperability.","part":1},{"p":"Never run a sample on your real machine: use an isolated VM with host-only or no networking, snapshot first, and revert afterward.","part":4},{"p":"Ghidra, the NSA's free open-source suite with a decompiler, is the best place to start.","part":2}]},"p11m2":{"big":"Reverse engineering rests on reading assembly: knowing what registers, the stack and the calling convention hold lets you work out what a function does without its source.","why":"This vocabulary lets you reason about crashes, compiler output and unfamiliar program behavior instead of guessing, and find things like a password check even when the names are stripped.","parts":[{"t":"Registers: the CPU's hands","gist":"A register is a tiny, very fast storage slot inside the CPU. x86-64 has sixteen general-purpose ones; RAX holds return values, RSP points to the stack, and RIP holds the address of the next instruction.","core":true},{"t":"The stack","gist":"The stack is a region of memory that grows downward, managed by RSP. It holds local variables, saved registers, extra arguments and, critically for security, each function call's return address.","core":true},{"t":"Calling conventions","gist":"A calling convention is the agreed contract for passing arguments and returning values. On 64-bit Linux the first six arguments go in RDI, RSI, RDX, RCX, R8 and R9, and the result comes back in RAX.","core":true},{"t":"The prologue and epilogue","gist":"Most functions start with a prologue that sets up a stack frame and end with an epilogue that tears it down, so you can find where functions begin and end even without names.","core":true},{"t":"A tiny reading","gist":"cmp eax, 0 followed by je reads as 'compare EAX with zero; if equal, jump.' A chain of compares against byte values is almost always a password or input check. ARM looks different, but the ideas carry over.","core":true},{"t":"Reasoning from machine state: ABI, stack discipline and data types","gist":"Read assembly with the platform's calling rules in mind, because which registers carry arguments differs between systems. Learn what each instruction actually does, and trace one value at a time instead of trusting the decompiler's guesses.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"RAX holds a function's return value, RSP is the stack pointer, and RIP points to the next instruction to run.","part":0},{"p":"The stack grows downward and holds each call's return address, which both control flow and exploits hinge on.","part":1},{"p":"64-bit Linux passes the first six integer arguments in RDI, RSI, RDX, RCX, R8, R9 and returns in RAX; 64-bit Windows uses RCX, RDX, R8, R9.","part":2},{"p":"The classic prologue is push rbp; mov rbp, rsp; sub rsp, N, and the epilogue is leave; ret.","part":3},{"p":"A chain of cmp and je against byte values is almost always a password or input check.","part":4}]},"p11m3":{"big":"Static analysis studies a program without running it: triage with file, strings and imports first, then read the control-flow graph and check the decompiler against the assembly.","why":"Static evidence is quick to collect, keeps an unknown file from running during the first look, and tells you which questions to test dynamically.","parts":[{"t":"Triage first","gist":"Before opening a disassembler, triage: file names the format and architecture, strings dumps readable text such as URLs and paths, and the import table lists the library functions it calls, which often reveals a lot.","core":true},{"t":"Disassembly vs decompilation","gist":"Disassembly shows every instruction faithfully. Decompilation rebuilds approximate C-like code that is easier to read but loses names and sometimes guesses wrong. Read the pseudocode for speed and drop to the assembly when it looks off.","core":true},{"t":"The control-flow graph","gist":"A control-flow graph draws a function as boxes of straight-line code joined by arrows for branches. Learn the shapes: a diamond is an if/else, a cycle back up is a loop.","core":true},{"t":"Cross-references, the analyst's superpower","gist":"Cross-references answer what calls a function and what code uses a string. Find an interesting string, then follow its xref to the code that uses it: the fastest way into an unknown binary.","core":true},{"t":"Naming as you go","gist":"As you understand each function, rename it and its variables, add comments and define types. Each pass makes the next easier, and the annotated database is the analyst's real deliverable.","core":false},{"t":"From a control-flow graph to a testable hypothesis","gist":"Build the control-flow graph from the real instruction addresses, then follow where each value comes from. Treat function names and static findings as guesses, and test them with a controlled debugger run.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Triage before disassembling: file for format and architecture, strings for readable text, then the import table.","part":0},{"p":"Decompiler pseudocode is an approximation that can be wrong; verify it against the assembly when it matters.","part":1},{"p":"In a control-flow graph, a diamond is an if/else, a cycle back up is a loop, and long chains of small compares are input validation.","part":2},{"p":"Find a string, then xref to the code that uses it: the fastest way into an unknown binary.","part":3}]},"p11m4":{"big":"Dynamic analysis watches a program actually run, pausing it with a debugger to read live values, and with malware it happens only inside an isolated lab.","why":"It answers what static reading struggles with, like what a value decrypts to or which branch really runs, connecting instructions to real effects so you can test hypotheses with evidence.","parts":[{"t":"The debugger","gist":"A debugger runs the program under your control: breakpoints pause it where you choose, stepping runs one instruction at a time, and you read registers and memory. A string encrypted on disk is plain in memory once decrypted.","core":true},{"t":"Behavioral analysis","gist":"Behavioral analysis runs the sample in a sandbox and watches its effects: files created, registry keys set, processes spawned and network connections tried. Behavior tells you what it does; code analysis tells you how.","core":true},{"t":"The non-negotiable safety frame","gist":"All dynamic malware work happens in the isolated VM: host-only or simulated networking, a clean snapshot before and a revert after, shared folders and clipboard off. Containment first, every time, no exceptions.","core":true},{"t":"Debugger method: change one variable, observe one consequence","gist":"Treat a debugger session as an experiment: state a hypothesis, start from a clean snapshot, change one thing and record what happens. Debuggers can change how a program behaves, so confirm big findings with a second method.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Breakpoints pause at a chosen address or function; watchpoints pause when a particular memory location changes.","part":0},{"p":"To read an encrypted string, set a breakpoint just after the decryption routine and read the plaintext from memory.","part":0},{"p":"Behavioral analysis tells you what a sample does; code analysis tells you how. You use both.","part":1},{"p":"Dynamic malware work happens only in an isolated, snapshotted VM with host-only or simulated networking.","part":2}]},"p11m5":{"big":"Executables have a defined structure that packing, obfuscation and anti-analysis tricks try to hide, and spotting those tricks is itself evidence about the file.","why":"Correct identification keeps you from using the wrong tools, and knowing the evasion tricks helps you tell ordinary packaging choices from genuinely suspicious evidence.","parts":[{"t":"The executable formats","gist":"A Windows PE file has a header and sections: .text for code, .data for data, .rdata for strings and imports, .rsrc for resources. ELF and Mach-O follow the same idea. The header gives architecture, entry point and imports.","core":true},{"t":"Packing","gist":"A packer compresses or encrypts the real program inside a small stub that unpacks it at runtime. Signs: few strings, few imports, high entropy (near-random bytes). Let it unpack in a debugger, then dump the real code from memory.","core":true},{"t":"Obfuscation","gist":"Obfuscation makes code hard to read: strings decrypted only when used, flattened control flow, junk branches, and library calls resolved by hash so the import table looks empty. Each technique has a known counter.","core":true},{"t":"Anti-analysis","gist":"Malware may check for a debugger, a virtual machine or the slowdown of single-stepping, then play dead. These checks have known counters, and benign software has no reason to hide from a debugger, so they are strong evidence.","core":true},{"t":"Format parsing, entropy and anti-analysis: avoid single-signal conclusions","gist":"High entropy, few imports or obfuscation are clues, not proof that a file is malicious; legitimate software shows them too. Compare several signals, and keep the original file next to any unpacked copy.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"PE sections: .text holds code, .data initialized data, .rdata read-only data such as strings and imports, .rsrc resources.","part":0},{"p":"Signs of packing: almost no readable strings, very few imports, unusually high entropy and odd section names.","part":1},{"p":"UPX is the common benign packer and is trivially reversible; malware uses custom or commercial packers to hide.","part":1},{"p":"An empty import table often means API hashing or packing is hiding the real imports: a red flag, not reassurance.","part":2},{"p":"Heavy anti-analysis is strong evidence of malicious intent, because benign software has no reason to hide from a debugger.","part":3}]},"p11m6":{"big":"Malware analysis is careful defensive work: contain the sample in an isolated lab, cycle through four analysis stages, and turn what you learn into detections others can use.","why":"The goal is reliable defensive indicators and a clear record, such as hashes, domains and YARA rules that let every defender detect a family, without spreading malware or contaminating evidence.","parts":[{"t":"The lab","gist":"Build an isolated lab: analysis VMs running REMnux and a Windows FLARE-VM, with host-only or simulated networking. Snapshot clean, detonate, observe and revert, with no path from the detonation VM to the real network.","core":true},{"t":"The four stages","gist":"Work in four stages: basic static triage, basic dynamic detonation to watch behavior, advanced static reading in Ghidra, and advanced dynamic debugging through the hard parts. You cycle between them, each finding feeding the next.","core":true},{"t":"IOCs and detection","gist":"The deliverable is detection: indicators of compromise such as hashes, C2 domains, file paths and registry keys, plus a YARA rule matching the family's stable byte patterns, mapped to MITRE ATT&CK.","core":true},{"t":"The families, behaviorally","gist":"Learn to recognize the major families by behavior so you can detect them: ransomware, trojans and RATs, droppers, rootkits, botnet clients, infostealers and keyloggers, and worms. Spotting the family early tells you what to look for next.","core":true},{"t":"Analysis pipeline, evidence quality and detection engineering","gist":"Run every sample through the same four stages and keep a lab notebook. Indicators differ in how long they stay useful, and a YARA rule is a hypothesis you test against harmless software and related samples.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Detonation networking is host-only or simulated with INetSim or FakeNet, never the real internet.","part":0},{"p":"Start by hashing the sample and checking threat-intel databases; someone may have analyzed it already.","part":1},{"p":"IOCs are concrete artifacts others can search for: file hashes, C2 domains and IPs, file paths, registry keys, mutex names.","part":2},{"p":"A YARA rule matches files containing chosen strings or byte sequences, so defenders can detect a family across a fleet.","part":2},{"p":"Ransomware encrypts files and demands payment; droppers and loaders fetch and run further payloads; worms self-propagate.","part":3}]},"p11m7":{"big":"Tools and AI assistants speed up reverse engineering, but only your own ability to read the code lets you catch their confident mistakes.","why":"A repeatable, scripted toolchain makes findings easier to reproduce and turn into detections, and knowing AI's limits keeps you from trusting plausible but wrong explanations.","parts":[{"t":"The disassembler/decompiler suites","gist":"The main suites: Ghidra, free with a superb decompiler and the place to learn; IDA Pro, the expensive long-time standard; Binary Ninja, modern and loved for automation; radare2 or rizin with Cutter, free and scriptable.","core":true},{"t":"Debuggers and instrumentation","gist":"Debuggers handle the dynamic work, and Frida injects scripts into a running process to hook and log function calls live, which is great for mobile and app analysis. QEMU emulates other architectures for firmware.","core":true},{"t":"Automation and scripting","gist":"Serious reverse engineering is scripted: every major tool has an API, so you automate repetitive work like batch triage, renaming by pattern and extracting configs. A day of clicking becomes a five-line loop.","core":true},{"t":"Where AI-assisted RE fits","gist":"AI assistants such as ReVa and r2ai explain decompiled code and suggest names, which speeds you up. But they can be confidently wrong or misled by obfuscated code, and only reading the assembly yourself catches that.","core":true},{"t":"Your path to professional","gist":"A legal path to professional: GhidraClass, crackmes.one and picoCTF practice, Practical Malware Analysis labs, Python scripting, writeups, then the GREM or OSED certifications, with AI assistants added last.","core":false},{"t":"Tool validation, automation and AI-assisted analysis","gist":"Pick the tool for the question and record its version. Automate only in ways that are repeatable and never change evidence. AI help can explain or draft, but it can invent details, so check every claim against the raw evidence.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Ghidra is the NSA's free, open-source suite; IDA Pro is the expensive commercial standard; rizin is the maintained fork of radare2.","part":0},{"p":"Frida injects scripts into a running process to hook and log function calls live.","part":1},{"p":"An LLM can give confident, wrong explanations and be baited by adversarial code; you can only tell if you can read the assembly yourself.","part":3},{"p":"AI doesn't change what you're authorized to analyze; the legal line applies whatever tool you use.","part":3}]},"p12m1":{"big":"Hermes Agent is an open-source program that wraps a language model in a read, think, act, observe loop, and it runs real commands on your machine.","why":"Everything after this depends on knowing which 'Hermes' is meant, what an agent actually is, and that it runs real commands on your machine, so safety habits come first.","parts":[{"t":"What an agent is","gist":"A chat model answers a message; an agent pursues a goal in a loop: read, think, act with a tool, observe, repeat. 'Self-improving' means Hermes writes skill and memory files; it never retrains the model.","core":true},{"t":"Why run it locally","gist":"Running locally keeps your prompts and files on your machine, with no per-token bills and offline use. The price is hardware, about 8-24 GB of GPU or unified memory, and small models are weaker than frontier cloud ones.","core":true},{"t":"How the pieces relate","gist":"Three things share the name: the Hermes models, open-weight LLMs from Nous Research; Hermes Agent, the open-source agent this domain is about; and the mesh sense, including Mesh LLM, which pools GPUs across machines.","core":true},{"t":"The responsibility frame","gist":"An agent runs real commands for you. Run it as a normal user, keep secrets in .env, use allowlists, isolate risky work in Docker or a VM, and stay on systems you own or have permission to use.","core":true},{"t":"Separate model, agent, tools and mesh: the system boundary matters","gist":"The model only produces text; the agent software around it decides which tools run. Every step from input to tool call is a place things can fail, and stored memory is data, not instructions.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The Hermes models are open-weight LLMs from Nous Research; Hermes Agent is the agent harness, and it can run many models.","part":2},{"p":"An agent loops: read the task, think, act with a tool, observe the result, and repeat until done.","part":0},{"p":"'Self-improving' means Hermes writes and edits skill and memory files you can read and delete; the model's weights never change.","part":0},{"p":"Useful local models want 8-24 GB of GPU or unified memory; most serious users mix local and cloud models.","part":1},{"p":"The --yolo flag turns off the approval step before dangerous commands; knowing when not to use it is part of mastery.","part":3}]},"p12m2":{"big":"One official installer sets up Hermes on each platform, and a careful first run that ends with hermes doctor reporting green prevents days of mysterious problems.","why":"A clean install saves days of confusion, and the WSL2 details, systemd, file placement and the GPU driver, are exactly where beginners get stuck.","parts":[{"t":"Install commands by platform","gist":"One curl command installs Hermes on Linux, macOS and WSL2, and a PowerShell one-liner does it on Windows. It brings a pinned Python 3.14, uv and helper tools; pip and Homebrew are unsupported. Data lives in ~/.hermes/.","core":true},{"t":"WSL2 on Windows: the professional way","gist":"On Windows, WSL2 is the professional setup: enable systemd, keep files in the Linux home because /mnt/c is 10-100× slower, install only the Windows NVIDIA driver, and use mirrored networking to reach Windows-side model servers.","core":true},{"t":"First run","gist":"Run hermes setup to choose a starting path, hermes model to pick a provider and model, and hermes doctor to check the install. Whenever anything seems odd, run hermes doctor first.","core":true},{"t":"Updating and removing","gist":"hermes update pulls the latest release on your channel and hermes uninstall removes it; --dry-run shows what would go. Back up ~/.hermes/ first, because it holds your memory, skills, sessions and keys.","core":true},{"t":"Installation is a software-supply-chain event","gist":"Running an installer means running someone else's code with your permissions. Read the official instructions first, use a separate account and test folder, never run the agent as administrator, and check what the install changed.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"pip install, uv tool install, Homebrew and AUR packages are unsupported; use the official installer.","part":0},{"p":"Under WSL2, keep Hermes, repositories and models in the Linux home; files under /mnt/c are 10-100× slower.","part":1},{"p":"For an NVIDIA GPU in WSL2, install only the Windows driver; nvidia-smi inside WSL confirms passthrough.","part":1},{"p":"Any model you pick needs a context window of at least 64,000 tokens, or Hermes rejects it at startup.","part":2},{"p":"Back up ~/.hermes/ before updating or uninstalling; it holds your memory, skills, sessions and keys.","part":3}]},"p12m3":{"big":"Every Hermes entry point, from the terminal to Telegram to cron, feeds one agent core, so the same memory, skills and configuration work everywhere.","why":"Understanding the single core explains why a skill works on every surface, why memory is small on purpose, and where to put a safety boundary: the terminal backend.","parts":[{"t":"The loop and the prompt","gist":"The loop builds the prompt, runs the model, executes tool calls and saves the session. The prompt is layered so its stable part can be cached, and by default long chats get their middle summarized at half the context window.","core":true},{"t":"Providers and API modes","gist":"Beneath more than eighteen providers there are only three API modes: OpenAI chat completions, OpenAI responses, and Anthropic messages. That is why 'OpenAI-compatible' servers work with Hermes, Open WebUI and Mesh LLM.","core":true},{"t":"Tools, toolsets and where commands run","gist":"Seventy-plus tools are grouped into toolsets you switch on per surface. The terminal backend decides where shell commands run: locally, in Docker, over ssh or in a cloud sandbox. Choosing Docker is the biggest safety upgrade.","core":true},{"t":"Memory, skills and sessions","gist":"Memory is two short curated files, MEMORY.md about the work and USER.md about you. Skills are folders with a SKILL.md that become slash commands. Sessions sit in a searchable SQLite database.","core":true},{"t":"Gateway, plugins, cron, ACP, trajectories","gist":"The gateway is one background process that serves every messaging app, enforces allowlists and ticks cron every sixty seconds. Plugins, editor integration through ACP, and conversation exports for fine-tuning round out the system.","core":false},{"t":"Prompt construction, tool authorization and untrusted content","gist":"Your instructions, files, web pages and tool results carry different levels of trust. Check every tool call against a policy the model cannot change, treat tool output as data, and log decisions without logging secrets.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Prompt tiers are stable (identity, tools, skills), context (memory, project files) and volatile (the live conversation); switching models mid-session resets the cache.","part":0},{"p":"The compressor summarizes middle turns at half the context window by default, protecting the first three and the last twenty.","part":0},{"p":"Hermes has three API modes underneath: OpenAI chat completions, OpenAI responses and Anthropic messages.","part":1},{"p":"The docker terminal backend is the safe default for untrusted work and the single biggest beginner safety upgrade.","part":2},{"p":"MEMORY.md and USER.md are capped at about 2,200 and 1,375 characters; sessions live in ~/.hermes/state.db.","part":3}]},"p12m4":{"big":"A local model works as an agent only if it fits your GPU memory, weights plus KV cache, and its server has tool calling turned on.","why":"Sizing a model to your GPU and enabling tool calling are the two things that decide whether your agent works at all.","parts":[{"t":"Backends and fit","gist":"Hermes picks a backend automatically: CUDA for NVIDIA, Metal for Apple, Vulkan for AMD and Intel, CPU otherwise. It rates models green, amber or red for fit and speed, never below 4-bit, always with 64K context.","core":true},{"t":"The arithmetic (Domain 9 applied)","gist":"Weight memory is about parameters × bits per weight ÷ 8, so a 35B model at 4.5 bits is about 20 GB. The KV cache, the attention memory for the context, adds more as the context grows.","core":true},{"t":"Bringing your own server","gist":"Ollama, vLLM, SGLang, llama-server and LM Studio all work, but each needs its own flags for a 64K context and for tool calling. Forget the tool-calling flag and the model chats but never acts.","core":true},{"t":"Hosted providers and mixing","gist":"Hosted providers such as Nous Portal, OpenRouter and Anthropic are one wizard step away. hermes fallback sets what runs when the primary fails, so a local model can do routine work and a frontier model the hard tasks.","core":false},{"t":"Memory budgeting and honest local-inference benchmarks","gist":"A local model needs memory for more than its weights: the key/value cache grows with context length, and the runtime adds overhead. Benchmark on a fixed set of real tasks, not one short prompt.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Auto backends: CUDA for NVIDIA, Vulkan for AMD and Intel GPUs, Metal for Apple Silicon, CPU for everything else.","part":0},{"p":"Weight memory ≈ parameters × bits per weight ÷ 8: a 35B model at 4.5 bits is about 20 GB, before the KV cache.","part":1},{"p":"4-bit is the floor Hermes recommends, 5-6 bit is the quality sweet spot if it fits, and 8-bit is near-lossless.","part":1},{"p":"Tokens per second follow memory bandwidth more than compute, so weights that spill to system RAM slow to a crawl.","part":1},{"p":"Ollama defaults to a 4,096 context, so set OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=64000; llama-server ignores tools without --jinja.","part":2}]},"p12m5":{"big":"Operating Hermes daily means driving the CLI, keeping config and secrets in their proper places, managing skills, memory and cron, and locking every door with default-deny security.","why":"These are the daily skills of a Hermes operator, and the security checklist is what keeps an agent with shell access from becoming a liability.","parts":[{"t":"The command line","gist":"hermes opens the full-screen TUI, and slash commands such as /model, /tools and /rollback control a chat. hermes -z runs one headless task and exits with a code your scripts can check, 0 for success.","core":true},{"t":"Configuration","gist":"Everything lives in ~/.hermes: settings in config.yaml, secrets in .env, identity in SOUL.md. Command-line flags beat config.yaml, which beats .env, which beats defaults; hermes config set sends each key to the right file.","core":true},{"t":"Skills, memory, sessions, cron","gist":"Install skills from the Skills Hub or write your own folder with a SKILL.md, and it becomes a slash command. Review skills the agent writes like code. hermes memory, sessions and cron manage the rest.","core":true},{"t":"The messaging gateway","gist":"The gateway connects Telegram, Discord, Slack and many more, and it is default deny: nobody can talk to your bot until you allowlist their ID or approve a pairing code, which expires after an hour.","core":true},{"t":"The API server and other front ends","gist":"Turn on the API server and Hermes speaks the OpenAI API at 127.0.0.1:8642, behind a required bearer key. Point a chat UI like Open WebUI at it for a private ChatGPT-style interface.","core":true},{"t":"The security checklist","gist":"The checklist: run as a normal user with the docker backend, keep secrets only in .env, bind servers to 127.0.0.1 and reach them over SSH or a VPN, allowlist bot users, and keep --yolo for throwaway sandboxes.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"hermes -z exit codes: 0 success, 1 failure, 2 usage error, 130 interrupted.","part":0},{"p":"Configuration precedence: command-line flags beat config.yaml, which beats .env, which beats defaults.","part":1},{"p":"The gateway is default deny; pairing codes expire after an hour and should be treated like passwords.","part":3},{"p":"The API server listens at 127.0.0.1:8642 and requires the bearer token even on loopback.","part":4},{"p":"Secrets belong only in .env with mode 600, never in config.yaml, prompts or memory.","part":5}]},"p12m6":{"big":"Hermes agents on different machines can hand work to each other through peer, A2A, Kanban and delegation, and staying safe comes down to where every port is bound.","why":"Knowing which mechanism exists, which is only a proposal, and where every port is bound is the difference between a private mesh and a public liability.","parts":[{"t":"1. hermes peer","gist":"hermes peer links your agent to another machine's Hermes through its API server, using a URL and key. An idempotency key stops a retried job from running twice. Keep it on a LAN, WireGuard or Tailscale.","core":true},{"t":"2. A2A: the Agent-to-Agent protocol","gist":"A2A is an open protocol that lets agents from different vendors discover and call each other; each publishes an agent card describing what it can do. Safe defaults: loopback binding, a bearer token, rate limits and an audit log.","core":true},{"t":"3. Profiles, projects, Kanban and subagents","gist":"Profiles give separate agents their own memory and keys, Kanban dispatches task cards to several profiles in parallel, and subagents split work inside one agent, possibly on a cheaper model. Route each task to the cheapest capable brain.","core":true},{"t":"4. The community \"Hermes Mesh\" layer — exactly what exists","gist":"The community 'Hermes Mesh' package and plugin are real but small. hermes mesh init is only an unimplemented proposal, so the official multi-machine paths are peer, A2A, Kanban and delegation.","core":true},{"t":"The networking underneath","gist":"Know the ports: 8642 for the API server and 9900 for A2A. Binding to 127.0.0.1 means local only; 0.0.0.0 means every interface. Across the internet use an overlay like WireGuard or Tailscale, never open ports.","core":true},{"t":"Mesh security: identity, transport, authorization and failure handling","gist":"When agents talk across machines, give each its own credentials, keep it off the public internet, and limit what a remote peer may do. Use job IDs so a retried task never runs twice.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"hermes peer run with --idempotency-key stops a retried request from launching the same job twice.","part":0},{"p":"An A2A agent publishes an agent card at /.well-known/agent-card.json and accepts JSON-RPC 2.0 calls.","part":1},{"p":"As of October 2026, hermes mesh init does not exist; it is an unimplemented RFC.","part":3},{"p":"Port 8642 is the API server and 9900 is A2A; 127.0.0.1 is local only, while 0.0.0.0 is every interface.","part":4},{"p":"Across the internet, connect agents through WireGuard or Tailscale, not an open port protected only by a key.","part":4}]},"p12m7":{"big":"Mesh LLM pools GPUs across several machines behind one OpenAI-compatible API, so a household of ordinary computers can run a model none of them could hold alone.","why":"It turns ordinary computers into one bigger AI machine for your Hermes agent, and knowing its trust model, where an invite token is not authorization, keeps your prompts private.","parts":[{"t":"Architecture","gist":"Each node gets an iroh identity and gossips which models and GPUs it has. Router mode sends a request to a node holding the model; split mode cuts a big model into layer ranges across nodes, called pipeline parallelism.","core":true},{"t":"Discovery, meshes and trust","gist":"Nodes find each other through Nostr relays, mDNS on your LAN, or an invite token. The token only helps peers connect; trust policies decide who is allowed. On the public mesh strangers process your prompts, so send nothing sensitive.","core":true},{"t":"Install, configure, run","gist":"Install with one command, then run mesh-llm serve with the same mesh name on each machine to pool them. The API is at localhost:9337/v1, the console at port 3131, and mesh-llm hermes --write makes it Hermes' model provider.","core":true},{"t":"Compared with the alternatives","gist":"How it differs from exo, Petals and llama.cpp RPC: Mesh LLM adds internet-wide discovery, NAT traversal, trust policies and an OpenAI-style API on top of consumer GGUF models.","core":false},{"t":"Limits","gist":"It is a young, work-in-progress tool: no RDMA or image generation, split mode needs low latency, the public mesh means strangers' hardware, and the invite token is not access control.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Router mode picks a node that hosts the model; split mode spreads contiguous layer ranges across nodes, which is pipeline parallelism.","part":0},{"p":"Split mode adds a network hop per layer boundary for every token: it makes bigger models possible, not faster ones.","part":0},{"p":"An invite token is connectivity material, not authorization; use --trust-policy require-owned or allowlist to control who joins.","part":1},{"p":"Mesh LLM serves one OpenAI-compatible API at http://localhost:9337/v1, and mesh-llm hermes --write makes it Hermes' model provider.","part":2}]},"p13m1":{"big":"You can know whether a program is correct and how its cost grows before you run it: prove correctness with invariants and count steps with Big-O.","why":"Every structure and algorithm, every interview time limit and every algorithmic-complexity attack you will analyze as a pentester comes down to counting steps before you run the code.","parts":[{"t":"Abstraction and the RAM model","gist":"Abstraction separates what something does from how it does it. The RAM model treats each simple operation and each memory access as one step, so running time becomes a count of steps as a function of input size n.","core":true},{"t":"Correctness: specification, invariants, induction","gist":"Tests check a few inputs; a proof covers them all. A loop invariant is true before the loop, stays true after every pass, and at exit gives you the result you wanted.","core":true},{"t":"Counting steps: Big-O, Θ and Ω","gist":"Big-O keeps only how a step count grows and drops constants: sequential code adds, nested loops multiply. At a billion operations per second, n log n on a million items takes 20 ms, while n² takes 17 minutes.","core":true},{"t":"Measuring versus analyzing","gist":"Analysis predicts the shape; measurement supplies the constants. Time several input sizes and watch the ratio when n doubles: near 2 means linear, near 4 quadratic. Constants matter at small n.","core":false},{"t":"Amortized analysis","gist":"Amortized analysis averages a rare expensive step over a whole sequence. A doubling array copies fewer than 2n items over n appends, so each append is Θ(1) on average, even though one append can cost Θ(n).","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How this lines up with CS50x Week 3, Algorithms, and its sorting problem set, and with HackerRank time limits that quietly demand faster algorithms.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"f = O(g) means f(n) ≤ c·g(n) for all n ≥ n0; Ω is the matching lower bound and Θ means both.","part":2},{"p":"At 10^9 operations per second and n = 10^6, n log n takes 20 ms and n² takes 17 minutes.","part":2},{"p":"A loop invariant must hold at initialization, survive every iteration (maintenance), and give the postcondition at termination.","part":1},{"p":"When n doubles, a time ratio near 2 suggests linear and near 4 suggests quadratic.","part":3},{"p":"Doubling-array appends are amortized Θ(1), but that isn't enough when one Θ(n) pause is unacceptable or an attacker can trigger it.","part":4}]},"p13m2":{"big":"How bytes sit in memory sets the real speed of every container: arrays scan fast thanks to 64-byte cache lines, and hash tables stay fast only while collisions stay short.","why":"Picking the right container and estimating its memory is daily engineering work, and knowing why a hash table can be driven to its worst case is the defender's view of complexity attacks.","parts":[{"t":"Memory layout and the cache line","gist":"Memory moves in 64-byte cache lines, and a trip to main memory costs 80-100 ns. So a contiguous array scans near full speed, while pointer-chasing structures pay a cache miss at almost every step.","core":true},{"t":"Arrays and dynamic arrays","gist":"An array finds item i in Θ(1) by arithmetic, and appending is amortized Θ(1), but inserting in the middle shifts everything after it, Θ(n). A Python list is an array of pointers to separate objects.","core":true},{"t":"Linked lists","gist":"A linked list chains nodes by pointers: inserting beside a node you already hold is Θ(1), but reaching the k-th item means walking there, and scattered nodes cause cache misses. Use one when you hold the node.","core":true},{"t":"Stacks and queues","gist":"A stack is last-in, first-out, like the call stack, where overrunning a buffer can overwrite a return address. A queue is first-in, first-out, often a ring buffer. Python's deque is Θ(1) at both ends.","core":true},{"t":"Hash tables","gist":"A hash table turns a key into a bucket number, and collisions are unavoidable. The load factor, items per bucket, sets the cost, so tables resize early. Predictable hashes let attackers force collisions, hence randomized keyed hashing.","core":true},{"t":"Worked numbers: CS50's Speller","gist":"CS50's Speller shows the levers in numbers: 143,091 words in 26 buckets is about 5,504 per bucket, while with 200,003 buckets most checks touch only one or two nodes.","core":false},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How this lines up with CS50x Weeks 2, 4 and 5 and with HackerRank, where most questions that say 'in O(1)' want a hash table or a deque.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A cache line is 64 bytes and a DRAM trip about 80-100 ns, so traversing a linked list is a chain of cache misses.","part":0},{"p":"A Python list stores 8-byte pointers to separate objects; NumPy stores raw values contiguously, which is much of why it is fast.","part":0},{"p":"Python's list.pop(0) is Θ(n), while collections.deque is Θ(1) at both ends.","part":3},{"p":"Load factor α = n/m; with chaining a lookup costs about 1 + α comparisons. CPython's dict resizes at two thirds full, Java's HashMap at 0.75.","part":4},{"p":"Hash flooding (2011) sent colliding keys to force Θ(n²) work; CPython now randomizes string hashes per process with SipHash.","part":4}]},"p13m3":{"big":"Divide and conquer gives fast algorithms like merge sort, and a proof shows no comparison sort can beat n log n, though counting digits instead of comparing can.","why":"Sorting is the first thing every library, database and interview asks you to understand, and the lower-bound argument is your first taste of proving what no program can do.","parts":[{"t":"Recursion done right","gist":"Correct recursion needs a base case, progress toward it, and a combine step you prove by induction. Each call costs a stack frame, so depth n can hit Python's limit of about 1,000 frames; depth log n is safe.","core":true},{"t":"Divide and conquer and the recurrence","gist":"Divide and conquer gives the recurrence T(n) = a·T(n/b) + f(n). The master theorem solves it by comparing the combining work with the leaves' work: merge sort comes out Θ(n log n), binary search Θ(log n).","core":true},{"t":"The quadratic sorts, and why they survive","gist":"Insertion, selection and bubble sort are Θ(n²) in general, but insertion sort is Θ(n) on nearly sorted data and the fastest choice for about 16-32 elements, so real library sorts hand it small ranges.","core":false},{"t":"Merge sort, quicksort, heapsort, Timsort","gist":"Merge sort is always n log n and stable but needs extra space. Quicksort is in place and fast, but a bad pivot makes it Θ(n²), hence introsort. Heapsort guarantees n log n; Python uses the stable Timsort.","core":true},{"t":"The n log n lower bound","gist":"Any comparison sort is a decision tree needing a leaf for each of the n! orderings, so its height, the worst-case number of comparisons, is at least log2(n!), about n log2 n. No comparison sort beats Ω(n log n).","core":true},{"t":"Beating the bound without comparing","gist":"If keys are small integers, you can count instead of compare. Counting and radix sort run in Θ(d(n + k)); for a million 32-bit integers that is roughly five times fewer operations. The model changed, not the bound.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How this lines up with CS50x Week 3's sorts and recursion and Problem Set 3's mystery sorts, plus HackerRank sorting questions.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Master theorem: T(n) = a·T(n/b) + f(n), compare f(n) with n^(log_b a). Merge sort has a=2, b=2, f=n, giving Θ(n log n).","part":1},{"p":"Quicksort averages about 1.39 n log2 n comparisons in place but degrades to Θ(n²) on bad pivots; introsort switches to heapsort to cap it.","part":3},{"p":"Merge sort, insertion sort and Timsort are stable (equal keys keep their order); quicksort and heapsort are not.","part":3},{"p":"Comparison sorting is Ω(n log n) because a decision tree with n! leaves has height at least log2(n!).","part":4},{"p":"Counting sort is Θ(n + k) and radix sort Θ(d(n + k)), and they work only for integer or fixed-width keys.","part":5}]},"p13m4":{"big":"Trees keep data ordered with logarithmic operations as long as their height stays small, which is why balanced trees, heaps and wide B-trees are everywhere.","why":"Ordered data, priority queues, routing tables and database indexes are all trees, and the fan-out arithmetic lets you reason about any index you will ever tune.","parts":[{"t":"Vocabulary and the binary search tree","gist":"In a binary search tree, smaller keys go left and larger ones right, so search, insert and delete cost the tree's height. Insert keys in sorted order and the tree becomes a chain, Θ(n) per operation.","core":true},{"t":"Balanced trees","gist":"Self-balancing trees use small local rotations to keep height logarithmic: AVL trees stay under 1.44 log2 n, red-black trees within 2 log2 n. Need order or ranges, use one; need only exact lookups, use a hash table.","core":true},{"t":"Heaps and priority queues","gist":"A heap is a complete tree in a plain array, minimum at the root, with index i's children at 2i + 1 and 2i + 2. It is the standard priority queue: Θ(log n) insert and extract.","core":true},{"t":"Tries","gist":"A trie stores strings one character per level, so a lookup costs the word's length, not the number of words, though it uses a lot of memory. Routers use a binary trie for longest-prefix matching of IP addresses.","core":true},{"t":"B-trees and databases","gist":"Storage is read in pages, so a B-tree makes each node one page holding hundreds of keys. With fan-out 256, three levels index about 16.8 million keys, so a lookup costs one or two real disk reads.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How this lines up with CS50x Week 5's trees and tries and Week 7's SQL indexes, plus the tree and heap questions common in interviews.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"BST operations cost Θ(height); sorted insertions turn an unbalanced BST into a chain with Θ(n) operations.","part":0},{"p":"Red-black trees keep height ≤ 2 log2(n + 1) and are used by Java's TreeMap, C++'s std::map and the Linux kernel.","part":1},{"p":"In an array heap, the children of index i are at 2i + 1 and 2i + 2, and the parent at (i − 1) // 2.","part":2},{"p":"IPv4 forwarding is a longest-prefix match in a binary trie: at most 32 steps, whatever the table size.","part":3},{"p":"B-tree fan-out: a 4 KB page with 16-byte entries gives 256, so four levels index about 4.3 billion keys.","part":4}]},"p13m5":{"big":"Many real problems are graphs of vertices and edges, and each question about them, shortest path, cheapest connection or connectivity, has its own algorithm.","why":"Routing protocols, build systems, dependency resolvers, attack-path tools and fiber-network planning are graph algorithms, so this makes your own trade and Domain 2 routing mathematically precise.","parts":[{"t":"Representations","gist":"An adjacency matrix gives instant edge lookups but needs V² memory; an adjacency list stores each vertex's neighbors in Θ(V + E). Real graphs are sparse, so lists are the default.","core":true},{"t":"BFS and DFS","gist":"Breadth-first search explores in layers with a queue and finds shortest paths by edge count. Depth-first search goes deep with a stack, detecting cycles and producing the topological order that build systems like make use.","core":true},{"t":"Shortest paths in weighted graphs","gist":"Dijkstra's algorithm settles the closest unsettled vertex and updates its neighbors; it needs non-negative weights, and OSPF routers run it. Bellman-Ford handles negative weights more slowly, and RIP runs it in distributed form.","core":true},{"t":"Minimum spanning trees","gist":"A minimum spanning tree connects every vertex with the least total edge weight. Prim grows one tree; Kruskal adds the cheapest edge joining two separate pieces. The cheapest fiber network reaching every site is exactly this.","core":true},{"t":"Union-find","gist":"Union-find answers 'are these two already connected?' by keeping each group as a tree with a root. With union by rank and path compression, each operation costs amortized α(n), which is at most 4 for any realistic input.","core":true},{"t":"Worked example","gist":"One six-site fiber graph worked by hand: Dijkstra from A finds the shortest route to F is 13 km, and Kruskal builds a 13 km spanning tree, rejecting A-B because both ends were already connected.","core":false},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How this lines up with CS50's Tideman problem, which uses DFS to refuse cycles, CS50 AI's search algorithms, and standard interview graph questions.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Adjacency lists use Θ(V + E) memory versus Θ(V²) for a matrix, so they are the default for sparse graphs.","part":0},{"p":"BFS uses a queue and finds unweighted shortest paths; DFS uses a stack, detects cycles and gives a topological sort. Both are Θ(V + E).","part":1},{"p":"Dijkstra needs non-negative weights and runs in Θ((V + E) log V) with a heap; OSPF and IS-IS run it.","part":2},{"p":"RIP is distributed Bellman-Ford with hop count as the metric, capped at 15 hops, with 16 meaning unreachable.","part":2},{"p":"Union-find with union by rank and path compression costs amortized α(n) ≤ 4 per operation, which makes Kruskal fast.","part":4}]},"p13m6":{"big":"When subproblems overlap, solve each one once and store it (dynamic programming); trust a greedy shortcut only when you can prove its choice is always safe.","why":"DP and greedy power diff, compression, spell-check and typosquat detection, and most medium interview questions; knowing when greedy is safe marks someone who proves instead of hopes.","parts":[{"t":"Overlapping subproblems","gist":"Naive recursive Fibonacci recomputes the same subproblems, so fib(40) makes 331,160,281 calls for 41 distinct values. Memoization stores each answer once, and tabulation fills a table bottom-up without recursion.","core":true},{"t":"The DP recipe","gist":"Every DP answers six questions: state, recurrence, base cases, order, answer, and reconstruction of the actual solution. The cost is the number of states times the work per state.","core":true},{"t":"Classic problems and their recurrences","gist":"The classic recurrences: knapsack Θ(nW), longest common subsequence and edit distance Θ(mn), coin change Θ(A·k). Knapsack's Θ(nW) is pseudo-polynomial, exponential in the number of bits of W.","core":true},{"t":"Greedy algorithms, and when they are provably right","gist":"A greedy algorithm makes one irrevocable best-looking choice at a time and is correct only when an exchange argument proves it. It works for US coins and Huffman coding but fails for coins {1, 3, 4} making 6.","core":true},{"t":"DP in your other domains","gist":"The same recipe shows up elsewhere: Viterbi decoding of radio codes, backpropagation in neural networks, regular-expression matching and text justification in TeX are all dynamic programming.","core":false},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How this lines up with CS50's early greedy 'Cash' problem and with HackerRank and LeetCode medium questions, where DP lives.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Naive fib(40) makes 331,160,281 calls; memoization solves each of the 41 subproblems once, Θ(n).","part":0},{"p":"DP needs optimal substructure and overlapping subproblems; divide and conquer has the first without the second.","part":1},{"p":"Edit distance is Θ(mn); paypal.com and paypa1.com are distance 1, which is how look-alike domains get flagged.","part":2},{"p":"Knapsack's Θ(nW) is pseudo-polynomial: exponential in the bits of W, consistent with knapsack being NP-complete.","part":2},{"p":"Greedy fails for coins {1, 3, 4} making 6: it takes 4 + 1 + 1, while 3 + 3 needs only two coins.","part":3}]},"p13m7":{"big":"Some patterns need more than a finite machine, some questions no program can answer, and some problems are easy to check but hard to solve; security rests on these limits.","why":"ReDoS, injection, antivirus limits, symbolic execution and every cryptographic strength claim come from this theory, which is why a senior pentester can explain a bug instead of just reproducing it.","parts":[{"t":"Finite automata and regular languages","gist":"A DFA has finitely many states and no other memory, and matches exactly what regular expressions can. It can't count without limit, so it can't check nested brackets, and backtracking regex engines can be made to hang (ReDoS).","core":true},{"t":"Context-free grammars and the Chomsky hierarchy","gist":"Context-free grammars, recognized by a finite machine plus a stack, describe programming languages, JSON and SQL. Injection is grammar confusion, user data read as code, so parse input with a recognizer for exactly the language you meant.","core":true},{"t":"Turing machines and the Church–Turing thesis","gist":"A Turing machine, an infinite tape with a read/write head and finite control, captures what we mean by computation. The Church–Turing thesis holds that it, the lambda calculus and the RAM model compute the same functions.","core":false},{"t":"Decidability and the halting problem","gist":"No program can decide in general whether another program halts, and Rice's theorem extends this to any property of what a program does. So no malware detector can be perfect; every tool is an approximation.","core":true},{"t":"P, NP, NP-completeness and reductions","gist":"P problems are solvable in polynomial time; NP problems have answers you can verify in polynomial time. SAT is NP-complete, and a reduction proves a new problem is at least as hard. Whether P equals NP is open.","core":true},{"t":"Hard problems underpin cryptography","gist":"Public-key cryptography bets that problems like factoring and discrete logarithms stay hard. Shor's quantum algorithm would break that bet, which is why NIST published the post-quantum standards FIPS 203, 204 and 205 in August 2024.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"CS50x stays below this level, so treat this as a bridge to CS50's AI course and Sipser's textbook, and as a foundation for the security domains.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Regular expressions, NFAs and DFAs describe exactly the same class, the regular languages (Kleene's theorem).","part":0},{"p":"Nested quantifiers like (a+)+$ make backtracking engines try about 2^n splits: ReDoS. Linear-time engines such as RE2 avoid it.","part":0},{"p":"Injection attacks are grammar confusion: user input reinterpreted as SQL, shell or HTML.","part":1},{"p":"Rice's theorem: every non-trivial property of what a program does is undecidable, so perfect malware detection is impossible.","part":3},{"p":"NP means verifiable in polynomial time, not 'not polynomial'; SAT is NP-complete (Cook–Levin, 1971).","part":4}]},"p14m1":{"big":"Logic gives the word 'true' exact rules: truth tables settle compound statements, four proof shapes move from known to unknown without error, and sets and functions supply the vocabulary.","why":"Firewall rules, access policies and exploit chains are chains of implications, and reading them exactly is the difference between a rule that protects and one that silently allows everything.","parts":[{"t":"Propositions and connectives","gist":"A proposition is definitely true or false, and a truth table checks every combination. 'If p then q' is false only when p is true and q is false. Its contrapositive means the same; its converse does not.","core":true},{"t":"Predicates and quantifiers","gist":"A predicate is a statement with a blank, and quantifiers fill it: 'for all' is Python's all(), 'there exists' is any(). Negating flips the quantifier: 'not all ports are open' means 'some port is closed.'","core":true},{"t":"Four ways to prove","gist":"Four proof shapes cover computing: direct, contrapositive, contradiction, and induction, which proves a base case and that each case implies the next. A recursive function or a loop invariant is induction in code.","core":true},{"t":"Sets, relations and functions","gist":"Sets, relations and functions are the vocabulary of later math. A bijection pairs two sets perfectly, and a function from more inputs than outputs must collide: that pigeonhole principle is why hash collisions exist.","core":true},{"t":"Where it meets your work","gist":"Access-control rules are propositional logic, so De Morgan's laws expose rules that never or always fire. Fuzzers hunt for counterexamples, SMT solvers check properties against a model, and 6.431x builds probability on sets.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"p → q is false only when p is true and q is false; its contrapositive ¬q → ¬p is equivalent, its converse q → p is not.","part":0},{"p":"De Morgan's laws: ¬(p ∧ q) ≡ ¬p ∨ ¬q and ¬(p ∨ q) ≡ ¬p ∧ ¬q.","part":0},{"p":"Negation flips quantifiers: ¬∀x P(x) ≡ ∃x ¬P(x), so 'not all ports are open' means 'some port is closed.'","part":1},{"p":"Induction: prove the base case P(1), then prove that P(n) implies P(n + 1).","part":2},{"p":"A hash from arbitrary files to 256-bit digests cannot be injective, so collisions must exist: the pigeonhole principle.","part":3}]},"p14m2":{"big":"RSA is wrap-around arithmetic with a huge modulus: anyone can lock with the public key, and only the private key unlocks, because factoring n is infeasible.","why":"TLS, SSH, code signing, checksums and integer-overflow bugs in a disassembler all run on this arithmetic, and the cryptography domain assumes you already own it.","parts":[{"t":"Divisibility and primes","gist":"Division gives a = qb + r, where r is a mod b. Every integer above 1 factors into primes in exactly one way, and about 1 in 1,420 numbers near 2^2048 is prime, so finding big primes is fast.","core":true},{"t":"The greatest common divisor and Euclid","gist":"The Euclidean algorithm finds gcd(a, b) using gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b), in logarithmic time. Run backwards, it gives x and y with ax + by = gcd(a, b), which is how you find modular inverses.","core":true},{"t":"Modular arithmetic","gist":"Numbers are congruent mod n when they share a remainder, and you can reduce at every step. A number has a modular inverse exactly when it is coprime to n, which is why 4 has none mod 12.","core":true},{"t":"Fermat, Euler and fast exponentiation","gist":"Euler's theorem says a^φ(n) ≡ 1 (mod n) for any a coprime to n, with φ(pq) = (p − 1)(q − 1). Square-and-multiply computes huge powers fast by squaring and reducing at each step.","core":true},{"t":"Why RSA works","gist":"Pick primes p and q, publish n = pq and e, and keep d = e⁻¹ mod φ(n) private. Euler's theorem makes c^d return m. Finding d needs φ(n), which needs n's factors, and factoring a 2,048-bit n is infeasible.","core":true},{"t":"Binary and hexadecimal arithmetic","gist":"Hardware arithmetic is modular too: unsigned n-bit math wraps modulo 2ⁿ, two's complement stores −x as 2ⁿ − x, AND applies subnet masks, and XOR is addition mod 2 and its own inverse.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Euclid's step is gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b); for example gcd(252, 105) = 21.","part":1},{"p":"a has an inverse mod n exactly when gcd(a, n) = 1; for example 17⁻¹ mod 3120 = 2753.","part":2},{"p":"For distinct primes, φ(pq) = (p − 1)(q − 1), and RSA chooses d so that ed ≡ 1 (mod φ(n)).","part":3},{"p":"RSA's security rests on factoring n being slow, not on exponentiation, which square-and-multiply makes fast.","part":4},{"p":"XOR is its own inverse, so reusing a one-time-pad key leaks m₁ XOR m₂ and the key drops out.","part":5}]},"p14m3":{"big":"Counting possibilities without listing them sets password search spaces and collision risk: length multiplies the search space, and collisions arrive after about the square root of the possible outputs.","why":"Every brute-force estimate, hash-length decision and password policy you will defend in a report is this arithmetic, and NIST SP 800-63B agrees: length beats complexity.","parts":[{"t":"The two rules","gist":"Independent choices multiply, so length dominates: 95⁸ printable passwords fall in about 18 hours at 10¹¹ guesses per second, while 95¹² takes about 171,000 years. Slow, salted hashing cuts the guess rate further.","core":true},{"t":"Permutations and combinations","gist":"Permutations count ordered picks, P(n, k) = n!/(n − k)!; combinations ignore order, C(n, k) = n!/(k!(n − k)!). There are C(52, 5) = 2,598,960 poker hands, and the same numbers appear in the binomial theorem.","core":true},{"t":"The pigeonhole principle","gist":"If n + 1 pigeons share n holes, two share one. So any hash from all files to 256-bit digests must have collisions, and a lossless compressor that shrinks some files must expand others.","core":true},{"t":"Inclusion–exclusion","gist":"Adding two sets' sizes double-counts their overlap, so subtract it. Often it is faster to count what you don't want and subtract it from the total, as with passwords containing at least one digit.","core":true},{"t":"The birthday paradox and hash collisions","gist":"23 people give a better than even chance of a shared birthday because they form 253 pairs. With N outputs, a collision is likely after about 1.177√N samples, so a hash gives only half its length in collision resistance.","core":true},{"t":"From counting to probability","gist":"When outcomes are equally likely, probability is favorable counts over total counts, such as 6/36 for two dice summing to 7. These counting habits carry straight into the probability modules.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"95⁸ ≈ 6.6 × 10¹⁵ eight-character passwords fall in about 18 hours at 10¹¹ guesses per second; 95¹² takes about 171,000 years.","part":0},{"p":"C(n, k) = n!/(k!(n − k)!); for example C(52, 5) = 2,598,960 poker hands.","part":1},{"p":"Pigeonhole: a lossless compressor that shrinks some files must expand others, so random or encrypted data does not compress.","part":2},{"p":"Birthday bound: a 50% collision chance after about 1.177√N samples; 23 people already give a 0.507 chance of a match.","part":4},{"p":"A hash gives only half its length in collision resistance: SHA-256 offers 128-bit, and a 128-bit digest only about 2⁶⁴.","part":4}]},"p14m4":{"big":"A matrix is a transformation that moves every point at once, and neural-network layers, attention and 3-D graphics all come down to multiplying matrices.","why":"Once you see the matrix, you can estimate the cost of an AI model, a GPU job or a 3-D scene on paper. The 18.6501x course also assumes you can multiply a matrix by a vector by hand.","parts":[{"t":"Vectors","gist":"A vector is an ordered list of numbers. The dot product multiplies matching entries and adds them up, and it measures how well two vectors point the same way. Cosine similarity uses it to rank stored text chunks.","core":true},{"t":"Matrices are transformations","gist":"A matrix is a function that moves every point of space, and its columns show where the unit vectors land. Multiplying matrices chains transformations, so order matters. Two n×n matrices cost n³ multiply-adds.","core":true},{"t":"Systems, rank, inverse and determinant","gist":"Ax = b asks which input A sends to b. Rank counts the independent columns. The determinant says how A scales area; when it is zero, a dimension was flattened and no inverse exists.","core":true},{"t":"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors","gist":"An eigenvector is a direction the matrix only stretches, never turns, and its eigenvalue is the stretch factor. PageRank, the long-run state of a Markov chain and PCA are all eigenvector problems.","core":true},{"t":"Why AI and graphics are matrix multiplication","gist":"A network layer computes y = Wx + b, attention multiplies every query with every key, and 3-D graphics moves each vertex with a 4×4 matrix. All are matrix products, so you can price them by counting multiply-adds.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"The dot product adds up matching products: (3, 4) · (1, 2) = 11. Cosine similarity, u · v / (|u||v|), is how retrieval ranks text chunks.","part":0},{"p":"Matrix multiplication is composition, so order matters: AB ≠ BA in general. Multiplying two n×n matrices the schoolbook way takes n³ multiply-adds.","part":1},{"p":"For [[a, b], [c, d]] the determinant is ad − bc. A determinant of 0 means the matrix is singular and has no inverse.","part":2},{"p":"An eigenvector satisfies Av = λv: for [[2, 1], [1, 2]], (1, 1) has eigenvalue 3 and (1, −1) has eigenvalue 1.","part":3},{"p":"Attention is QKᵀ, a matrix product whose cost grows with the square of the context length, which is why long context windows are expensive.","part":4}]},"p14m5":{"big":"Probability is careful reasoning about uncertainty, and Bayes' theorem shows that when something is rare, most positives from even an accurate test or detector are false alarms.","why":"Alert triage, risk figures such as annual loss expectancy, and uptime arithmetic all rest on these ideas. Understanding the base-rate fallacy alone can spare you a career of false alarms.","parts":[{"t":"Models and axioms","gist":"A probability model is a sample space, the list of possible outcomes, plus Kolmogorov's three axioms. Rules like the complement, 1 − P(A), follow from them. Write the sample space down before computing anything.","core":true},{"t":"Conditioning and independence","gist":"P(A | B) is the chance of A once you know B happened. Events are independent when knowing one tells you nothing about the other, and that is an assumption you must justify, never a default.","core":true},{"t":"Bayes' theorem","gist":"Bayes' theorem reverses a conditional. When the condition is rare, false positives swamp true ones: with 1% prevalence, 99% sensitivity and 5% false positives, a positive test means only about a 17% chance of being sick.","core":true},{"t":"Random variables, expectation, variance","gist":"A random variable attaches a number to each outcome. Its expectation is the long-run average, 3.5 for a fair die, and its variance measures spread. Risk analysis multiplies probability by impact to get expected loss.","core":true},{"t":"The named distributions","gist":"A few named distributions cover most cases: binomial for successes in n trials, geometric for trials until the first success, Poisson for counts of rare events, exponential for waiting times, and normal for sums of many small effects.","core":true},{"t":"Law of large numbers and the central limit theorem","gist":"The average of many independent samples settles toward the expectation, which is why Monte Carlo simulation works. The central limit theorem adds that averages come out roughly normal, with spread shrinking like σ/√n.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"At 1 malicious event in 10,000, a detector catching 99% with 1% false alarms is right only about 1% of the times it alerts.","part":2},{"p":"Independent means P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B), and it must be justified. Disjoint events, which never happen together, are a different thing.","part":1},{"p":"Annual loss expectancy is yearly probability times impact: a 2% yearly chance of a $500,000 breach is a $10,000-per-year expected loss.","part":3},{"p":"In a normal distribution about 68% of values lie within one σ of the mean, 95% within two and 99.7% within three.","part":4},{"p":"Uncertainty shrinks like 1/√n, so halving it costs four times the data.","part":5}]},"p14m6":{"big":"Every estimate from a sample wobbles by a measurable amount, and statistics tells you whether a difference is real or just luck without fooling yourself.","why":"Benchmarks, detector comparisons and vulnerability statistics are all noisy data. This is how you report them honestly, without a claim you later have to retract.","parts":[{"t":"Populations, samples, estimators","gist":"A parameter is the true but unknown value; an estimator turns your sample into a guess at it. The guess is itself random, and its standard error, σ/√n, says how much it would wobble.","core":true},{"t":"Confidence intervals","gist":"A 95% confidence interval is the estimate plus or minus 1.96 standard errors. It means 95% of intervals built this way would contain the true value, not that the true value has a 95% chance of being inside.","core":true},{"t":"Hypothesis tests and p-values","gist":"A test asks how surprising your data would be if the null hypothesis, 'no difference', were true; that probability is the p-value. It is not the chance the null is true, and testing many things inflates false positives.","core":true},{"t":"A/B tests","gist":"An A/B test compares two conversion rates. B's 240 of 10,000 against A's 200 looks like a 20% lift but gives p ≈ 0.054, not significant. Peeking at results and hunting through segments create false winners.","core":true},{"t":"Least squares and the linear model","gist":"Least squares fits a line by making the squared gaps between the data and the line as small as possible, and r² says how much of the variation the line explains. Correlation still is not causation.","core":true},{"t":"Reading statistics like a professional","gist":"Habits of a careful reader: report percentiles like p95 and p99, not just means, repeat benchmarks, and watch for Simpson's paradox, survivorship bias and selection bias. Ends with how 18.6501x's units map onto this lesson.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A 95% confidence interval is estimate ± 1.96 × standard error; a 1,000-person poll has a margin of about ±3.1 points, whatever the population size.","part":1},{"p":"The p-value is the probability of data at least this extreme if the null hypothesis is true. It is not the probability that the null is true.","part":2},{"p":"Running 20 tests at α = 0.05 on pure noise gives a 64% chance of at least one false 'discovery'; Bonferroni uses α/m for m tests.","part":2},{"p":"Peeking, stopping the first time p dips under 0.05, inflates false positives several-fold. The cure is a sample size fixed in advance.","part":3},{"p":"Least squares minimizes the sum of squared residuals and r² grades the fit, but correlation is not causation.","part":4}]},"p14m7":{"big":"Learning is walking downhill: the derivative gives the slope, gradient descent steps against it, and Shannon's entropy and capacity set hard limits on compression and communication.","why":"This is the arithmetic behind neural-network training loops, the entropy scans that spot packed malware, and the fiber loss budgets you read off a power meter on the job.","parts":[{"t":"Derivatives","gist":"The derivative is the slope of a function at a point: how fast it is changing there. A derivative of zero marks a flat point, which is what optimization hunts for. You can always check a derivative numerically.","core":true},{"t":"Gradients and the chain rule","gist":"The gradient collects the slope in every direction and points steepest uphill. The chain rule multiplies rates along a chain of functions, and backpropagation is that rule worked backwards through a network's layers.","core":true},{"t":"Gradient descent","gist":"Gradient descent takes small steps against the gradient to reach a minimum. The learning rate sets the step size: on x², a rate of 0.1 converges, while 1.1 overshoots the valley and diverges.","core":true},{"t":"Entropy and information","gist":"Entropy is the average surprise of a source, in bits: a fair coin carries 1 bit, a 90/10 coin about 0.47. No lossless code can beat it, and cross-entropy is a common training loss for language models.","core":true},{"t":"Shannon's channel capacity","gist":"Shannon's formula, C = B log₂(1 + S/N), caps how fast any channel can carry data with low error. Doubling bandwidth doubles capacity, but doubling power adds only one bit per symbol.","core":true},{"t":"Decibels, your field, and safety","gist":"Decibels turn multiplied losses into added ones, so fiber loss budgets become simple sums. Never look into a fiber with the naked eye; the light is invisible and can damage the retina. Treat each fiber shard as a sharp.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Gradient descent updates θ ← θ − η∇L. Too large a learning rate overshoots the valley and diverges.","part":2},{"p":"Backpropagation is the chain rule organized backwards: multiply the local derivatives along the path from a weight to the output.","part":1},{"p":"Entropy is average surprise in bits: a fair coin is 1 bit, an 8-sided die 3. Encrypted or compressed data sit near 8 bits per byte.","part":3},{"p":"Channel capacity is C = B log₂(1 + S/N): it grows linearly with bandwidth but only logarithmically with power.","part":4},{"p":"Every 3 dB halves or doubles power, 10 dB is a factor of ten, and 0 dBm is 1 mW.","part":5}]},"p15m1":{"big":"C gives you the machine's view of the world, bytes, addresses and registers behind your variables, and gcc turns it into machine code in four stages you can watch.","why":"The binaries you will reverse engineer in Domain 11 were almost always C, and the memory bugs pentesters report in Domain 8 are C bugs. Reading C fluently opens up the lower half of computing.","parts":[{"t":"The compilation pipeline","gist":"gcc hides four stages: the preprocessor pastes in headers and expands macros, the compiler turns C into assembly, the assembler encodes that as machine code in an object file, and the linker stitches everything into an executable.","core":true},{"t":"Types are sizes","gist":"A C type says how many bytes a value takes and how to read them. On 64-bit Linux, int is 4 bytes and long and pointers are 8. Unsigned math wraps around; signed overflow is undefined behavior.","core":true},{"t":"Pointers and arrays","gist":"A pointer holds an address plus a type that says what lives there, and adding 1 moves it by the size of that type. An array handed to a function becomes a bare pointer and loses its length.","core":true},{"t":"Strings, structs and alignment","gist":"A C string is bytes ending in a zero byte, and strcpy and gets ignore the destination's size. The compiler pads structs so each field is aligned, so the order of fields changes the struct's size.","core":true},{"t":"The standard library","gist":"A quick tour of the small C library: printf formats, file functions, malloc and free, strtol instead of atoi, and the man pages that document it all on your own machine.","core":false},{"t":"Reading C as the gateway to reverse engineering","gist":"Every C construct compiles to a recognizable shape: loops become compare-and-jump, struct fields become fixed offsets, and arguments arrive in set registers. Comparing your own code at -O0 and -O2 makes the shapes automatic.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The four stages are preprocess (gcc -E), compile (-S), assemble (-c) and link (ld).","part":0},{"p":"On 64-bit Linux, char, short, int, long and pointers are 1, 2, 4, 8 and 8 bytes. On Windows, long is only 4.","part":1},{"p":"Pointer arithmetic is scaled by the pointee size: if an int pointer p holds 0x1000, p + 1 is 0x1004.","part":2},{"p":"An array passed to a function decays to a pointer and loses its length: sizeof on a ten-int array is 40, on a pointer 8.","part":2},{"p":"struct { char c; int i; char d; } takes 12 bytes because of padding; reordered as int, char, char it takes 8.","part":3}]},"p15m2":{"big":"A program's memory is a map of regions with different rules, and almost every security bug of the last forty years is a program breaking one of them.","why":"Memory corruption is the bug class behind most serious CVEs. Understanding it defensively, along with the mitigations that stop it, is the core of exploit-aware penetration testing and reverse engineering.","parts":[{"t":"The address space","gist":"From the bottom up: code, constants, globals, the heap growing upward, shared libraries, and the stack growing down from near the top. On a modern system no region is both writable and executable.","core":true},{"t":"The stack","gist":"Each function call pushes a frame holding the return address, the saved frame pointer and the locals. Locals vanish when the function returns, so returning a local's address leaves a dangling pointer.","core":true},{"t":"The heap and the allocator","gist":"malloc hands out heap memory that lives until you free it. glibc rounds each request up to a 16-byte-aligned chunk with a small header, and requests of 128 KiB or more get their own mmap region.","core":true},{"t":"Undefined behavior: the contract you did not read","gist":"The C standard lists more than 200 situations, such as signed overflow or use after free, where any outcome is allowed. The compiler assumes they never happen, so the bug may move or vanish instead of crashing.","core":true},{"t":"Buffer overflows, explained defensively","gist":"Writing past a stack buffer can overwrite the return address and redirect execution. Canaries, NX, ASLR, RELRO and shadow stacks each block one stage, which is why they are layered. Practise only on systems you own.","core":true},{"t":"Tools that see memory","gist":"Valgrind runs your program on a simulated CPU at a 20–30× slowdown; AddressSanitizer is compiled in and costs roughly 2×. Both point straight at invalid reads and writes, use-after-free and leaks.","core":true},{"t":"Ownership discipline","gist":"Every allocation has exactly one owner, who frees it exactly once. Set the pointer to NULL after freeing and clean up in a single exit path. C++, Rust and CHERI build this same idea in.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The heap grows upward and the stack grows downward from near the top, 8 MiB by default (ulimit -s).","part":0},{"p":"A glibc chunk is (n + 8) rounded up to a multiple of 16, at least 32 bytes: malloc(24) costs 32 and malloc(100) costs 112.","part":2},{"p":"Undefined behavior does not mean a crash. Any result is permitted, and at -O2 GCC may simply delete a signed-overflow check.","part":3},{"p":"Canaries detect an overwritten return address, NX stops injected bytes running as code, and ASLR randomizes addresses so a hard-coded target misses.","part":4},{"p":"Build with -fsanitize=address to catch overflows, use-after-free and double free, at roughly a 2× slowdown.","part":5}]},"p15m3":{"big":"Between pressing Enter and seeing output, the kernel loads an ELF file, the loader links its libraries, and fork, exec and wait create and collect the process.","why":"ELF, the loader and strace are the first tools of binary analysis in Domain 11, and fork, exec and signals are tested in CLP block 5. Writing your own shell proves you understand all of it.","parts":[{"t":"ELF: the shape of a Linux executable","gist":"ELF is the file format for Linux programs and libraries, and it starts with the bytes 7f 45 4c 46. Its sections are the linker's view of the file; its segments tell the loader what to map into memory.","core":true},{"t":"Static versus dynamic linking","gist":"Static linking copies libc into your program, making hello world about 800 KB. Dynamic linking keeps it near 16 KB and finds the libraries at run time, calling them through the PLT and GOT tables.","core":true},{"t":"The loader and the first microseconds","gist":"When you run a program, the kernel maps its segments into memory and hands control to the dynamic loader, which maps the libraries and fixes up addresses. Then _start calls __libc_start_main, which calls main.","core":false},{"t":"System calls: the only door","gist":"Programs can't touch hardware or files directly; they ask the kernel through system calls. Each round trip costs on the order of 100–300 nanoseconds, and strace shows every call a program makes.","core":true},{"t":"Processes: fork, exec, wait","gist":"fork clones the current process and returns twice, 0 in the child. exec swaps in a new program, and the parent's waitpid collects the exit status. A finished child nobody has collected yet is a zombie.","core":true},{"t":"Signals and exit codes","gist":"Signals are small asynchronous messages, like SIGINT from Ctrl-C or SIGKILL, which can't be caught. Exit codes run from 0 to 255, and a process killed by signal N exits with 128 + N.","core":true},{"t":"Write a tiny shell","gist":"A working shell is a short loop: read a line, handle builtins like cd and exit yourself, otherwise fork, exec and wait. cd has to be a builtin, because a child changing directory wouldn't move the shell.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"An ELF file starts with the magic bytes 7f 45 4c 46: 0x7f followed by the letters ELF.","part":0},{"p":"LD_PRELOAD injects a library ahead of all others; defenders check /etc/ld.so.preload because it is a classic persistence trick.","part":1},{"p":"strace shows every system call a program makes, and strace -c counts the calls and the time spent in them.","part":3},{"p":"fork returns twice: 0 in the child, the child's PID in the parent. exec replaces the program but keeps the PID and open files.","part":4},{"p":"Killed by signal N means exit code 128 + N: 139 is a segfault (SIGSEGV, 11) and 137 is SIGKILL (9).","part":5}]},"p15m4":{"big":"The operating system performs two illusions: it shares one CPU core among many programs by switching fast, and it gives each process its own memory by translating every address.","why":"This is the mental model behind every performance tool you will run and every systems interview question, and the kernel side of attacks like Meltdown.","parts":[{"t":"Scheduling: who runs next","gist":"The scheduler decides who runs next. Linux's CFS ran the task with the least weighted CPU time, with nice values setting the weights. Since Linux 6.6, EEVDF keeps that fairness and adds deadlines for quicker response.","core":true},{"t":"Context switches","gist":"A context switch saves one task's registers, swaps the page-table base and restores the next task. The direct cost is about 1–5 microseconds, and the cold caches afterwards cost more.","core":true},{"t":"Virtual memory and page tables","gist":"Every address a program uses is virtual and is translated through a page table. On x86-64 that table is a four-level tree over 4 KiB pages, because a flat table would need 256 GiB per process.","core":true},{"t":"The TLB","gist":"The TLB is a small cache of recent address translations. Its reach is entries times page size: 2,048 entries cover 8 MiB with 4 KiB pages but 4 GiB with 2 MiB huge pages.","core":true},{"t":"Page faults, demand paging and copy-on-write","gist":"A page fault is normal: the CPU telling the kernel a translation is missing. Minor faults are served from RAM, major faults need the disk, and only a fault the kernel can't satisfy becomes SIGSEGV.","core":true},{"t":"Swapping, eviction and the OOM killer","gist":"When RAM fills, the kernel drops clean pages and writes others to swap. Thrashing means the machine spends its time faulting. If all else fails, the OOM killer sends SIGKILL, so the job exits with 137.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"nice 0 has weight 1024 and each nice step changes the weight by about 1.25×; nice 0 against nice 10 splits a core 90.3% to 9.7%.","part":0},{"p":"A 48-bit x86-64 address splits 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 + 12: four 512-entry table levels, then a 12-bit offset into a 4 KiB page.","part":2},{"p":"TLB reach is entries × page size: 2,048 × 4 KiB = 8 MiB, and with 2 MiB huge pages it is 4 GiB.","part":3},{"p":"Minor page faults are served from RAM; major faults go to disk, roughly 100 µs on NVMe and 10 ms on a hard drive.","part":4},{"p":"An OOM-killed job ends with exit code 137 (SIGKILL). Thrashing shows in vmstat as high si and so while CPU use falls.","part":5}]},"p15m5":{"big":"Threads share memory, so two of them updating the same data without coordination can lose writes; locks fix that but bring deadlock risk and Amdahl's limit on speedup.","why":"Race conditions are a real vulnerability class, such as time-of-check to time-of-use. Data races, deadlock and Amdahl's law are a standard interview trio, and CLP block 5 tests pthreads directly.","parts":[{"t":"Threads versus processes","gist":"Threads inside one process share its code, heap and open files, but each has its own stack and registers. Sharing makes communication easy and corruption easy. Processes are for isolation: one crashing doesn't take the others down.","core":true},{"t":"Data races","gist":"A data race is two threads touching the same memory without ordering, at least one of them writing. counter++ is really load, add, store, so interleaved threads lose increments. ThreadSanitizer finds races at run time.","core":true},{"t":"Mutexes and the cost of locking","gist":"A mutex lets only one thread at a time into a critical section; hold it briefly and never during I/O. Amdahl's law says the part that can't run in parallel caps your speedup, and that part is usually a lock.","core":true},{"t":"Condition variables","gist":"A condition variable lets a thread sleep until shared state changes, as in a producer–consumer queue. Always wait inside a while loop, never an if, because wakeups can be spurious.","core":true},{"t":"Deadlock","gist":"Deadlock is threads each holding a lock the other needs and waiting forever. It needs four conditions at once, so breaking any one prevents it; the usual fix is taking locks in one global order.","core":true},{"t":"Atomics and memory ordering, conceptually","gist":"Atomic operations happen in one indivisible step, so an atomic counter fixes counter++ with no lock. The subtle part is ordering: compilers and CPUs reorder memory operations for speed, and ARM reorders more than x86.","core":false},{"t":"Thread pools and event loops","gist":"A thread pool creates its workers once and feeds them from a queue; an event loop uses one thread that never blocks to watch thousands of sockets. Threads for computing, events for waiting, processes for isolation.","core":false},{"t":"Why Python has a GIL","gist":"CPython counts references on every object, and making that thread-safe everywhere would slow single-threaded code. So one global lock lets only one thread run Python bytecode at a time, though threads still overlap during I/O.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"counter++ is load, add, store, so two threads incrementing a shared counter lose updates. Find races with gcc -fsanitize=thread.","part":1},{"p":"Amdahl's law: speedup = 1 / ((1 − p) + p / N). With p = 0.95 on 16 cores the speedup is 9.1×, not 16×.","part":2},{"p":"Wait on a condition variable inside a while loop, never an if, because wakeups can be spurious.","part":3},{"p":"Deadlock needs mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait; a global lock ordering breaks circular wait.","part":4},{"p":"With the default CPython build, threads help I/O-bound Python but not CPU-bound Python; multiprocessing is how you use your cores.","part":7}]},"p15m6":{"big":"When write() returns, your data is almost certainly still in RAM, not on disk; fsync forces it there. A file is an inode, and names just point to it.","why":"Durability decisions and inode forensics, such as MFT timestamps, deleted-but-open files and journals, are daily on-call and investigation skills, and CLP block 3 tests the low-level I/O calls.","parts":[{"t":"File descriptors","gist":"A file descriptor is a small number: 0 is input, 1 output, 2 errors, and a newly opened file gets the lowest free one. The shell redirects with dup2, which is why the order of > f and 2>&1 matters.","core":true},{"t":"Three layers of buffering and the meaning of durability","gist":"printf fills a buffer inside your program, write() copies into the kernel's page cache, and the kernel writes to disk later, by default within about 30 seconds. Only fsync forces the data to the device and waits.","core":true},{"t":"mmap","gist":"mmap makes a file's bytes appear directly as memory, with no copying, and pages load on demand. The catch is that a disk I/O error then arrives as a SIGBUS in the middle of an ordinary memory access.","core":false},{"t":"Inodes, directories and links","gist":"An inode is the file itself: owner, size, times and where its blocks are, but no name. A directory maps names to inode numbers, so one file can have several hard links. A symlink just stores a path.","core":true},{"t":"Journaling and copy-on-write file systems","gist":"A crash halfway through updating several blocks leaves a file system inconsistent. Journaling writes the change to a log first, so recovery takes seconds; copy-on-write file systems never overwrite live blocks, which gives cheap snapshots.","core":true},{"t":"ext4 versus NTFS versus APFS","gist":"A side-by-side comparison: ext4 uses inodes and a journal, NTFS keeps every file as a record in the Master File Table, and APFS is copy-on-write with snapshots. exFAT is for transport, not storage.","core":false},{"t":"The VFS and the I/O stack","gist":"The kernel's virtual file system layer gives every file system the same operations, so open, read and write work identically on ext4, network shares, /proc and FUSE. Below it sit the block layer and the drivers.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"cmd > f 2>&1 sends both streams to the file; cmd 2>&1 > f sends errors to the terminal, because 2>&1 copied descriptor 1 first.","part":0},{"p":"write() returning means the data reached the page cache. fsync forces it to the device, costing roughly 0.1–1 ms on NVMe.","part":1},{"p":"A hard link is another name for the same inode; a symbolic link is a separate tiny file containing a path.","part":3},{"p":"A deleted file keeps its blocks until the last open descriptor closes; lsof +L1 finds the process holding it.","part":3},{"p":"ext4's default ordered mode journals metadata only, so data written just before a crash can still be lost.","part":4}]},"p15m7":{"big":"A compiler is a pipeline: text becomes tokens, a syntax tree, then intermediate code that optimization passes rewrite, and finally machine instructions.","why":"Knowing which pass produced each strange shape is what makes Ghidra output readable in Domain 11, and Make, CMake and GDB are tools you will drive on every C project you audit or build.","parts":[{"t":"From characters to syntax trees","gist":"The lexer splits source text into tokens, such as keywords, names and symbols. The parser arranges them into a syntax tree whose shape encodes precedence, and semantic analysis checks names and types using a symbol table.","core":true},{"t":"Intermediate representation and optimization","gist":"The tree is lowered to a simple intermediate code, then optimization passes rewrite it in small, provable steps: folding constants, deleting unused code, inlining functions, turning a * 8 into a shift. The -O levels choose which passes run.","core":true},{"t":"Code generation","gist":"The back end picks real instructions, orders them for the pipeline and fits values into the 16 general-purpose registers. At -O2, return a * 8 + 1 becomes a single lea instruction.","core":true},{"t":"The nand2tetris path: from one gate to Tetris","gist":"A course path that builds a whole computer in twelve projects, from logic gates through a CPU, an assembler, a VM, a compiler and an operating system, until your own game runs on it.","core":false},{"t":"Make and CMake","gist":"Make rebuilds only the targets whose inputs changed, and recipe lines must start with a tab. CMake doesn't build anything itself; it generates Makefiles or Ninja files from a portable description.","core":true},{"t":"Debugging with GDB","gist":"GDB lets you stop a program at breakpoints, step through it, print variables, examine memory, watch a variable change and read a backtrace. With core dumps enabled, it shows where a crash happened after the fact.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"The pipeline: lexer makes tokens, parser builds an AST, semantic analysis checks it with a symbol table, then IR, optimization passes and code generation.","part":0},{"p":"Dead-code elimination removes work whose result is never used, which is why a naive benchmark can end up measuring nothing.","part":1},{"p":"At -O2, return a * 8 + 1 compiles to lea eax, [rdi*8+1]: one instruction doing the multiply and the add.","part":2},{"p":"Make rebuilds only what changed and needs tab-indented recipes; CMake only generates build files for Make or Ninja.","part":4},{"p":"In GDB, backtrace shows the call chain, watch stops when a variable changes, and gdb ./prog core shows where a crash happened.","part":5}]},"p16m1":{"big":"A running Windows system is a few hundred processes, each a container of memory, threads, handles and an access token, all asking one privileged kernel for service.","why":"Every troubleshooting session, malware triage and privilege-escalation finding in a pentest report is a story about these objects, and the Sysinternals triage recipe is one you will use for the rest of your career.","parts":[{"t":"Two modes, one kernel","gist":"Applications run in user mode; the kernel, its executive managers and drivers run in kernel mode. Programs reach the kernel only through ntdll.dll, and because drivers share the kernel's space, a buggy driver crashes the whole machine.","core":true},{"t":"Processes, threads, handles, tokens","gist":"A process is a container: its own address space, a handle table, an access token and at least one thread. The token says who the process is: its user and group SIDs, its privileges and its integrity level.","core":true},{"t":"Services","gist":"Services are background programs run by the Service Control Manager, services.exe, many hosted inside svchost.exe. The account a service runs as sets how much a compromise can reach; LocalSystem has more power than Administrator.","core":true},{"t":"The registry","gist":"The registry is a database of settings, stored on disk as hive files plus an NTUSER.DAT for each user. Its auto-start keys, like Run and RunOnce, are where both admins and attackers make programs come back.","core":true},{"t":"NTFS and access control","gist":"Every NTFS file carries a security descriptor saying who may do what. Explicit entries beat inherited ones, Deny beats Allow at the same level, and on a shared folder the stricter of share and NTFS permissions wins.","core":true},{"t":"Sysinternals: seeing the machine","gist":"The free Sysinternals tools show what Task Manager hides. The triage recipe: Process Explorer for what runs and whether it's signed, Autoruns for how it comes back, Procmon for what it touches.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"Where this material shows up on MD-102 and AZ-802, and why every later Windows module assumes this vocabulary.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Programs reach the kernel only through ntdll.dll's syscall. An app crash kills one process; a buggy driver brings down the machine with a blue screen.","part":0},{"p":"S-1-5-18 is LocalSystem; a domain SID ending in 500 is the built-in Administrator, 512 Domain Admins, 519 Enterprise Admins.","part":1},{"p":"The Run and RunOnce keys under HKLM and HKCU are classic auto-start points, and Autoruns lists every one.","part":3},{"p":"Share and NTFS permissions intersect: the more restrictive wins. Explicit beats inherited, and Deny beats Allow at the same level.","part":4},{"p":"Triage: Process Explorer for what runs and whether it's signed, Autoruns for how it comes back, Procmon for what it touches.","part":5}]},"p16m2":{"big":"PowerShell passes objects, not text, down its pipeline, so you ask for a property by name instead of parsing columns.","why":"It is the one interface that reaches files, the registry, Active Directory, Intune and Azure with one grammar. It automates your audits and is also the attacker's favorite tool, so you must be able to read it both ways.","parts":[{"t":"Objects, not text","gist":"Bash pipes text and you cut out columns; PowerShell pipes objects and you simply ask for a property, like Id. Get-Member shows what any object carries. Format-Table and Format-List always go last.","core":true},{"t":"Verb-Noun and discoverability","gist":"Every cmdlet is named Verb-Noun, using about a hundred approved verbs, so knowing the noun is enough to find commands with Get-Command. Get-Help reads the manual, and -WhatIf previews a destructive command.","core":true},{"t":"The pipeline toolkit","gist":"The everyday toolkit: Where-Object filters, Select-Object picks or computes properties, Sort-Object, Group-Object and Measure-Object shape results, and Export-Csv hands them on. Comparison operators are words, like -eq and -gt.","core":true},{"t":"Two PowerShells","gist":"Windows PowerShell 5.1 ships in every Windows; PowerShell 7.6 installs alongside it and also runs on Linux and macOS. Execution policy only prevents accidents, since one flag bypasses it, so it is not a real security control.","core":true},{"t":"Scripting: functions, modules, errors","gist":"A function with [CmdletBinding()] and a typed param() block behaves like a real cmdlet. Use -ErrorAction Stop so try/catch actually catches errors, and package your functions as modules.","core":true},{"t":"Remoting and scheduled tasks","gist":"Remoting runs commands on other machines over WinRM, with Kerberos inside a domain, or over SSH, and Invoke-Command fans out to many machines at once. Scheduled tasks automate jobs and should run as a low-privilege account.","core":true},{"t":"Logging: what defenders see","gist":"Script Block Logging records every script block, after de-obfuscation, as event 4104, and AMSI lets Defender inspect script content before it runs. That is how defenders see PowerShell being misused.","core":false},{"t":"Bash and PowerShell, side by side","gist":"A small translation table between Bash and PowerShell: finding big files, pulling out a field, reading the exit status and running a command on a remote host.","core":false},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"How MD-102 and AZ-802 cover PowerShell. Both show you cmdlets and ask what they do, so Verb-Noun fluency pays off directly.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Format-* goes last: it emits formatting objects, so anything piped after it, such as Export-Csv, produces garbage.","part":0},{"p":"Get-Command finds commands, Get-Help explains them, and Get-Member shows any object's properties and methods.","part":1},{"p":"Execution policy prevents accidental script runs, not attackers: -ExecutionPolicy Bypass is one flag away.","part":3},{"p":"-ErrorAction Stop turns a non-terminating error into a terminating one, so the catch block fires.","part":4},{"p":"WinRM remoting uses HTTP 5985 or HTTPS 5986, and Invoke-Command fans out to 32 machines at a time.","part":5}]},"p16m3":{"big":"Windows Server is the same NT kernel sold with the roles that run a business: DNS, DHCP, files, printing and Hyper-V, managed remotely and licensed per physical core.","why":"This is four of AZ-802's seven domains and arithmetic real buyers get wrong, and it is the lab platform on which you build every later module.","parts":[{"t":"Editions and what 2025 added","gist":"Standard allows 2 VMs per full set of licenses, Datacenter unlimited VMs plus extra storage and networking features, and Essentials suits one small-business server. Windows Server 2025 also tightened defaults, such as requiring LDAP signing for new deployments.","core":false},{"t":"Server Core versus Desktop Experience","gist":"Server Core has no desktop; you manage it remotely with PowerShell, RSAT or Windows Admin Center. That means a smaller attack surface and fewer reboots. Choose at install time, because since 2016 you can't switch later.","core":true},{"t":"Roles and features","gist":"A role is a job the server does, like DNS, DHCP or Hyper-V; a feature supports roles, like Failover Clustering or BitLocker. You install both with Server Manager or Install-WindowsFeature.","core":true},{"t":"DNS and DHCP, the Windows way","gist":"Windows DNS can store zones in Active Directory and lets members register their own records, while domain controllers publish SRV records so clients can find them. DHCP hands out leases, 8 days by default, and must be authorized in AD.","core":true},{"t":"File and print services","gist":"The effective right on a share is the stricter of share and NTFS permissions, so the convention is Full for Authenticated Users on the share and precise NTFS permissions below. Disable the Print Spooler wherever nothing prints.","core":true},{"t":"Hyper-V","gist":"Hyper-V is a type-1 hypervisor. Use Generation 2 VMs with a virtual TPM, keep standard checkpoints away from domain controllers, and pick external, internal or private switches. Nested virtualization fits a whole lab in one box.","core":true},{"t":"Licensing arithmetic","gist":"You license every physical core, at least 8 per processor and 16 per server. A fully licensed Standard host runs two VMs; Datacenter runs unlimited VMs and wins at about twelve per host. Users or devices also need CALs.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"Which AZ-802 domains this covers, and the scenarios to expect: Windows Admin Center, Hyper-V generations and checkpoints, DHCP failover, DNS forwarders and share versus NTFS permissions.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The Server Core or Desktop Experience choice is permanent: since Windows Server 2016 you cannot switch after install.","part":1},{"p":"A Windows DHCP server must be authorized in AD. Key options: 003 router, 006 DNS servers, 015 domain name.","part":3},{"p":"Effective access to a shared folder is the more restrictive of the share permissions and the NTFS permissions.","part":4},{"p":"Generation 2 Hyper-V VMs bring UEFI, Secure Boot and a virtual TPM, which Windows 11 guests need.","part":5},{"p":"License every physical core, minimum 8 per processor and 16 per server; Standard covers two VMs per full set, Datacenter unlimited.","part":6}]},"p16m4":{"big":"Active Directory does three jobs at once, a directory of identities, Kerberos authentication and Group Policy configuration, all tied to DNS so machines can find a domain controller.","why":"Deploying and managing AD DS is the largest AZ-802 domain, at 20–25%. A tester who can't read AD is blind in an enterprise, and an admin who can't design it builds the weaknesses attackers use.","parts":[{"t":"The database and its boundaries","gist":"Every domain controller holds a copy of the directory database. The forest is the real security boundary; a domain only bounds replication, policy and naming. The modern design is one forest, one domain, named like corp.example.com.","core":true},{"t":"OUs, users, groups, service accounts","gist":"OUs exist to link Group Policy and delegate administration; they don't grant access, groups do. Follow AGDLP: accounts into global groups, global groups into domain local groups, which get the permissions. Run services as gMSAs.","core":true},{"t":"Kerberos, step by step","gist":"At logon you get a ticket-granting ticket, then trade it for a service ticket the service decrypts with its own key, so no password crosses the wire. Clocks must agree within 5 minutes; NTLM is the deprecated fallback.","core":true},{"t":"Group Policy","gist":"GPOs apply in the order Local, Site, Domain, OU, and the last writer wins, so the closest OU has the final word. Enforced overrides Block Inheritance. Clients refresh every 90 minutes plus up to 30 random minutes.","core":true},{"t":"DNS dependency and FSMO roles","gist":"Clients find domain controllers through DNS SRV records, so no DNS means no domain. Five special jobs, the FSMO roles, each need a single owner, such as the RID Master and the busy PDC Emulator.","core":true},{"t":"Replication","gist":"Domain controllers replicate changes using update sequence numbers: 15 seconds after a change inside a site, every 180 minutes by default between sites. Never restore a DC from a VM snapshot, because of USN rollback.","core":true},{"t":"Design a small domain","gist":"A worked design for forty users in two sites: two Server Core DCs at headquarters, one at the branch, tiered OUs, AGDLP groups, baseline GPOs, gMSAs, Protected Users, backups and monitoring.","core":false},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"'Deploy and manage AD DS' is AZ-802's largest domain. Know the FSMO roles, the Kerberos defaults, the GPO order and the replication intervals cold.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The forest, not the domain, is the security boundary: a child-domain admin can escalate to the whole forest.","part":0},{"p":"AGDLP: Accounts go into Global groups, those into Domain Local groups, which get the Permissions. OUs never grant access.","part":1},{"p":"Kerberos uses port 88; a TGT lasts 10 hours, renewable for 7 days, and clocks must agree within 5 minutes.","part":2},{"p":"GPO order is Local, Site, Domain, OU, last writer wins; Enforced beats Block Inheritance; refresh every 90 minutes plus up to 30.","part":3},{"p":"The five FSMO roles: Schema Master and Domain Naming Master (one per forest), plus RID Master, PDC Emulator and Infrastructure Master.","part":4}]},"p16m5":{"big":"Cloud identity in Entra ID plus device management in Intune lets you manage laptops that never touch the office, with Conditional Access judging every sign-in.","why":"This is the half of Windows administration every employer now expects, and MD-102, which this module covers, is the first certification in this domain's study order.","parts":[{"t":"Entra ID is not Active Directory in the cloud","gist":"Entra ID is a flat cloud directory that speaks web protocols like OAuth 2.0 and SAML, with no OUs, Kerberos or Group Policy. Groups drive assignment, Intune replaces Group Policy, and the P1 tier adds Conditional Access.","core":true},{"t":"Hybrid identity","gist":"Most organizations sync their on-premises AD into Entra ID with Connect Sync or Cloud Sync, ideally with password hash synchronization. Devices are Entra registered for BYOD, Entra joined for new corporate hardware, or Hybrid joined for existing fleets.","core":true},{"t":"Intune: management at scale","gist":"Intune manages devices over the internet: compliance policies decide whether a device is healthy, configuration profiles and endpoint security set it up, and apps and updates are pushed to it. Remote actions include wipe and key rotation.","core":true},{"t":"Windows Autopilot","gist":"Autopilot turns a factory-sealed laptop into a managed device without imaging: upload its hardware hash, assign a profile, and the user just signs in. The modes are user-driven, pre-provisioning and self-deploying.","core":true},{"t":"Conditional Access, MFA and Windows Hello","gist":"Conditional Access applies if-then rules to every sign-in: user, app, device state and risk decide whether to block or require MFA or a compliant device. Windows Hello for Business and passkeys resist phishing; SMS doesn't.","core":true},{"t":"The MD-102 skill set","gist":"What exam MD-102 measures, domain by domain, plus the practical details: 100 minutes, a pass mark of 700 out of 1,000 and a free yearly renewal. It is the one to take first.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Entra ID has no OUs, Kerberos or Group Policy; it speaks OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect and SAML over HTTPS.","part":0},{"p":"Password hash synchronization is Microsoft's recommended default sign-in method, and Connect Sync synchronizes every 30 minutes.","part":1},{"p":"Entra registered is for BYOD, Entra joined is the target for new corporate devices, and Hybrid Entra joined bridges existing domain fleets.","part":1},{"p":"Autopilot modes: user-driven (the common case), pre-provisioning (device apps applied first by IT or the reseller) and self-deploying (no user, for kiosks).","part":3},{"p":"Start Conditional Access with MFA for all users, a compliant device for Microsoft 365, a block on legacy authentication, and two excluded break-glass accounts.","part":4}]},"p16m6":{"big":"Logging, hardening, patching and tested backups turn a built domain into one you can defend; the hard part is doing all of it, everywhere, every month.","why":"This is the operational core of both MD-102 and AZ-802 and of every blue-team job: what to log, what to turn on, how to keep machines current, how to recover and how to find out why a machine is slow.","parts":[{"t":"Event Viewer and audit policy","gist":"The Security log only records what audit policy asks for, so turn on the right categories and learn the key event IDs. The log defaults to just 20 MB, so make it much bigger and forward events to a SIEM.","core":true},{"t":"Microsoft Defender and the platform protections","gist":"Defender Antivirus is built in; add attack surface reduction rules, in audit mode first and then blocking, and Defender for Endpoint for EDR. Credential Guard and LSA protection keep LSASS secrets out of reach. Apply Microsoft's security baselines.","core":true},{"t":"BitLocker","gist":"BitLocker encrypts the disk and seals the key to the TPM, so it opens only on its own machine. Escrow the 48-digit recovery password to Entra ID or AD before encryption finishes, or a forgotten PIN means a wiped laptop.","core":true},{"t":"Windows LAPS","gist":"The same local Administrator password on every PC lets one compromise unlock them all. Windows LAPS gives each machine a unique random password, rotates it, by default every 30 days, and backs it up to Entra ID or AD.","core":true},{"t":"Windows Firewall","gist":"The host firewall blocks inbound and allows outbound traffic, with three profiles. Scope every inbound rule to source subnets, such as WinRM and RDP only from the management network, so one compromised PC can't reach its neighbors.","core":false},{"t":"Patching","gist":"WSUS is deprecated; Windows Update for Business uses rings, Autopatch lets Microsoft run them, and Hotpatch cuts reboots to four a year. Roll updates out in rings: 1% on day 0, 10% on day 3, everyone by day 7.","core":true},{"t":"Backup and recovery","gist":"Keep three copies on two media with one offline, so ransomware can't reach them all. Back up a domain controller by system state, restore deleted objects from the AD Recycle Bin, and test a restore every quarter.","core":true},{"t":"Performance troubleshooting","gist":"Work by method: define the symptom, measure, compare with a baseline, change one thing, verify. Rules of thumb: disk above 20 ms per transfer is slow storage, and CPU above 80% sustained means CPU-bound.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"Where this material sits on MD-102 and AZ-802. Both reward knowing the default numbers from this lesson.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Event IDs: 4624/4625 logon succeeded/failed, 4688 process created, 4740 account locked out, 1102 audit log cleared.","part":0},{"p":"The Security log defaults to 20 MB, which a busy DC can wrap in about eleven minutes; set 1 GB or more and forward it off-box.","part":0},{"p":"BitLocker's 48-digit recovery password must be escrowed to Entra ID or AD before encryption completes.","part":2},{"p":"Windows LAPS gives every machine a unique local Administrator password, rotated every 30 days by default.","part":3},{"p":"Back up a DC by system state, never by VM snapshot, which causes USN rollback. Follow 3-2-1, with one copy offline or immutable.","part":6}]},"p16m7":{"big":"Attackers read Active Directory as a map of paths to the domain controllers. Defenders read the same map first and remove those paths.","why":"Whoever controls a domain controller controls every machine and credential in the forest. Reading the map as a defender lets you walk it legitimately as a tester, but only on systems you own or have written authorization to test.","parts":[{"t":"The tiered administration model","gist":"Rank every asset by what its compromise would cost: Tier 0 is identity control, such as domain controllers, Tier 1 servers, Tier 2 workstations. Credentials never flow down: a Tier 0 admin never signs in to a lower tier.","core":true},{"t":"Common weaknesses, conceptually, and the fix for each","gist":"Most AD compromises use misconfigurations, not unpatched bugs: too many Domain Admins, one shared local admin password, legacy protocols, weak Kerberos settings, sprayable passwords and dangerous permissions. Each one has a named fix.","core":true},{"t":"Credential theft is the engine","gist":"Almost every attack path runs through LSASS, which keeps credentials in memory on any machine where an admin has logged on. LAPS, tiering, Credential Guard, firewall scoping and MFA each cut one link in that chain.","core":true},{"t":"Sysmon and detection engineering","gist":"Sysmon logs rich events like process creation, network connections and LSASS access. Write detections as questions the logs can answer, such as 'did Office spawn a shell?', send them to a SIEM, and tune out the noise.","core":true},{"t":"Hardening checklist for your lab domain","gist":"A checklist for hardening your own lab domain, pulling it all together: tiering, LAPS, gMSAs, signing, legacy protocols off, Credential Guard, audit policy, Sysmon and regular PingCastle or Purple Knight scans.","core":false},{"t":"On the exams and your career","gist":"Where this appears on AZ-802 and MD-102, and why understanding the map as a defender makes a pentest report useful to the administrators who read it.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Tier 0 is the identity control plane: DCs, the AD database and its backups, AD CS, Entra Connect and PAWs. Credentials never flow down.","part":0},{"p":"Protected Users members get no NTLM, no RC4, no delegation, no cached credentials and a 4-hour TGT.","part":0},{"p":"A shared local Administrator password means one workstation compromise unlocks them all; Windows LAPS everywhere is the fix.","part":1},{"p":"Sysmon events: 1 process creation, 3 network connection, 10 process access (including LSASS), 22 DNS query.","part":3},{"p":"Password spraying shows as 4625 or 4771 failures for many accounts from one source within minutes, with few or no 4740 lockouts.","part":3}]},"p17m1":{"big":"The cloud is real data centers, grouped into zones and regions and rented by the second through an API; the provider secures the cloud, you secure what's in it.","why":"CLF-C02 Domain 1 and AZ-900's cloud-concepts section are this module almost word for word, and the shared responsibility model frames every cloud security question you will ever answer.","parts":[{"t":"The physical layer","gist":"A data center is a warehouse of servers with backup power and cooling. An Availability Zone groups two or more such buildings, each with independent power, cooling and networking; a Region is a cluster of zones on fast private fiber.","core":true},{"t":"Service models and the shared responsibility model","gist":"IaaS rents virtual machines, PaaS rents a runtime for your code, and SaaS rents finished software. The provider secures the cloud itself; you secure what's in it, and the line moves with the service model.","core":true},{"t":"Pay-as-you-go economics","gist":"Buying a server is capital expenditure, paid whether it's busy or idle; the cloud is operating expenditure, billed per second or gigabyte for what you use. A forgotten instance bills all month, so set a billing alarm first.","core":true},{"t":"The big three compared","gist":"AWS is the largest with the broadest catalog, Azure dominates where Microsoft products already live, and Google Cloud is strongest in data analytics, Kubernetes and AI. The advice: learn AWS first, then Azure.","core":false},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"How this lesson maps onto CLF-C02 Domain 1, AZ-900's cloud-concepts section and SAA-C03, which assumes all of it.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"An Availability Zone is separate buildings with independent power, cooling and networking; a Region is a cluster of AZs linked by low-latency private fiber.","part":0},{"p":"Shared responsibility: the provider secures the cloud (buildings, hardware, hypervisor); you secure what's in it (data, identities, network rules, encryption settings).","part":1},{"p":"On IaaS such as EC2 you patch the OS; on serverless such as Lambda the kernel is Amazon's, but your code and IAM permissions stay yours.","part":1},{"p":"Owning a server is capex; cloud pay-as-you-go is opex. The first act in any new account is a billing alarm.","part":2},{"p":"An EC2 Linux instance bills per second with a 60-second minimum, and data leaving the provider's network (egress) bills per gigabyte.","part":2}]},"p17m2":{"big":"Cloud compute comes in three shapes, virtual machines, containers and serverless functions, and each has a pricing formula you can work out on a napkin before you click anything.","why":"Choosing the compute shape and purchase option from a scenario is the heart of SAA-C03's performance and cost domains, and the GB-second arithmetic is what separates an architect from a clicker.","parts":[{"t":"Virtual machines and instance types","gist":"An instance name is a sentence: m7i.large means general purpose, generation 7, Intel, 2 vCPU and 8 GiB. Pay on demand, commit for a year to save, or use cheap spot capacity that can be reclaimed with two minutes' warning.","core":true},{"t":"Autoscaling and load balancers","gist":"An Auto Scaling group keeps a minimum, desired and maximum number of servers spread across zones and replaces any that die. A load balancer in front spreads the traffic and pulls unhealthy instances out of rotation.","core":true},{"t":"Serverless functions","gist":"Serverless functions run your code only when an event arrives and bill per request and per GB-second of execution. Lambda allows up to 15 minutes per run. The catch is the cold start after idle time.","core":true},{"t":"Containers in the cloud","gist":"An orchestrator decides which machine runs each container copy and restarts failures. On AWS that's ECS or EKS, running either on EC2 instances you manage or on Fargate, where there are no servers to patch.","core":true},{"t":"Choosing, with numbers","gist":"Match the shape to the workload: a reserved VM for a steady 24/7 API, spot for interruptible batch jobs, Lambda for light or bursty traffic, and Kubernetes when a team needs its ecosystem.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Which CLF-C02, SAA-C03, AZ-900 and AZ-104 domains test compute, mostly by asking you to pick an instance family, purchase option and compute shape from a scenario.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"m7i.large reads as family m (general purpose), generation 7, processor i (Intel), size large (2 vCPU, 8 GiB).","part":0},{"p":"Spot instances are spare capacity reclaimable with a two-minute warning; use them for work that can be interrupted and resumed.","part":0},{"p":"An Application Load Balancer works at layer 7, routing HTTP by host and path; a Network Load Balancer works at layer 4.","part":1},{"p":"Lambda costs $0.20 per million requests plus $0.0000166667 per GB-second, with one million requests and 400,000 GB-seconds free every month.","part":2},{"p":"Serverless pricing loses to a flat reserved VM at sustained load; serverless wins when idle time dominates.","part":4}]},"p17m3":{"big":"Cloud storage comes in six shapes, object, block, file, relational, NoSQL and cache, and you choose by durability, consistency, access pattern and price per gigabyte-month.","why":"SAA-C03 lives on picking the storage class or database that meets durability, retrieval time and price, and the Multi-AZ-versus-read-replica distinction alone is worth several questions.","parts":[{"t":"Object storage: S3, Blob Storage, Cloud Storage","gist":"Object storage keeps whole files under keys in a bucket; you replace an object, never edit it in place. S3 is designed for eleven nines of durability, is private by default, and gets cheaper in colder classes.","core":true},{"t":"Block and file storage","gist":"Block storage is a virtual disk attached to one machine, so filesystems and databases can change bytes in place; an EBS volume lives in one AZ. File storage is a shared network filesystem many machines mount at once.","core":true},{"t":"Managed relational databases","gist":"A managed database service runs the engine for you, handling backups, patching and failover. Multi-AZ keeps a synchronous standby for failover; read replicas are asynchronous copies for scaling reads. You still own the schema, queries and users.","core":true},{"t":"NoSQL and caching","gist":"NoSQL databases like DynamoDB trade joins for fast key lookups at any scale; use SQL when you need joins and transactions. A cache keeps hot data in RAM, turning a 10 ms query into a 0.3 ms read.","core":true},{"t":"Backups, done properly","gist":"The 3-2-1 rule still applies in the cloud: three copies, two media or services, one off-site in another Region or an account an attacker can't reach. Test a restore every quarter; an untested backup is only a hope.","core":false},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Which CLF-C02, SAA-C03, AZ-900 and AZ-104 domains test storage, typically by asking for the cheapest class or the right database for a retrieval time or resilience need.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"S3 is designed for 99.999999999% (eleven nines) durability by storing objects across at least three AZs, and is strongly consistent since December 2020.","part":0},{"p":"A lifecycle policy moves objects to colder, cheaper classes by age; Deep Archive costs $0.00099/GB-month but takes twelve hours to restore.","part":0},{"p":"An EBS volume lives in one AZ; incremental snapshots to S3 are how you back it up, clone it or move it to another AZ.","part":1},{"p":"RDS Multi-AZ is a synchronous standby for failover only; read replicas are asynchronous copies for scaling reads.","part":2},{"p":"Use NoSQL when access is by key, write volume is huge or the schema varies; use SQL for joins, cross-table transactions and ad-hoc queries.","part":3}]},"p17m4":{"big":"A VPC is your own private network inside the cloud, where route tables decide what is public and security groups and network ACLs decide which packets may pass.","why":"The subnetting you learned in Domain 2 becomes the skill SAA-C03 tests hardest, and the fiber and optics behind Direct Connect are literally the work you already do.","parts":[{"t":"The VPC and its subnets","gist":"A VPC is an isolated network in one Region with a CIDR block such as 10.0.0.0/16. You carve it into subnets, each in exactly one AZ. AWS reserves five addresses per subnet, so a /24 gives 251 usable hosts.","core":true},{"t":"Routing and gateways","gist":"Each subnet's route table decides where traffic goes. A route to an internet gateway makes a subnet public; private subnets reach out through a NAT gateway, which costs money, or use free gateway endpoints for S3 and DynamoDB.","core":true},{"t":"Two firewalls, different natures","gist":"Security groups are stateful, attach to instances and only allow; they can reference other groups, so rules follow the fleet. Network ACLs are stateless, attach to subnets and can deny, with numbered rules where the lowest wins.","core":true},{"t":"Hybrid: VPN and the fiber you already pull","gist":"Site-to-Site VPN builds two encrypted IPsec tunnels to your router. Direct Connect is a physical single-mode fiber port in a colocation building, with VLAN tagging and BGP, for more bandwidth and a private path.","core":true},{"t":"DNS and CDN","gist":"Route 53 is the cloud's DNS, with routing policies like weighted, latency, failover and geolocation. CloudFront is the CDN: it caches content at edge locations near users and lets your bucket stay private behind it.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Which SAA-C03, CLF-C02, AZ-900 and AZ-104 domains test networking: public versus private subnets, NAT versus endpoints, security groups versus NACLs, VPN versus Direct Connect.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"AWS reserves five addresses in every subnet, so a /24 has 251 usable hosts.","part":0},{"p":"A subnet is public only because its route table sends 0.0.0.0/0 to an internet gateway.","part":1},{"p":"VPC peering is not transitive: A–B and B–C don't give A–C. A hub of many VPCs needs Transit Gateway.","part":1},{"p":"Security groups: stateful, instance-level, allow-only. Network ACLs: stateless, subnet-level, allow and deny, lowest rule number wins.","part":2},{"p":"Direct Connect needs single-mode fiber with optics such as 1000BASE-LX or 10GBASE-LR, 802.1Q VLAN tagging and BGP.","part":3}]},"p17m5":{"big":"In the cloud, identity is the perimeter: decide who may call which API on what, keep credentials short-lived, encrypt with KMS and log every call.","why":"Stolen keys and over-broad permissions, not zero-days, are how cloud accounts fall. Security is 30% of both CLF-C02 and SAA-C03, and the pentest rules are the lines a tester must know before touching a client's cloud.","parts":[{"t":"IAM: principals, policies, evaluation","gist":"IAM policies are JSON evaluated in a fixed order: everything is denied by default, an explicit Deny wins, and otherwise you need an Allow. Machines use roles with temporary credentials; root gets hardware MFA and no keys.","core":true},{"t":"Encryption with KMS","gist":"KMS uses envelope encryption: data is encrypted with a data key, which is itself encrypted by a KMS key that never leaves its hardware. Encrypt at creation; an unencrypted RDS instance can't simply be encrypted later.","core":true},{"t":"Logging, detection and compliance","gist":"CloudTrail records every API call: who, what, when and from where. GuardDuty reads the logs for threats like cryptomining or stolen credentials, Config checks rules, and Security Hub gathers the findings. Compliance is shared: you configure your side.","core":true},{"t":"Cloud penetration testing: the rules","gist":"You may test your own resources on AWS's listed services without approval, but denial of service, flooding and DNS attacks are always forbidden. Red-team and phishing simulations need a form, and testing without written authorization is a crime.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Which CLF-C02, SAA-C03, AZ-900 and AZ-104 domains test identity and security, from IAM and KMS to CloudTrail, GuardDuty and Azure RBAC.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"IAM evaluation: default deny, an explicit Deny wins, then an Allow is needed from an identity or resource policy, within SCPs and any permissions boundary.","part":0},{"p":"Give machines roles with temporary STS credentials, never pasted access keys. The root account gets hardware MFA and no access keys.","part":0},{"p":"A KMS customer-managed key costs $1 a month, and its key policy decides who may use it, independently of IAM.","part":1},{"p":"CloudTrail keeps 90 days of management events free; a trail writes them durably to S3, ideally in a separate logging account.","part":2},{"p":"On AWS, DoS, flooding and Route 53 DNS attacks are forbidden even on your own resources; C2 and red-team simulations need a Simulated Events form.","part":3}]},"p17m6":{"big":"Good cloud architecture starts from the failures you refuse to tolerate: duplicate the parts that would cause them, decouple components with queues, and prove the design with availability arithmetic.","why":"SAA-C03's resilient-architecture domain is 26% of the exam and is exactly this lesson; the arithmetic lets you prove a design instead of just asserting it.","parts":[{"t":"The six pillars","gist":"The Well-Architected Framework judges a design against six pillars: operational excellence, security, reliability, performance efficiency, cost optimization and sustainability. Each comes with design principles and the questions a reviewer asks.","core":true},{"t":"Availability arithmetic","gist":"High availability recovers with a short interruption; fault tolerance has none. Parts in series multiply their availabilities, so it gets worse; independent parts in parallel multiply their failure chances, so it gets better. RPO and RTO set the targets.","core":true},{"t":"Multi-AZ and multi-Region patterns","gist":"Multi-AZ is the default good design and survives any single AZ failing. Multi-Region adds protection against losing a whole Region and serves distant users faster, but keeping data consistent across Regions is the hard part.","core":true},{"t":"Decoupling with queues and events","gist":"A queue between components absorbs bursts and failures, so one slow service doesn't stall the rest. SQS holds messages, SNS fans one message out to many subscribers, EventBridge routes events, and Step Functions runs multi-step workflows.","core":true},{"t":"Cost optimization, systematically","gist":"Cut cost in order: right-size first, commit to the steady baseline with Savings Plans, run the variable part on spot, move cold data down storage classes, delete idle resources, and tag everything to see who spent what.","core":true},{"t":"Three case studies, SAA style","gist":"Three SAA-style designs: a serverless photo app that scales to zero, a legacy PHP shop rebuilt to survive an AZ outage, and a cheap, interruption-tolerant transcoding fleet on spot instances fed by a queue.","core":false},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this sits on SAA-C03, mainly the resilient and cost-optimized domains, and how the same logic appears on CLF-C02, AZ-900 and AZ-104.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The six pillars: operational excellence, security, reliability, performance efficiency, cost optimization and sustainability.","part":0},{"p":"In series, availabilities multiply: 0.9999 × 0.9995 = 99.94%. In parallel, two independent 99.9% AZs give 99.9999%.","part":1},{"p":"RPO is how much data you may lose; RTO is how long you may be down. DR strategies: backup and restore, pilot light, warm standby, multi-site active-active.","part":1},{"p":"Multi-AZ handles nearly every real failure; go multi-Region only for a stated RTO/RPO against Regional loss or for distant users.","part":2},{"p":"An SQS message stays hidden from other consumers during its visibility timeout, 30 seconds by default; repeated failures move it to a dead-letter queue.","part":3}]},"p17m7":{"big":"Professionals drive the cloud from the command line and code, build environments from templates, watch them with alarms, and set spending limits before the first server exists.","why":"That is how you avoid the two classic disasters, an unexplained bill and an environment nobody can reproduce. The migration plan with a cost table is what an interviewer will ask you to walk through.","parts":[{"t":"The CLI and SDKs","gist":"The console is for looking; the CLI is for doing, because commands are repeatable. Use aws configure sso so sessions expire, and aws sts get-caller-identity to check who you are. Every CLI call lands in CloudTrail.","core":true},{"t":"Infrastructure as code","gist":"Infrastructure as code declares your whole setup in text files that a tool builds the same way every time. CloudFormation deploys a template as a stack, previews changes and deletes everything cleanly; Terraform does the same across clouds.","core":true},{"t":"Monitoring with CloudWatch","gist":"CloudWatch collects metrics, logs and alarms. EC2 metrics come free every five minutes, but memory needs the agent. Set log retention or pay forever, and alarm on what users feel, like errors and latency.","core":true},{"t":"Billing guardrails: do this first, in every new account","gist":"Do this first in every new account: turn on billing alerts, add an EstimatedCharges alarm in us-east-1, create a budget with 50%, 80% and 100% alerts, enable anomaly detection, and tag every resource.","core":true},{"t":"Your home-lab-to-cloud migration plan","gist":"Plan a migration with the seven Rs, from retire and retain to refactor. For a home lab, the website goes to S3 and CloudFront, backups to a locked S3 bucket, the database to RDS, and the GPU box stays home.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Which CLF-C02, SAA-C03, AZ-104 and AZ-900 domains test operations, billing tools, support plans and the migration services.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"aws sts get-caller-identity tells you which identity the CLI is using; aws configure sso gives you sessions that expire.","part":0},{"p":"CloudFormation: a template deploys as a stack, a change set previews an update, drift detection finds manual changes, and deleting the stack deletes everything.","part":1},{"p":"EC2 memory metrics need the CloudWatch agent, because the hypervisor cannot see inside the guest.","part":2},{"p":"AWS billing metrics live only in us-east-1, so the EstimatedCharges alarm must be created there.","part":3},{"p":"The seven Rs of migration: retire, retain, rehost, relocate, replatform, repurchase, refactor.","part":4}]},"p18m1":{"big":"Store every fact exactly once: tables of rows linked by keys, normalized so each non-key column depends on the key, the whole key and nothing but the key.","why":"Every application you will build or test sits on a schema like this, and both DP-900 and 1Z0-071 open with keys and normalization. Get the model right and queries, indexes and backups become mechanical.","parts":[{"t":"Codd's bargain","gist":"Codd's model stores data in tables that are sets of rows over typed columns. Being a set means rows have no built-in order and no duplicates, and you ask for what you want while the engine works out how.","core":true},{"t":"Keys: the grammar of identity","gist":"A primary key is the column chosen as each row's identity; a foreign key must point at a row that really exists in another table. Professional practice is a surrogate key plus UNIQUE constraints on natural identifiers like email.","core":true},{"t":"Entity–relationship modeling","gist":"Before creating tables, draw the entities, their attributes and how they relate: one-to-one, one-to-many or many-to-many. One-to-many becomes a foreign key on the many side; many-to-many needs a junction table.","core":true},{"t":"Normalization: a worked example","gist":"Normalization removes redundancy so each fact is stored once. 1NF makes every cell a single value, 2NF removes dependence on part of a key, 3NF removes dependence on other non-key columns. That ends the update, insert and delete anomalies.","core":true},{"t":"When to denormalize","gist":"Denormalizing adds redundancy back for read speed, like a cached order total. Only do it when reads vastly outnumber writes and you can keep it fresh, and try an index, a view or a materialized view first.","core":true},{"t":"Modeling habits that save you later","gist":"Habits that prevent later pain: store money as NUMERIC, never a float, use timestamptz in UTC, give every table a primary key, index every foreign key, and enforce rules as constraints in the database.","core":false},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"How DP-900 and 1Z0-071 open with this material: relational concepts, normalization, keys and common database objects.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A foreign key's values must exist as a primary or unique key in the other table; that guarantee is referential integrity.","part":1},{"p":"Prefer a surrogate primary key plus UNIQUE constraints on natural identifiers, because natural keys like emails change.","part":1},{"p":"A many-to-many relationship becomes a junction table whose composite primary key is the pair of foreign keys.","part":2},{"p":"The normalization slogan: every non-key column depends on the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key.","part":3},{"p":"Normalization removes three anomalies: update (one fact edited in many rows), insert (can't record a fact on its own) and delete (losing a fact with a row).","part":3}]},"p18m2":{"big":"SQL describes the result you want, not how to get it, and the order in which the engine evaluates each clause decides what your query really means.","why":"Roughly 60% of 1Z0-071 is this material, and in Domain 22 you will do your data preparation in SQL rather than pandas. A six-line query can replace two hundred lines of Python.","parts":[{"t":"The shape of a query and its logical order","gist":"You write SELECT first, but the engine runs FROM and joins, then WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, DISTINCT, ORDER BY and LIMIT. That order explains why an alias fails in WHERE and aggregates belong in HAVING.","core":true},{"t":"Joins, drawn as sets","gist":"Picture joins as overlapping circles: inner keeps only the matches, left keeps every left row and fills gaps with NULL, full keeps both sides, and cross pairs everything. NOT EXISTS finds rows with no match.","core":true},{"t":"Aggregation","gist":"Aggregate functions like COUNT, SUM and AVG collapse each group to one row; GROUP BY names the groups and HAVING filters them. COUNT(*) counts rows, while COUNT(column) counts only non-NULL values.","core":true},{"t":"Subqueries and CTEs","gist":"A subquery is a query inside a query. A CTE, written WITH name AS (...), names an intermediate result so a complex query reads top-down, and WITH RECURSIVE walks hierarchies of unknown depth, like an org chart.","core":true},{"t":"Window functions","gist":"A window function computes over related rows while keeping every row, unlike GROUP BY. It gives running totals, rankings with ROW_NUMBER or RANK, and previous-row values with LAG, without a self join.","core":true},{"t":"NULL: the third truth value","gist":"NULL means unknown, not zero, and any comparison with it is unknown, so WHERE phone = NULL never matches; use IS NULL. Never use NOT IN against a column that can contain NULL; use NOT EXISTS.","core":true},{"t":"Dialect differences you will actually hit","gist":"A reference table of where PostgreSQL, Oracle, MySQL and SQL Server differ: limiting rows, joining strings, quoting names, auto-numbering, the current time, selecting without a table, and the boolean type.","core":false},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"How 1Z0-071 spends most of its topics on querying, including Oracle details like DUAL, SYSDATE and ROWNUM, while DP-900 only asks you to recognize the statement types.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Logical order: FROM/JOIN, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, DISTINCT, ORDER BY, LIMIT. Only ORDER BY can use SELECT aliases.","part":0},{"p":"A cross join, or a forgotten ON clause, returns every pairing: 1,000 films × 599 customers = 599,000 rows.","part":1},{"p":"WHERE filters rows before grouping and can't see aggregates; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.","part":2},{"p":"Top-N per group: assign ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY ...) in a CTE, then filter rn <= N outside, because window results can't be filtered in the same SELECT.","part":4},{"p":"NOT IN (subquery) returns no rows at all if the subquery contains a single NULL; use NOT EXISTS instead.","part":5}]},"p18m3":{"big":"Constraints, transactions and isolation levels let you change data so that a crash, a concurrent user or a bug never leaves the database half-right.","why":"Money, inventory and medical records depend on it. Race conditions are a real vulnerability class, a transaction log is what an incident responder reconstructs from, and interviewers ask about isolation levels.","parts":[{"t":"The five sublanguages","gist":"SQL splits into DDL for structure, DML for changing rows, DQL for SELECT, TCL for transactions and DCL for permissions. PostgreSQL can roll back DDL, but Oracle and MySQL silently commit before and after every DDL statement.","core":true},{"t":"Constraints: rules the engine enforces","gist":"Constraints like NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK and FOREIGN KEY are checked by the engine inside every write's transaction, so two concurrent requests can't both slip past. Each violation returns a SQLSTATE code your program can handle.","core":true},{"t":"Transactions and ACID","gist":"A transaction succeeds or fails as a unit. ACID means atomic, consistent, isolated and durable. PostgreSQL gets atomicity and durability from its write-ahead log, which is flushed to disk before COMMIT returns.","core":true},{"t":"Isolation levels and the anomalies","gist":"Isolation levels decide what concurrent transactions may see of each other. PostgreSQL defaults to Read Committed, a fresh snapshot per statement; Serializable aborts one of two conflicting transactions with 40001, and you simply retry.","core":true},{"t":"Locking versus MVCC","gist":"Instead of overwriting a row, MVCC writes a new version, so readers never block writers and writers never block readers. Old versions become dead tuples that VACUUM must clean up, or the table bloats.","core":true},{"t":"Deadlocks","gist":"A deadlock is two transactions each waiting for a lock the other holds. PostgreSQL detects it after 1 second and aborts one with 40P01. Prevent it by always locking rows in the same order, and retry.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"What 1Z0-071 and DP-900 test here, from DML, transaction control and constraints to ACID. Isolation levels and MVCC go beyond both exams but come up in every serious interview.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Oracle and MySQL commit implicitly before and after every DDL statement; PostgreSQL's DDL is transactional and can be rolled back.","part":0},{"p":"ACID is atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability. In PostgreSQL the write-ahead log is fsynced before COMMIT returns.","part":2},{"p":"Dirty read: seeing uncommitted changes. Non-repeatable read: a row changes between two reads. Phantom read: the set of matching rows changes.","part":3},{"p":"PostgreSQL defaults to Read Committed. Under Serializable, catch SQLSTATE 40001 (serialization failure) and run the transaction again.","part":3},{"p":"Avoid deadlocks by locking rows in a consistent order, such as the lower account_id first; treat 40P01 like 40001 and retry.","part":5}]},"p18m4":{"big":"Query speed comes down to how many pages are read from disk, and the planner picks the plan with the lowest estimated cost, which is not always the index.","why":"Reading a plan and doing the cost arithmetic is what separates someone who knows SQL from someone a team trusts with production. It explains why one query takes 3 milliseconds and a similar one 30 seconds.","parts":[{"t":"Storage: pages and heap files","gist":"PostgreSQL stores each table as a heap file of 8 kB pages in no particular order, and each row carries a 23-byte header. Reading pages from disk is what costs time, so every technique aims to read fewer of them.","core":true},{"t":"Indexes","gist":"A B-tree index finds one row among a billion in four page reads. A multicolumn index on (a, b) helps queries on a, or on a and b, but not on b alone. Every index slows down writes.","core":true},{"t":"Covering indexes and index-only scans","gist":"If an index holds every column a query needs, the engine can answer from the index alone without visiting the table. INCLUDE adds extra columns to an index for exactly that purpose.","core":false},{"t":"The planner","gist":"The planner estimates each possible plan's cost from table statistics and fixed constants, then picks the cheapest. Above roughly 5–10% of rows a sequential scan usually beats an index. ANALYZE keeps the statistics accurate.","core":true},{"t":"Reading EXPLAIN","gist":"EXPLAIN shows the chosen plan; EXPLAIN ANALYZE also runs the query and reports real times and rows. The most useful clue is estimated versus actual rows; a big mismatch is the first thing to fix.","core":true},{"t":"Common mistakes and the fix","gist":"Ten classic slowdowns and their fixes, such as a function hiding a column from its index, LIKE with a leading wildcard, OFFSET pagination, ORM N+1 queries, unindexed foreign keys and stale statistics after a bulk load.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"What 1Z0-071 and DP-900 ask about indexes, which stops well short of this lesson; an interviewer who asks you to read a plan does not.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"PostgreSQL pages are 8 kB and every row carries a 23-byte header, so a 40-byte row really costs about 70 bytes.","part":0},{"p":"Leftmost-prefix rule: an index on (a, b) serves a alone or a and b, but not b alone. Put the equality column first.","part":1},{"p":"Above roughly 5–10% selectivity a sequential scan usually beats an index, so skipping the index can be the correct plan.","part":3},{"p":"Plain EXPLAIN never runs the query; EXPLAIN ANALYZE does, so wrap anything that writes in BEGIN … ROLLBACK.","part":4},{"p":"Compare estimated with actual rows in a plan; a 100× mismatch can mean stale statistics, fixed by running ANALYZE.","part":4}]},"p18m5":{"big":"Running a database safely means controlling who may connect and what they may do, keeping backups you have tested, and never letting outside text become SQL.","why":"Every database finding in a penetration test report is a failure of one of these: a listener on the internet, a role with superuser, a backup never restored, or a query built by string concatenation.","parts":[{"t":"Installing PostgreSQL","gist":"PostgreSQL 18 runs in Docker or as a native install and listens on TCP 5432. postgresql.conf holds the settings and pg_hba.conf decides who may connect. Binding Docker to 127.0.0.1 keeps it off your network.","core":false},{"t":"Roles and privileges","gist":"PostgreSQL has one idea, the role: a user if it can log in, a group if other roles belong to it. Give the application only the rights it needs, and never let it connect as the superuser postgres.","core":true},{"t":"Authentication: pg_hba.conf","gist":"pg_hba.conf is read top to bottom and the first matching line wins, so order is everything. Use scram-sha-256 passwords, and never put a database port on the internet; reach it through SSH, WireGuard or a private network.","core":true},{"t":"Backups","gist":"Logical backups (pg_dump) are portable snapshots of one moment. Physical backups (pg_basebackup) plus archived WAL, the stream of changes, let you recover to the second before a mistake. Restore one every month: an untested backup is a hope.","core":true},{"t":"Replication and failover","gist":"A standby server replays the primary's WAL to stay a live copy. By default this is asynchronous, so a crash can lose the last few transactions. Failover means promoting a standby; tools like Patroni automate it.","core":true},{"t":"Monitoring","gist":"Most monitoring is reading built-in views: pg_stat_activity shows every connection, and pg_stat_statements ranks queries by total time, the first place to look when the database is slow. Turn on slow-query logging and alert on lag and disk space.","core":false},{"t":"SQL injection: mechanism and the fix","gist":"SQL injection happens when outside text is glued into a query and the database reads part of it as code. A parameterized query sends the SQL and the values separately, so a value can never change the statement.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"How these skills map to Oracle 1Z0-071's user-access topics and to DP-900's database administrator duties, including what managed cloud services do for you and what you still own.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"pg_hba.conf is read top to bottom and the first matching line wins; scram-sha-256 is the right password method, and trust accepts anyone.","part":2},{"p":"The application must never connect as the superuser postgres; give it its own role with only the privileges it needs.","part":1},{"p":"Point-in-time recovery is a base backup from pg_basebackup plus archived WAL, replayed up to the second before the bad DELETE.","part":3},{"p":"A standby is not a backup: it replays every mistake, including DROP TABLE, within seconds. Replication is for availability; backups are for recovery.","part":4},{"p":"Parameterized queries stop SQL injection structurally because values travel separately from the SQL; escaping quotes is only a filter and has been bypassed.","part":6}]},"p18m6":{"big":"Each non-relational store trades joins or consistency for scale or a better-fitting shape, so you choose by access pattern and know what you give up.","why":"The applications you will assess carry Redis, Elasticsearch and MongoDB, and the largest accidental data exposures of the last decade were unauthenticated instances of exactly these systems left on the internet.","parts":[{"t":"Why \"NoSQL\" happened","gist":"Around 2006–2009, web companies needed more writes, more flexible records and faster replies than one relational server could give. NoSQL means 'not only SQL', and relational engines have since absorbed many of its best ideas.","core":false},{"t":"The five shapes","gist":"Five shapes suit five jobs: documents (MongoDB) for nested records, key-value (Redis) for caching and sessions, wide-column (Cassandra) for huge write volumes, graph (Neo4j) for connections, and search (Elasticsearch) for full-text search and logs.","core":true},{"t":"CAP and consistency models","gist":"When the network splits, a distributed store must choose: refuse some requests to stay correct, or answer with possibly stale data. With N copies, reads are guaranteed to see the latest write when W + R > N.","core":true},{"t":"Picking the right store","gist":"Start with PostgreSQL and add another store only when a real access pattern needs it. Every extra store brings its own backups, monitoring and attack surface, plus the job of keeping two copies of a fact consistent.","core":true},{"t":"SQLite: the database in your Python (Domain 4)","gist":"SQLite is a library, not a server: the whole database is one file, with no network listener and one writer at a time. It suits local apps, tools and tests, but not two machines writing at once.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"How the shapes map onto DP-900's non-relational section and the APIs of Azure Cosmos DB, plus the one Oracle fact to know: it stores and indexes JSON natively.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Default ports: MongoDB 27017, Redis 6379, Cassandra 9042, Neo4j Bolt 7687, Elasticsearch 9200.","part":1},{"p":"Several of these systems historically shipped with no authentication. Harden every one: bind to localhost or a private network, enable authentication and TLS, firewall the port.","part":1},{"p":"CAP: partitions are not optional, so during a split the choice is consistency or availability. PACELC adds latency versus consistency when there is no partition.","part":2},{"p":"Reads see the latest write when W + R > N; N = 3, W = 2, R = 2 is Cassandra's QUORUM and survives one node down.","part":2},{"p":"SQLite allows one writer at a time; declare tables STRICT, or a column typed INTEGER will store 'hello'.","part":4}]},"p18m7":{"big":"Running a business and analysing it want opposite things from storage, so analytics copies the facts into star schemas in columnar storage built for scanning.","why":"Running a three-year aggregation on the production database slows every checkout; a separate warehouse lets analysts scan freely. This is also 25–30% of DP-900 and where the clean tables for data science come from.","parts":[{"t":"OLTP versus OLAP","gist":"Running the store (OLTP) touches a few rows by key in milliseconds. Analysing it (OLAP) scans millions of rows across a few columns. The two want opposite storage, so analytics gets its own warehouse.","core":true},{"t":"The star schema","gist":"A star schema has one fact table of events at a declared grain, such as one row per order line, joined to wide dimension tables describing the who, what, where and when. Only the fact table is big.","core":true},{"t":"ETL, ELT and the pipeline","gist":"ETL transforms data before loading it; ELT loads the raw data first and transforms it inside the warehouse with SQL. Data moves from ingestion to a lake, a lakehouse and a warehouse, then out to Power BI.","core":true},{"t":"Columnar storage: the arithmetic","gist":"A column store keeps each column's values together, so a query reads only the columns it needs. Reading 3 of 50 columns is 16× less I/O, and similar values compress 5–10× more.","core":true},{"t":"DuckDB: the warehouse in your laptop","gist":"DuckDB is to analytics what SQLite is to transactions: it runs inside your program with no setup, but stores data by column. It queries CSV, JSON and Parquet files where they sit and returns DataFrames.","core":false},{"t":"How databases feed data science (Domain 22)","gist":"Shape and aggregate data in SQL at the source, then hand a clean table to the model. Version the SQL, and compute features only from data that existed before the prediction time, or the model will cheat.","core":false},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"What DP-900's analytics section asks, from pipelines and data stores to Power BI and which chart fits which question, and how 1Z0-071's GROUP BY material is the foundation.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"OLTP touches 1–100 rows by key with normalized row storage; OLAP scans millions of rows with denormalized star schemas and columnar storage.","part":0},{"p":"A Type 2 slowly changing dimension inserts a new row with valid_from and valid_to dates so old facts keep their old values; Type 1 just overwrites.","part":1},{"p":"ETL transforms before loading; ELT loads raw data first and transforms it in the warehouse with SQL, and dbt is the standard tool.","part":2},{"p":"A batch job processes a bounded set on a schedule; a streaming job processes each event within seconds of arrival.","part":2},{"p":"Columnar arithmetic: 3 of 50 columns is 16× less I/O, and 5–10× compression turns a 400 GB scan into about 3 GB.","part":3}]},"p19m1":{"big":"DevOps treats the path from a commit to a running, observed system as an engineered pipeline: automated, measured, versioned and owned by the people who write the code.","why":"It is how one team ships fifty times a day with fewer outages than a team deploying once a quarter. Pipelines also hold production's keys, so you must understand them to assess them.","parts":[{"t":"The delivery pipeline","gist":"Every change passes the same stages: source, build, test, package, deploy, operate. Continuous Integration builds and tests every merge. Continuous Delivery keeps main ready to ship; Continuous Deployment ships every green build. The difference is who presses the button.","core":true},{"t":"Measuring it: DORA","gist":"DORA's decade of research found that speed and stability are not a trade-off: the best teams have both. Five numbers measure it, and you can compute them from your own Git log and incident tickets.","core":true},{"t":"Environments, releases and flags","gist":"Code moves from your machine through staging to production, which should differ only in size and data. Rolling updates, blue-green and canaries limit risk, and a feature flag lets code ship switched off and be turned on later.","core":true},{"t":"GitOps and the twelve-factor app","gist":"In GitOps the desired state lives in Git, and an agent inside the cluster pulls it and keeps reality matching. The twelve-factor app is the checklist that makes software fit this world, such as config in environment variables.","core":true},{"t":"Why this matters to a penetration tester","gist":"Pipelines hold cloud credentials, signing keys and deploy access, so CI/CD is a high-value target and a frequent finding. You test only pipelines you are hired and authorized in writing to test.","core":false},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"What KCNA and the GitHub Actions exam (GH-200) expect here: the five DORA metrics by name, the four GitOps principles and the twelve factors.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Continuous Delivery keeps main always deployable and a human decides when to release; Continuous Deployment ships every green build automatically.","part":0},{"p":"The five DORA metrics: deployment frequency, change lead time, change fail rate, failed deployment recovery time and deployment rework rate.","part":1},{"p":"Elite teams deploy on demand, with lead time under one day, a change fail rate around 5% and recovery under one hour.","part":1},{"p":"A canary sends 1–5% of traffic to the new version first; a feature flag separates deploying code from releasing the feature.","part":2},{"p":"GitOps: desired state lives in Git and an in-cluster agent such as Argo CD or Flux pulls and reconciles it, so git revert is a rollback.","part":3}]},"p19m2":{"big":"A container is not a small virtual machine but an ordinary Linux process with a restricted view and a resource budget, run from a stack of image layers.","why":"Nothing is virtualized: the kernel starts an ordinary process and limits it. Understanding that is what separates people who use Docker from people who can debug, secure and shrink it.","parts":[{"t":"Images are layers","gist":"An image is a stack of read-only layers, each named by its SHA-256 digest, plus a small config. Docker caches layers in order, so install dependencies before copying your code. Files deleted later are still inside earlier layers.","core":true},{"t":"A container is a process","gist":"A container is an ordinary process the kernel restricts: namespaces give it its own view, and cgroups cap its memory and CPU. It shares the host's kernel, so --privileged or a kernel bug means a host compromise.","core":true},{"t":"Networking and storage","gist":"By default containers sit on a private bridge network behind NAT, and -p maps a host port in. Named volumes keep data after the container is removed, and Compose describes a whole multi-container app in one file.","core":true},{"t":"Registries, scanning and minimal images","gist":"Tags can move but digests cannot, so production pins digests. Scan what you ship, and keep it small: build in a full image, copy only the result into a slim or distroless base, and run as non-root.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"How this maps to KCNA's Kubernetes Fundamentals and CKA's runtime chain, and the proof employers want: a scanned, multi-stage, non-root image well under 200 MB.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Order a Dockerfile for the cache: copy requirements.txt and run pip install before copying the source, so a code edit rebuilds in seconds.","part":0},{"p":"A secret deleted in a later layer is still in the earlier one; docker history or dive will show it.","part":0},{"p":"Exceed the cgroup memory limit and the kernel's OOM killer ends the container with exit code 137.","part":1},{"p":"A VM has its own kernel; a container shares the host's kernel, so never use --privileged on anything you do not fully trust.","part":1},{"p":"Tags such as :latest are mutable pointers; digests (app@sha256:…) are immutable, which is why production manifests pin digests.","part":3}]},"p19m3":{"big":"GitHub Actions runs your tests, builds and deploys on fresh machines at every push, and the professional skill is giving it short-lived, least-privilege keys.","why":"It is the most common CI/CD system you will meet at work and in authorized assessments, and its security rules are exactly what attackers exploit when teams skip them.","parts":[{"t":"Anatomy of a workflow","gist":"A workflow is a YAML file in .github/workflows that says when to run (triggers), what to run (jobs and steps) and where (runners). Jobs run in parallel on fresh machines; only steps inside one job share files.","core":true},{"t":"Matrix, cache, artifacts, secrets","gist":"A matrix turns one job into many, such as one per Python version. Caching avoids re-downloading dependencies, artifacts carry files between jobs, and secrets are masked in logs and withheld from fork pull requests.","core":true},{"t":"OIDC: keys that expire in minutes","gist":"Instead of storing a cloud key that lasts forever, the job asks GitHub for a signed token saying exactly which repository and branch is running. The cloud trades it for credentials valid about an hour.","core":true},{"t":"Environments, approvals and deploying","gist":"An environment such as production carries its own secrets and rules, like required reviewers who must approve before the job starts. The deploy itself is ordinary shell, for example SSH to your server and docker compose up -d.","core":false},{"t":"Pipeline security","gist":"The pipeline holds production's keys, so guard it: least privilege, pin actions to a full commit SHA, never put untrusted input into run:, and be wary of pull_request_target, which can expose secrets to a fork's code.","core":true},{"t":"Cost and the exam","gist":"What runners cost once the free minutes run out, and how the GH-200 exam is weighted; the enterprise domain is the part self-taught engineers under-study.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Jobs run on separate fresh runners and do not share files; only steps in one job do, so pass files between jobs with artifacts.","part":0},{"p":"Scope GITHUB_TOKEN with a top-level permissions: block, and grant packages: write only to the job that pushes an image.","part":1},{"p":"OIDC (permissions: id-token: write) swaps a short-lived signed token for cloud credentials valid about an hour, so no long-lived keys are stored.","part":2},{"p":"Pin third-party actions to a full commit SHA, not a tag: in March 2025 the tj-actions/changed-files tags were re-pointed at a malicious commit.","part":4},{"p":"Never interpolate untrusted input such as a pull request title into run:; pass it through an env: variable and quote it.","part":4}]},"p19m4":{"big":"Infrastructure as code describes every machine, network and permission in versioned files that a tool applies, so you can rebuild, diff and review your environment like code.","why":"Your environment stops depending on who clicked what in a console last year: it can be rebuilt from nothing, compared against reality and reviewed in a pull request. The code is the as-built.","parts":[{"t":"Terraform in one mental model","gist":"Terraform reads files describing what you want, compares them with a state file that remembers what it built, and calls cloud APIs through providers to make reality match. The core workflow is init, plan, apply and destroy.","core":true},{"t":"State: the crown jewels","gist":"The state file maps your code to real objects and can contain passwords, so it never goes in Git. Keep it in a remote backend with locking. Drift is reality changing behind Terraform's back, and plan reveals it.","core":true},{"t":"Variables, modules, workspaces","gist":"Variables, outputs and modules let one design be reused across many environments. Workspaces keep separate state for one configuration, and lifecycle rules add guards, such as stopping a database from being destroyed.","core":true},{"t":"Ansible, idempotency and testing","gist":"Terraform creates infrastructure; Ansible configures machines that already exist, over SSH with no agent. Both are safe to rerun because they are idempotent: applying the same description twice changes nothing the second time.","core":true},{"t":"Scope and money","gist":"Run IaC only against accounts you own. Set a billing alarm first, then plan, apply, verify and destroy the lab the same day, because forgotten resources keep costing money.","core":false},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"The Terraform Associate (004) objectives, from IaC concepts and the core workflow to state management and HCP Terraform, plus Red Hat's EX294 if configuration management becomes your specialty.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"In a plan, + means create, ~ update in place, -/+ destroy and recreate, and - destroy; apply a saved plan to run exactly that.","part":0},{"p":"State is plain JSON that can hold passwords and keys, so never commit it; use a remote backend with locking, such as S3 with use_lockfile = true.","part":1},{"p":"terraform plan -refresh-only shows drift; then either apply to enforce the code or change the code to accept the change.","part":1},{"p":"Idempotent means a second run changes nothing: apt: name=nginx state=present is idempotent, while shell: apt-get install nginx reports changed every time.","part":3},{"p":"Terraform provisions resources through cloud provider APIs and keeps a state file; Ansible configures existing hosts over SSH with YAML playbooks and no state file.","part":3}]},"p19m5":{"big":"In Kubernetes you declare the desired state, and controllers keep comparing it with reality and fixing any difference, so you never place containers by hand.","why":"Kubernetes is the most widely deployed distributed system on earth, and this architecture is KCNA's two largest domains. Knowing the pieces lets you trace any deployment from kubectl apply to running containers.","parts":[{"t":"The control plane","gist":"The control plane is four processes. The API server is the front door and the only one that talks to etcd, the cluster's store. The scheduler places new Pods, and controllers keep closing gaps between desired and actual state.","core":true},{"t":"The node","gist":"Each worker runs the kubelet, which starts Pods through containerd and runc, and kube-proxy, which routes Service traffic to Pods. A CNI plugin gives every Pod its own IP, and any Pod reaches any other without NAT.","core":true},{"t":"The objects","gist":"A Pod is one or more containers sharing one IP. Deployments keep the right number of Pods running and roll out updates, and a Service gives a set of Pods one stable address and DNS name.","core":true},{"t":"kubectl fluency","gist":"kubectl talks to the API server. Learn get, describe, logs and apply, and generate YAML with --dry-run=client -o yaml instead of typing it. Production keeps manifests in version control and applies them declaratively.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"This module is KCNA's Kubernetes Fundamentals (44%) and Container Orchestration (28%), and CKA's workloads and networking sections. Practise until deploying, exposing and reading events takes under three minutes.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"kube-apiserver is the only component that talks to etcd, which runs as three or five members so a majority survives failures.","part":0},{"p":"Every Pod gets its own IP on a flat network with no NAT, and CoreDNS answers Service names like web.shop.svc.cluster.local.","part":1},{"p":"A Deployment owns ReplicaSets and rolls updates with at most 25% extra and 25% unavailable at once; kubectl rollout undo reverses one.","part":2},{"p":"Service types: ClusterIP is internal only, NodePort opens a port 30000–32767 on every node, LoadBalancer puts a cloud load balancer in front.","part":2},{"p":"A Secret is base64-encoded, not encrypted; enable encryption at rest and restrict it with RBAC.","part":2}]},"p19m6":{"big":"Operating Kubernetes means steering where Pods run, budgeting their resources, securing access and traffic, upgrading safely, and diagnosing failures from events and logs.","why":"The CKA is mostly the cluster not working: Pods stuck Pending, a node NotReady, a Service with no endpoints, an upgrade half done. These are the skills that fix them.","parts":[{"t":"Scheduling: where Pods land","gist":"The scheduler decides where Pods run, and you steer it with labels and affinity rules. A taint on a node repels any Pod without a matching toleration. Cordon and drain clear a node for maintenance.","core":true},{"t":"Resources: requests, limits and the arithmetic","gist":"A request is what the scheduler reserves; a limit is the ceiling enforced at run time. Over the CPU limit a Pod is throttled; over the memory limit it is killed. Whether Pods fit on a node is simple arithmetic.","core":true},{"t":"Storage","gist":"A Pod's own files vanish with it. For data that must survive, a PersistentVolumeClaim asks for a size and access mode, a StorageClass creates a matching volume, and the Pod mounts the claim.","core":false},{"t":"RBAC and NetworkPolicy","gist":"RBAC decides who may do what: a Role lists allowed actions on resources, and a binding grants it to a user, group or ServiceAccount. A NetworkPolicy is a firewall for Pods, but only if the network plugin enforces it.","core":true},{"t":"Helm and cluster lifecycle","gist":"Helm and Kustomize package and install applications. Upgrade a kubeadm cluster one minor version at a time, control plane first, and back up etcd before you start. kubeadm certificates expire after one year.","core":true},{"t":"The troubleshooting playbook","gist":"When something breaks, read the events, then the logs, then check the layer below. Each common symptom, like a Pending Pod or CrashLoopBackOff, has a usual place to look and a short list of causes.","core":true},{"t":"Exam tactics","gist":"How to win on time in the CKA: aliases, generating YAML, kubectl explain, skipping and returning, and daily practice on killer.sh and free lab scenarios.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Only requests count for scheduling. At run time, CPU over the limit is throttled and memory over the limit is OOMKilled with exit code 137.","part":1},{"p":"QoS classes: requests equal to limits is Guaranteed, some requests is Burstable, none is BestEffort, and the class sets eviction order under pressure.","part":1},{"p":"A NetworkPolicy does nothing unless the CNI implements it, such as Calico or Cilium; kind's default kindnet does not.","part":3},{"p":"kubeadm upgrades go one minor version at a time, control plane first, after an etcdctl snapshot save; the kubelet must never be newer than the API server.","part":4},{"p":"For a Pending Pod, run kubectl describe pod and read Events: usual causes are insufficient CPU or memory, an untolerated taint or an unbound PVC.","part":5}]},"p19m7":{"big":"Observability lets you tell from a system's own signals whether it is working for users, and SRE makes reliability a measured target with a budget for failure.","why":"A green deployment does not tell you the system works for users at 3 a.m. These signals and rules tell you when it does not, who acts, and how to learn from it without blame.","parts":[{"t":"Logs, metrics, traces","gist":"Logs are timestamped events, metrics are cheap numeric time series used for alerting, and traces follow one request across services. Prometheus pulls metrics by scraping each target's /metrics endpoint every 15–60 seconds.","core":true},{"t":"SLIs, SLOs and the error budget","gist":"An SLI is the share of good events, an SLO is the target for it, and the error budget is what that target allows to fail. Alert on how fast the budget is burning, not on causes like high CPU.","core":true},{"t":"On-call, incidents and postmortems","gist":"During an incident the roles are explicit: an incident commander decides, an operations lead touches the system, a communications lead updates users. Mitigate first, diagnose second, then write a blameless postmortem.","core":true},{"t":"Cost and chaos","gist":"The same data finds waste: CPU you request but never use still costs money. Chaos engineering breaks things on purpose to test reliability claims, but only on systems you own, in an announced game day.","core":false},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this shows up in KCNA's architecture domain and CKA's troubleshooting domain, plus the optional Prometheus Certified Associate and the free Google SRE book and Workbook.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Logs are events, metrics are numeric time series, and traces follow one request across services; OpenTelemetry standardizes all three.","part":0},{"p":"Prometheus pulls by scraping /metrics. A label carrying user IDs creates millions of series and kills the server.","part":0},{"p":"Error budget is 1 minus the SLO: 99.9% over 30 days allows 43.2 minutes of full outage, 99.99% only 4.32.","part":1},{"p":"Page when the 1-hour burn rate exceeds 14.4× (2% of the monthly budget in an hour); open a ticket at 1× sustained over three days.","part":1},{"p":"A blameless postmortem never names 'human error' as a root cause; it lists contributing factors and action items with owners and dates.","part":2}]},"p20m1":{"big":"Git is a content-addressed database of snapshots, not a list of diffs; once you see that, every command becomes predictable and almost nothing is truly lost.","why":"It lets you change code fearlessly, collaborate with strangers and recover from a deleted branch, a bad reset or a force-push. Your GitHub profile is also the résumé a hiring manager actually reads.","parts":[{"t":"The object model: four kinds of objects, one graph","gist":"Git stores four kinds of objects, each named by a hash of its contents: blobs (file contents), trees (directories), commits and tags. A branch is just a tiny file holding one commit's hash, so branching is free.","core":true},{"t":"Three areas: working tree, staging area, repository","gist":"You edit in the working tree, git add copies a snapshot into the staging area, and git commit turns that into a commit. So one file can have a staged and an unstaged version at once.","core":true},{"t":"Merge vs rebase","gist":"A merge joins two histories with a two-parent commit; a rebase replays your commits onto a new base as new commits. Never rebase commits others may already have, and push rebased work with --force-with-lease.","core":true},{"t":"Resolving a merge conflict","gist":"A conflict just means both sides changed the same lines. Git marks them and stops; you edit, remove the markers, add and continue. The zdiff3 setting shows the common ancestor, which usually makes the answer obvious.","core":false},{"t":"Bisect and reflog: finding bugs and recovering anything","gist":"git bisect binary-searches history for the commit that broke something, so 1,000 commits take about 10 steps. The reflog remembers where HEAD and each branch pointed for 90 days, so lost work is almost always recoverable.","core":true},{"t":".gitignore and signed commits","gist":".gitignore only affects files Git is not tracking yet, so an already committed file must be removed with git rm --cached. Signing commits with your SSH key earns GitHub's Verified badge.","core":false},{"t":"The GitHub workflow","gist":"On GitHub, work flows from issue to branch to pull request to review to automated checks to merge. Branch protection rules or rulesets enforce the rules, and this loop is what the GH-900 exam tests.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Git's four object types are blob, tree, commit and tag, each named by the hash of its contents; a branch is a 41-byte ref pointing at a commit.","part":0},{"p":"Never rebase commits others may already have; push a rewritten branch with --force-with-lease, which refuses if someone else pushed meanwhile.","part":2},{"p":"git bisect finds a regression in about log2 of N steps: 1,000 commits take about 10.","part":4},{"p":"After a bad git reset --hard, run git reflog, find the entry before the mistake and reset or branch back to it.","part":4},{"p":".gitignore affects only untracked files; a file already committed must be removed from the index with git rm --cached.","part":5}]},"p20m2":{"big":"Exhaustive testing is impossible, so testing means choosing the few samples most likely to find defects and automating them to run after every change.","why":"The scanners, parsers and automation you build for authorized engagements must be trustworthy, and the ISTQB Foundation Level is the industry's shared vocabulary for testing them.","parts":[{"t":"Vocabulary and the seven principles","gist":"An error is a human mistake that creates a defect in the code, which may cause a failure you can observe. The seven principles start here: testing can show that defects are present, never that there are none.","core":true},{"t":"Levels, types and the test pyramid","gist":"Tests are grouped by scope, from unit up to acceptance, and by purpose. The test pyramid says most tests should be fast unit tests, fewer integration tests and very few slow end-to-end tests, because cost rises as you climb.","core":true},{"t":"TDD and pytest","gist":"Test-driven development is red, green, refactor: write a failing test, write the least code that passes, then clean up. pytest finds your tests and gives you fixtures for setup and parametrization for tables of cases.","core":true},{"t":"Mocks, coverage and their limits","gist":"A mock stands in for something slow or external, like the network; patch it where it is looked up. Coverage shows what never ran, not that the code is right: a test with no assertion still covers lines.","core":true},{"t":"Black-box design techniques (CTFL chapter 4)","gist":"You cannot test every input, so pick smart ones: one test per group of inputs the program should treat alike, plus the edges where off-by-one errors live. Decision tables cover combinations of conditions.","core":true},{"t":"The defect lifecycle and the report","gist":"A defect moves from new through fixed and retest to closed. A report that gets fixed gives steps to reproduce, expected versus actual result, evidence, severity and priority. The part ends with how the CTFL exam is laid out.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Error (a human mistake) creates a defect (a flaw in code), which may cause a failure (observed wrong behavior).","part":0},{"p":"The test pyramid: mostly fast unit tests, fewer integration tests, very few end-to-end tests, because cost rises as you climb.","part":1},{"p":"Patch a mock where the name is looked up, not where it is defined, and mock only at external boundaries.","part":3},{"p":"100% coverage only proves lines ran; mutation testing checks that a test actually fails when the code changes.","part":3},{"p":"For a field accepting 18–65, 3-value boundary analysis tests 17, 18, 19 and 64, 65, 66.","part":4}]},"p20m3":{"big":"The enemy of good code is complexity, anything that makes software hard to understand or change, and every naming, design and refactoring habit here fights it.","why":"Tooling you write for authorized engagements will be read by clients and teammates. Clean design lets the next person, often you, change one part without breaking the rest.","parts":[{"t":"Naming and functions","gist":"Names should carry intent, like loss_db rather than x. Functions should do one thing and take few arguments, and comments should explain why, never what the code already says.","core":true},{"t":"Cohesion, coupling and deep modules","gist":"Cohesion is how well a module's parts belong together; coupling is how much one module must know about another. Aim for high cohesion and low coupling, and prefer deep modules: a small interface hiding a lot of work.","core":true},{"t":"SOLID","gist":"SOLID is five rules for code that is easy to change. Dependency inversion makes testing cheap: you pass in the clock, the network and the storage, so tests can use fakes instead of the real ones.","core":true},{"t":"Six patterns, and when not to","gist":"A design pattern is a named fix for a recurring design problem, such as factory, strategy or decorator. Use one when you feel the pain it cures; a singleton is usually the wrong cure, because it is hidden global state.","core":true},{"t":"Refactoring with tests as the net","gist":"Refactoring changes structure without changing behavior, in tiny steps protected by tests. If there are no tests yet, first write ones that pin current behavior, then fix one smell at a time, test and commit.","core":true},{"t":"Code review etiquette, and reviews as testing","gist":"Keep changes small, review within one business day, and review the code, not the person. ISTQB treats reviews as static testing, with four types from informal review to formal inspection.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Aim for high cohesion and low coupling; a deep module hides a lot of functionality behind a small interface.","part":1},{"p":"Dependency inversion: depend on abstractions and inject them, so a Service gets Postgres in production and an in-memory fake in tests.","part":2},{"p":"A singleton is global mutable state that makes tests order-dependent; a module-level instance or dependency injection almost always serves better.","part":3},{"p":"Before refactoring untested code, write characterization tests that pin current behavior, bugs included; then apply one named refactoring per commit.","part":4},{"p":"ISTQB review types, by rising formality: informal review, walkthrough, technical review, inspection.","part":5}]},"p20m4":{"big":"Programs that never met can work together because APIs are written-down contracts: HTTP semantics, resource design, a machine-readable description, authentication and rules for retrying.","why":"These contracts keep systems working when one side changes, fails or is attacked, and every authorized web assessment begins by reading them. The JWT pitfalls here fill real bug-bounty reports.","parts":[{"t":"HTTP semantics (RFC 9110)","gist":"HTTP methods and status codes have precise meanings. GET is safe and idempotent: repeating it has the same effect as doing it once. PUT and DELETE are idempotent; POST is not, so a retry may charge a card twice.","core":true},{"t":"REST and resource design","gist":"REST names resources with plural nouns in URLs and acts on them with HTTP methods. Return errors in one standard shape, page through large results with cursors rather than offsets, and version with a /v1/ prefix.","core":true},{"t":"OpenAPI: the contract as a file","gist":"OpenAPI describes a whole HTTP API in one YAML or JSON file. From it come interactive docs, request validation, mock servers and generated clients, and FastAPI can produce it from your type hints.","core":true},{"t":"Authentication: OAuth 2.0 and JWT, explained","gist":"In OAuth 2.0 you log in at an authorization server, and the app swaps a short-lived code for an access token. A JWT is signed, not encrypted: anyone holding it can read the payload.","core":true},{"t":"Rate limiting, retries and idempotency keys","gist":"A token bucket lets bursts through but caps the steady rate, answering 429 beyond it. Good clients retry with growing, randomized waits, and an Idempotency-Key stops a retried POST from running twice.","core":false},{"t":"gRPC and GraphQL compared","gist":"Use REST for public APIs, gRPC for fast, typed calls between internal services, and GraphQL when many kinds of clients need different shapes of the same data.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"GET is safe and idempotent, PUT and DELETE are idempotent, POST is not; a retried POST needs an idempotency key.","part":0},{"p":"Offset pagination makes the database walk every skipped row; cursor (keyset) pagination seeks an index and stays stable under inserts.","part":1},{"p":"Authorization Code with PKCE is the app flow, Client Credentials is machine-to-machine, and the Device flow is what gh auth login uses.","part":3},{"p":"A JWT is header.payload.signature, base64url-encoded and signed but never encrypted; reject alg: none and always check exp and aud.","part":3},{"p":"REST for public APIs, gRPC between internal services, GraphQL when many client types need different shapes of one graph.","part":5}]},"p20m5":{"big":"System design is applied arithmetic plus a small kit of building blocks with known weaknesses, so you can defend a design with numbers and see where it breaks first.","why":"It is the format of senior-engineer interviews, and read from the other side it is the map a penetration tester uses to find an authorized target's trust boundaries and single points of failure.","parts":[{"t":"Requirements and estimation","gist":"First separate what the system does from how well it must do it, then estimate with round numbers. A day is about 10^5 seconds, so a million requests a day is about 12 per second.","core":true},{"t":"The building blocks","gist":"Four standard parts, each with a known weakness: load balancers spread requests over stateless servers, caches speed up reads but can go stale, replicated databases can lag, and queues absorb bursts but may deliver a message twice.","core":true},{"t":"Consistency, CAP and idempotency","gist":"When a network split divides your nodes, you choose: refuse some requests to stay consistent, or answer with possibly stale data. Idempotency, where doing something twice equals doing it once, is what makes retries safe.","core":true},{"t":"Worked design 1: URL shortener (interview format)","gist":"The interview format, worked through: clarify, estimate, API, data model, high-level design, deep dive, bottlenecks. For a URL shortener, reads outnumber writes 10 to 1, so a cache with about 90% hits serves most redirects.","core":true},{"t":"Worked design 2: a chat service","gist":"Chat clients hold an open WebSocket to a chat server; messages are stored per channel in time order, then pushed to the recipient's server. Ordering is per channel, not global, and clients drop duplicates by message ID.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A day is about 10^5 seconds; one million requests a day is about 12 per second, and peaks run 3–10× the average.","part":0},{"p":"Availability per year: 99.9% is 8.8 hours of downtime, 99.99% is 53 minutes; services in series multiply, so two at 99.9% give 99.8%.","part":0},{"p":"With 90% cache hits at 1 ms and misses at 20 ms, average latency is 2.9 ms and database load drops 10×.","part":1},{"p":"Queues deliver at-least-once in practice, so consumers must be idempotent; a dead-letter queue catches poison messages.","part":1},{"p":"CAP: during a partition you choose consistency or availability; PACELC adds latency versus consistency the rest of the time.","part":2}]},"p20m6":{"big":"Teams ship predictably through empiricism, meaning short cycles, visible work and frequent inspection, plus shared conventions for versions, changelogs and decisions that let strangers collaborate.","why":"Requirements change weekly and nobody estimates accurately, yet this is how work still ships. Security consultancies run engagements the same way, and every report you write is technical writing under deadline.","parts":[{"t":"Agile and Scrum","gist":"Scrum works in short Sprints of one month or less. It has three accountabilities (Product Owner, Scrum Master, Developers), five timeboxed events, and three artifacts that each carry a commitment, such as the Sprint Goal.","core":true},{"t":"Kanban and flow","gist":"Kanban swaps fixed Sprints for steady flow: show the work on a board and limit how much is in progress. Little's Law ties it together: lead time equals work in progress divided by throughput.","core":true},{"t":"Estimation and its limits","gist":"Story points measure relative size, not hours. Velocity turns them into a forecast range, and early estimates can be off 4× either way, so you re-plan every Sprint. Estimates are for planning, never for blame.","core":true},{"t":"Technical writing, documentation and ADRs","gist":"Good docs do one of four jobs: teach, guide a task, give reference or explain. An architecture decision record is a short numbered file in the repository that records a decision, its context and its consequences.","core":true},{"t":"Semantic versioning and changelogs","gist":"Semantic versioning makes MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH a promise: PATCH for fixes, MINOR for new features, MAJOR for breaking changes. A changelog lists changes newest first, for humans, and Conventional Commits let tools compute both.","core":true},{"t":"Release engineering","gist":"A release is a Git tag plus a GitHub Release with notes, shipped through CI/CD with feature flags and canary rollouts, and rollback is a rehearsed operation. The part ends with how the PSM I exam works.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Scrum timeboxes: Sprint one month or less, Daily Scrum 15 minutes; for a one-month Sprint, Planning ≤ 8 h, Review ≤ 4 h, Retrospective ≤ 3 h.","part":0},{"p":"Little's Law: lead time = WIP ÷ throughput, so twelve items finishing 3 a week is a 4-week lead time; cutting WIP to 6 halves it.","part":1},{"p":"Story points are relative size, not hours; velocity gives a forecast range, never a promise.","part":2},{"p":"An ADR is a short numbered file in the repository recording a decision, its context and its consequences.","part":3},{"p":"SemVer: PATCH for compatible fixes, MINOR for compatible features, MAJOR for breaking changes; ^1.4.2 means at least 1.4.2 and below 2.0.0.","part":4}]},"p20m7":{"big":"Secure software treats every input as hostile, keeps data from ever becoming code, keeps secrets out of the repository, and fails closed when something unforeseen goes wrong.","why":"Defensive engineering is the half of security most pentesters never learn. It is why the best ones write findings developers can actually fix, and it keeps your own tools from becoming someone else's foothold.","parts":[{"t":"The OWASP Top 10:2025, as a developer's checklist","gist":"The OWASP Top 10:2025 lists ten categories of web risk, each with a defense in code. Broken access control comes first: deny by default and check permission on every request, for every object.","core":true},{"t":"Input validation and output encoding","gist":"Check every input at the boundary against an allowlist of what is allowed, and reject the rest. Then make injection impossible: parameterized queries and subprocess argument lists keep data from ever becoming code.","core":true},{"t":"Dependencies, SBOMs and the build pipeline","gist":"Most of your program is other people's code. Pin exact versions with a lockfile, scan dependencies in CI, keep an SBOM (a machine-readable ingredient list), and pin workflow actions to full commit SHAs.","core":true},{"t":"Secrets handling","gist":"A secret pushed to Git is compromised the moment it is pushed, and rewriting history does not un-leak it. Keep secrets in environment variables or a secret manager; if one escapes, rotate first, purge history second, audit third.","core":true},{"t":"Logging, error handling and observability","gist":"Log structured JSON with a correlation ID, and never log secrets. Catch only the exceptions you can handle, return a generic error to users, and fail closed: an exception in a permission check means deny.","core":true},{"t":"Professional ethics and the law","gist":"Codes of ethics bind engineers to the public interest and honesty, and the CFAA makes unauthorized access a crime: authorization is written scope. Flaws found outside an engagement go through coordinated disclosure.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A01 Broken Access Control: deny by default and authorize every request server-side, per object.","part":0},{"p":"Use parameterized queries and subprocess.run with an argument list, never shell=True with user input, so data never becomes code.","part":1},{"p":"An SBOM is the machine-readable ingredient list of your software, in SPDX or CycloneDX format, generated with tools such as Syft.","part":2},{"p":"If a secret leaks: rotate first, purge history second, audit third.","part":3},{"p":"Fail closed: an exception in the permission check means deny. Log the trace server-side and return a generic Problem Details error.","part":4}]},"p21m1":{"big":"A SOC turns logs into alerts and alerts into action, and its core skill is catching the one real attack without going numb from false alarms.","why":"When you run an authorized test, a SOC decides whether your actions become a ticket, an incident or nothing. Reports that name the detections that should have fired are the ones that get you hired again.","parts":[{"t":"Tiers and roles","gist":"Tier 1 analysts triage the alert queue, Tier 2 investigates and contains incidents, and Tier 3 hunts, does forensics and builds detections. A 24×7 SOC needs roughly five analysts per seat, which is why automation matters.","core":true},{"t":"The SIEM","gist":"A SIEM collects logs from everything, normalizes them into common field names, correlates events across sources and raises alerts. Its cost is set by volume: 500 endpoints at 50 MB a day is about 25 GB a day.","core":true},{"t":"Detection engineering","gist":"Detection engineering treats alert rules like software. A rule is judged by precision, the share of its alerts that are real; low-precision rules bury analysts in false alarms until they close the real one too.","core":true},{"t":"The frameworks: kill chain, ATT&CK, Pyramid of Pain","gist":"Defenders share a language: the Kill Chain's seven stages, MITRE ATT&CK's tactics (the why) and techniques (the how), and the Pyramid of Pain, which ranks indicators by how costly they are for an attacker to change.","core":true},{"t":"Triage, and the numbers that frame it","gist":"Triage asks six fast questions: what fired, on which asset, under which identity, when, is it true, and how far it reaches. Median dwell time, from intrusion to detection, was 14 days in M-Trends 2026.","core":false},{"t":"Why the pentester must think like a defender","gist":"A pentest is valuable to the extent it improves detection and response, so you must understand defense. Purple teaming, attacker and defender side by side, is the fastest-growing form of testing. Ends with the CySA+ mapping.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A SIEM collects, normalizes, correlates and alerts. 500 endpoints at 50 MB a day is about 25 GB a day; Splunk's Free license stops at 500 MB.","part":1},{"p":"Precision = true positives ÷ all alerts raised; a rule firing 200 times a day for 2 real incidents has 1% precision.","part":2},{"p":"Cyber Kill Chain: reconnaissance, weaponization, delivery, exploitation, installation, command and control, actions on objectives.","part":3},{"p":"In ATT&CK, tactics are the attacker's goal (why) and techniques the method (how), each with an ID like T1059.001.","part":3},{"p":"Pyramid of Pain: hashes and IP addresses are trivial for attackers to change; TTPs at the top are expensive, so detect behavior.","part":3}]},"p21m2":{"big":"Every attacker action leaves a record somewhere; defending means knowing which source writes it, putting all sources in one vocabulary and one clock, and searching them fast.","why":"Every investigation, hunt and detection in this domain is a search over these records. If you cannot read the logs, you cannot defend or prove anything.","parts":[{"t":"Windows: the Security log and Sysmon","gist":"Windows records logons, process starts and other actions as numbered events, such as 4624 for a successful logon and 4625 for a failed one. Sysmon adds richer detail, above all which process launched which.","core":true},{"t":"Linux","gist":"On Linux, logins and sudo commands land in /var/log/auth.log or /var/log/secure, and systemd's journal holds the rest. Forward logs over TLS, because logs an attacker can alter or drop in transit are not evidence.","core":false},{"t":"Network: firewall, proxy, DNS, flow, Zeek, Suricata","gist":"Each network source answers a different question: a firewall shows a connection was allowed or denied, a proxy adds the user and URL, DNS shows every name asked for, and NetFlow shows who talked to whom.","core":true},{"t":"Normalization","gist":"Correlation only works if a field like the source address has one name across all products. Schemas such as CIM, ECS and OCSF provide that, so when a detection does not fire, check the field names first.","core":true},{"t":"Splunk SPL: the searches you will actually run","gist":"An SPL search starts by narrowing with index and sourcetype filters, then pipes results through commands such as stats, timechart, eval, where and rex, much like the shell. Splunk Core Certified User tests this vocabulary.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Event 4624 is a successful logon and 4625 a failed one; many 4625s followed by a 4624 is a brute force that worked.","part":0},{"p":"Logon type 2 is interactive at the keyboard, 3 is network (SMB share, PsExec), and 10 is RemoteInteractive (RDP).","part":0},{"p":"Sysmon Event 1 is process creation with its parent, 3 a network connection, 22 a DNS query; parent-child chains are the richest signal.","part":0},{"p":"A firewall log shows only that bytes moved between two addresses on a port; user, content and intent come from proxy, EDR and host logs.","part":2},{"p":"In SPL, narrow early with index and sourcetype filters: it is the single largest speed lever.","part":4}]},"p21m3":{"big":"Incident response replaces adrenaline with a rehearsed plan that says what to do, in what order and who decides, while keeping everything usable as evidence.","why":"When an alert is true, a plan decided in advance lets you stop the attacker without destroying evidence or missing a legal deadline. CySA+ gives this 24% of the exam.","parts":[{"t":"The lifecycle: Rev. 2 phases, Rev. 3 functions, SANS steps","gist":"NIST's Rev. 2 gives four phases: Preparation; Detection and Analysis; Containment, Eradication and Recovery; Post-Incident Activity. Rev. 3 maps them onto CSF 2.0 functions, and SANS teaches six steps. Learn the mapping once.","core":true},{"t":"Preparation: plan, playbooks, jump bag","gist":"Preparation means a written plan, a playbook per incident type, a jump bag of tools, and an out-of-band channel in case the attacker reads your email. It also means backups that have actually been restored from.","core":false},{"t":"Detection and analysis: scoping and severity","gist":"Analysis turns an alert into a scoped incident: which hosts, identities and data, and since when. Keep a UTC incident log from the first minute, rate the severity, and reassess it as the scope changes.","core":true},{"t":"Containment, eradication, recovery","gist":"Contain without destroying evidence: isolate the host through EDR rather than pulling the plug, which wipes memory. Then remove every persistence, rotate every exposed credential, and restore from verified backups, identity first.","core":true},{"t":"Communication, legal and HR","gist":"An incident commander owns decisions, a scribe records them, and one communications lead speaks for the team. Legal joins at SEV2 to protect privilege and evidence and to track notification clocks, such as GDPR's 72 hours.","core":true},{"t":"Post-incident activity and the tabletop","gist":"Within two weeks, a blameless lessons-learned meeting turns the incident into new detections, controls and playbook edits. A tabletop exercise rehearses all of it, with timed injects that force real decisions.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Rev. 2 phases: Preparation; Detection and Analysis; Containment, Eradication and Recovery; Post-Incident Activity. SANS calls its six steps PICERL.","part":0},{"p":"Severity is reassessed as scope changes: a SEV3 phishing click becomes SEV1 when vssadmin delete shadows appears on a file server.","part":2},{"p":"Isolate a host through EDR instead of pulling the plug, which destroys memory; the exception is ransomware actively encrypting shared storage.","part":3},{"p":"Eradication sweeps every host in scope for persistence and rotates credentials, including KRBTGT twice if a domain controller was touched.","part":3},{"p":"GDPR: 72 hours after becoming aware. SEC 8-K: 4 business days after determining materiality. HIPAA: at most 60 days.","part":4}]},"p21m4":{"big":"Forensics reconstructs exactly what happened, in what order and by whom, from evidence collected and handled so that it still holds up when challenged a year later.","why":"Your findings must survive a lawyer, an HR director or an insurer. As a pentester, the same artifacts are how you prove exactly what you did and did not touch.","parts":[{"t":"Evidence handling and chain of custody","gist":"Collect the most perishable evidence first: memory before disk, network state before isolating. Every item gets a chain-of-custody record of who held it and when, and you work only on a verified copy.","core":true},{"t":"Imaging and hashing","gist":"A write blocker stops any change to the suspect drive while you copy it bit for bit. A matching SHA-256 hash proves the copy equals the source at that moment, but says nothing about what happened before you arrived.","core":true},{"t":"NTFS and the Windows artifacts","gist":"NTFS keeps two sets of timestamps per file, and only the kernel updates $FILE_NAME, which exposes faked dates. Artifacts like prefetch prove a program ran, and shellbags show which folders a user browsed.","core":true},{"t":"Registry and browser evidence","gist":"The registry shows persistence keys, which USB device by serial got which drive letter, and Wi-Fi networks joined. Browser histories are SQLite databases, and Chromium counts time in microseconds since 1601.","core":false},{"t":"Memory forensics with Volatility 3","gist":"Memory holds what disk never sees: unpacked malware, injected code, keys, live connections and full command lines. Volatility 3 reads a memory image with plugins such as pstree, cmdline, netscan and malfind.","core":true},{"t":"Timeline analysis","gist":"A super timeline merges every timestamp into one story. Normalize to UTC, start from a known-bad moment and read outward in both directions; the first anomaly is usually initial access.","core":false},{"t":"The report","gist":"A forensic report ties every finding to its artifact and keeps conclusions apart from opinions. Write what the system did, such as 'the prefetch file indicates EXCEL.EXE executed', never guesses about intent.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Order of volatility: capture RAM before you pull power, network state before you isolate, disks before you rebuild.","part":0},{"p":"A matching hash proves the image equals the source at acquisition time; everything before that rests on the chain of custody.","part":1},{"p":"A 2 TB drive read at 150 MB/s takes about 3.7 hours to image, and verification reads it again.","part":1},{"p":"Timestomping changes $STANDARD_INFORMATION, but only the kernel updates $FILE_NAME. Prefetch proves execution; shellbags prove folders browsed.","part":2},{"p":"In Volatility 3, a process in psscan but not pslist has been unlinked from the process list; malfind finds injected code.","part":4}]},"p21m5":{"big":"Triage tells you safely, in minutes, what a file is and does; hunting looks for the next intrusion by its behavior, and both should end in new detections.","why":"Triage decides whether deep reverse engineering is needed and gives the SOC something to search for today. Hunting helps you find the next intrusion before it raises an alert.","parts":[{"t":"Static triage in ten minutes","gist":"Start with what a file really is: its first bytes reveal the type, so an 'invoice.pdf' starting with MZ is a Windows executable. Then check hashes, strings, entropy and imports, and look up the hash on VirusTotal rather than uploading.","core":true},{"t":"Dynamic triage: the detonation lab","gist":"Running the sample shows what it drops, changes and contacts. Do it only in an isolated lab: a FLARE VM victim and REMnux faking the internet on a host-only network, reset to a clean snapshot every run.","core":false},{"t":"IOCs vs TTPs, and what to do with each","gist":"An IOC such as a hash or domain describes this one sample and changes quickly; a TTP describes how the attacker works and survives recompiles. IOCs go to searches and blocklists; behaviors become detections mapped to ATT&CK.","core":true},{"t":"YARA: describing files","gist":"YARA describes files: a rule lists strings or byte patterns and a condition that combines them. Test every rule against clean files first, because a rule that matches a Microsoft DLL pages someone at 3 a.m.","core":true},{"t":"Sigma: describing log events","gist":"Sigma describes detections in log events, written in vendor-neutral YAML, and converts to each SIEM's query language, such as Splunk's SPL. That is how one rule can serve every SIEM.","core":true},{"t":"Threat hunting","gist":"Threat hunting assumes the alerts missed something and tests a hypothesis about attacker behavior, using stacking, baselines and beacon analysis. A hunt ends with a written result and new detections, even when nothing is found.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Section entropy above about 7.2 bits per byte (of a possible 8) means the file is packed or encrypted.","part":0},{"p":"Look up a hash on VirusTotal instead of uploading the file: an upload shares it with every vendor and can tip off the adversary.","part":0},{"p":"IOCs go to retrospective search, blocklists and enrichment; behaviors (TTPs) are mapped to ATT&CK and become detections.","part":2},{"p":"YARA describes files and memory; Sigma describes log events in YAML and converts to SPL, KQL and other SIEM languages.","part":4},{"p":"Stacking is least-frequency analysis: look for processes, services or parent-child pairs seen on fewer than a handful of hosts.","part":5}]},"p21m6":{"big":"Of the thousands of findings a scanner reports, you fix first what attackers are actually exploiting on your exposed, critical assets, and you can show why.","why":"Exploiting a vulnerability was the leading way in, at 32% of intrusions in M-Trends 2026. Choosing what to fix today, and defending that choice to a manager, is the job.","parts":[{"t":"Asset inventory: the control before the control","gist":"You cannot fix what you do not know exists, so the asset inventory comes first. Build it from several sources, give each asset an owner and a criticality, and measure coverage: assets scanned divided by assets inventoried.","core":true},{"t":"Scanning","gist":"An unauthenticated scan sees only what an attacker sees from the network and guesses from version banners. A credentialed scan logs in and reads installed packages directly, so it is far more accurate. Validate every finding.","core":true},{"t":"CVSS: the arithmetic","gist":"CVSS v3.1 turns eight metrics into a severity score by formula: an unauthenticated remote code execution scores 9.8, Critical. But CVSS measures how bad an exploit would be, not how likely one is.","core":true},{"t":"Prioritization: KEV, EPSS and context","gist":"Most CVEs score High or Critical, so CVSS alone cannot set the order. Patch what CISA's KEV lists as actively exploited first, weigh EPSS's exploitation probability and the asset's exposure, and write the order into SLAs.","core":true},{"t":"Risk, quantified, in a register","gist":"Risk is likelihood times impact. ALE = SLE × ARO tells you in dollars whether a control pays for itself, and a risk register records every risk with its owner, treatment and review date.","core":true},{"t":"Reporting to management","gist":"Executives want risk, money and trends, not scanner exports: remediation times, SLA compliance, open KEV findings on exposed assets and coverage. Report accepted exceptions with expiry dates so they are never forgotten.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Credentialed scans read installed packages directly and are far more accurate; unauthenticated scans guess from banners, giving false positives and negatives.","part":1},{"p":"AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H scores 9.8; with Scope Changed, as for Log4Shell, it caps at 10.0.","part":2},{"p":"CVSS bands: Low 0.1–3.9, Medium 4.0–6.9, High 7.0–8.9, Critical 9.0–10.0.","part":2},{"p":"KEV means actively exploited; EPSS is the probability of exploitation in the next 30 days. With exposure, they set the patch order.","part":3},{"p":"ALE = SLE × ARO: an SLE of $500,000 at ARO 0.2 gives an ALE of $100,000 a year.","part":4}]},"p21m7":{"big":"Defense in depth stacks independent layers, each assumed to fail sometimes, and assumes breach: limit what a compromised machine can reach, see it quickly, and make sure data survives.","why":"It makes the attacker's job expensive before anything alerts. This is what you will recommend at the end of every authorized test, and what you will design when someone trusts you with a network.","parts":[{"t":"Segmentation and zero trust","gist":"Splitting a network into segments with default-deny rules between them limits how far a phished laptop can reach. Zero trust goes further: every request is authenticated and authorized, so being on the network no longer means being trusted.","core":true},{"t":"Firewalls, IDS/IPS and sensors","gist":"Firewalls need egress rules, default-deny outbound, which stop most command-and-control and exfiltration. An IDS watches a copy of traffic and alerts; an IPS sits inline and drops. Place sensors inside the perimeter firewall and at segment boundaries.","core":true},{"t":"Email: SPF, DKIM, DMARC","gist":"SPF lists which servers may send for your domain, DKIM signs each message, and DMARC ties both to the visible From address and says what to do with failures. Roll DMARC from p=none to p=reject in stages.","core":true},{"t":"Endpoints: EDR and hardening baselines","gist":"EDR records process, file, registry and network activity, detects behavior and lets you isolate a host from the console. Hardening baselines like the CIS Benchmarks remove what attackers need, for example Office macros from the internet.","core":true},{"t":"Backups and resilience","gist":"Ransomware actors delete backups first, so follow 3-2-1-1-0: three copies, two media, one off-site, one offline or immutable, zero restore errors. Check with arithmetic that you can really restore within your RTO.","core":true},{"t":"Metrics: measuring the program without lying to yourself","gist":"Report metrics that drive decisions, such as detection and response times, coverage and KEV exposure, as trends rather than snapshots. Goodhart's law warns that a rewarded number, like alerts closed, gets gamed.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Splitting 1,000 flat hosts into eight /25 segments cuts what a phished laptop can probe from 999 neighbors to 124.","part":0},{"p":"Zero trust (NIST SP 800-207) authenticates and authorizes every request; ZTNA with phishing-resistant MFA replaces the full-tunnel VPN.","part":0},{"p":"SPF checks the envelope sender, not the visible From; DMARC alignment protects the From header, and only at p=quarantine or p=reject.","part":2},{"p":"Backups follow 3-2-1-1-0 with one offline or immutable copy and tested restores; backup consoles get separate credentials and MFA.","part":4},{"p":"RPO is how much data you may lose, RTO how long you may be down; restoring 40 TB over 1 Gbps takes 89 hours.","part":4}]},"p22m1":{"big":"Turning a raw table into a claim you can defend is a discipline: a clear question, tidy and cleaned data, and metrics whose denominators are written down first.","why":"It decides whether your numbers become a decision or a pile, in Excel, pandas or Power BI alike, and it is what PL-300 and the Google Data Analytics certificate both test.","parts":[{"t":"The analytics lifecycle","gist":"Analysis runs in six looping phases: ask, prepare, process, analyze, share, act. Start by writing the question as something a number can answer. Most of the hours go into preparing and cleaning the data.","core":true},{"t":"Data types and the shape of a table","gist":"A column's measurement scale decides which math makes sense, and every table needs a primary key. Tidy data means each variable is a column and each observation a row; melt turns a wide sheet into that shape.","core":true},{"t":"Data quality and cleaning","gist":"Check data against six quality dimensions, such as accuracy, completeness and uniqueness, and keep a written cleaning log. Find out why values are missing, and treat outliers as rows to investigate, not to delete.","core":true},{"t":"Spreadsheets done professionally","gist":"Spreadsheets are legitimate tools when used with discipline: one header row, no merged cells, raw data never edited by hand. Use Tables, XLOOKUP, pivot tables and validation, and move to Python when the same cleaning repeats.","core":false},{"t":"The questions behind the numbers","gist":"A number without its denominator is a rumor. Define every metric with a numerator, denominator, time window and filters, and always ask 'compared to what?' Watch for base rates, Goodhart's law and Simpson's paradox.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"The six phases are ask, prepare, process, analyze, share, act, and most of the hours go into prepare and process.","part":0},{"p":"Tidy data: each variable a column, each observation a row, each kind of thing its own table; melt goes from wide to long.","part":1},{"p":"Tukey's fence flags values below Q1 − 1.5 × IQR or above Q3 + 1.5 × IQR, for a look, never for automatic deletion.","part":2},{"p":"Values missing not at random bias any analysis that drops those rows; find out why before you drop, fill or flag them.","part":2},{"p":"Simpson's paradox: a trend in every subgroup can reverse in the total when group sizes differ, so check the groups did the same work.","part":4}]},"p22m2":{"big":"NumPy and pandas let you load, shape, combine and summarize real data in Python fast enough to think with it, as long as you vectorize and merge carefully.","why":"Python is where you go when data outgrows the spreadsheet, when the same cleaning repeats, or when someone must check your work line by line. The later modules assume these operations are reflexes.","parts":[{"t":"NumPy: arrays, dtypes and vectorization","gist":"A NumPy array stores numbers of one type in one solid block of memory, so compiled code can loop over it fast. That is vectorization: work on the whole array at once, typically 10–100× faster than a Python loop.","core":true},{"t":"pandas: Series, DataFrames and the index","gist":"A DataFrame is a table of columns, each a Series with one dtype, sharing a row index. In pandas 3.0 text defaults to str and every selection acts like a copy, so set values with df.loc[mask, col] = value.","core":true},{"t":"Loading data","gist":"pandas reads CSV, JSON, SQL and Parquet. CSV is universal but loses types, a big database should do the heavy filtering itself, and anything you will read twice belongs in Parquet, which keeps types and compresses.","core":false},{"t":"Combining tables: merge and concat","gist":"merge is SQL's join for pandas tables. The silent danger is a key that repeats on the right side, which multiplies rows and inflates totals; validate='many_to_one' raises an error when that happens.","core":true},{"t":"Groupby, aggregation, reshaping and dates","gist":"groupby splits rows by a key, applies a function to each group and combines the results. pivot_table and melt reshape tables, and to_datetime, .dt and resample handle dates. Never fill a missing measurement with zero.","core":true},{"t":"A worked analysis: the Titanic training set","gist":"On the Titanic data, one groupby shows survival of 0.742 for women and 0.189 for men. But 177 ages are missing, about a fifth of the rows, and an honest findings paragraph must say so.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A million float64 values take 8 MB as a NumPy array versus roughly 32 MB as a Python list.","part":0},{"p":"Vectorized NumPy and pandas operations are typically 10–100× faster than a Python loop over rows.","part":0},{"p":"Under pandas 3.0's Copy-on-Write, df[cond]['col'] = value never changes the frame; write df.loc[cond, 'col'] = value.","part":1},{"p":"A non-unique key on the right side of a merge multiplies rows, even in a left join; validate='many_to_one' catches it.","part":3},{"p":"groupby is split-apply-combine: split rows by a key, apply a function to each group, combine the results.","part":4}]},"p22m3":{"big":"A chart exists to make one comparison obvious, so you choose it from the question, state the conclusion in the title, and never let it distort the data.","why":"Visualization is where analysis meets the decision maker, and interviews judge it hardest because a portfolio is pictures. Naming the lie factor lets you challenge a dishonest chart in a meeting.","parts":[{"t":"Chart choice follows the question","gist":"Pick the chart from the question: bars compare categories, lines show change over time, histograms and box plots show distributions, and scatters show relationships. Pies, 3D and dual axes usually mislead.","core":true},{"t":"matplotlib and seaborn","gist":"matplotlib draws, and seaborn adds statistical charts on top, from tidy data. The professional pattern: a title that states the conclusion, bars starting at zero, and the key event annotated on the chart itself.","core":false},{"t":"Power BI visuals and dashboards","gist":"A Power BI report is pages of visuals over one model, and a dashboard pins tiles from reports. Its strength over a static chart is interaction: cross-filtering, drillthrough, bookmarks and slicers.","core":false},{"t":"Color and accessibility","gist":"About 8% of men have red-green color-vision deficiency, so never rely on red versus green alone. Use colorblind-safe palettes, add labels or shapes, and keep text contrast at 4.5:1 or better.","core":true},{"t":"How charts lie","gist":"Most dishonest charts use one of six tricks, such as a bar axis that does not start at zero or a cherry-picked time window. The lie factor, visual change divided by real change, puts a number on the distortion.","core":true},{"t":"Presenting to decision makers","gist":"Put the conclusion in the title and the recommendation in the first sentence. One message per chart, clutter removed, one color for the point that matters. If a viewer cannot say what it shows in five seconds, redesign.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Bars compare categories (sorted, axis at zero), lines show change over time, histograms and box plots show distributions, scatters show relationships.","part":0},{"p":"Use a pie only with 2–3 slices, because humans compare angles badly.","part":0},{"p":"About one in twelve men cannot reliably tell red from green; use Okabe-Ito or viridis and add labels or shapes.","part":3},{"p":"Lie factor = visual change ÷ data change: bars of height 1 and 3 for values 50 and 52 give 200% ÷ 4% = 50.","part":4},{"p":"The five-second test: show a chart for five seconds and ask what it says; if they cannot say, redesign it.","part":5}]},"p22m4":{"big":"Statistics says how good your guess is, so you can tell a real difference from noise and report every claim with its sample size and interval.","why":"It separates 'the number went up' from 'the number went up and here is how sure we are', the sentence every stakeholder, pentest client and hiring manager actually wants.","parts":[{"t":"Descriptive statistics","gist":"The mean is the sum divided by n; the median is the middle value and the honest center of skewed data. The standard deviation measures spread, and a z-score counts how many standard deviations a value sits from the mean.","core":true},{"t":"Distributions","gist":"In a normal distribution, 68% of values lie within 1 SD and 95% within 2. The central limit theorem says a sample's mean is approximately normal, whatever the population's shape, once n is a few dozen.","core":true},{"t":"Sampling error, standard error and confidence intervals","gist":"The standard error measures how much an estimate would vary across repeated samples, and it shrinks with the square root of n. A 95% confidence interval is the estimate plus or minus 1.96 standard errors.","core":true},{"t":"Hypothesis tests and p-values","gist":"A p-value is the chance of seeing a difference at least this large if there were truly no effect. It is not the chance that the null is true, and it says nothing about how big the effect is.","core":true},{"t":"A/B testing design","gist":"An A/B test randomly splits users, fixes one primary metric in writing before launch, and computes the needed sample size first. Run it to that size without peeking, and check the split ratio and guardrail metrics.","core":true},{"t":"Correlation versus causation","gist":"Correlation measures linear association but does not prove cause: reverse causation, a confounder or selection can explain it. Randomization, as in an A/B test, is what licenses a causal claim.","core":true},{"t":"Regression","gist":"Least-squares regression fits the best straight line through the data. In multiple regression each coefficient is the change in y per unit of one x with the others held fixed, which adjusts for a confounder you can measure.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Standard error = σ / √n, so quadrupling the sample halves the error.","part":2},{"p":"A p-value is the probability of data this extreme if the null were true, not the probability that the null is true.","part":3},{"p":"100 of 1,000 versus 125 of 1,000 gives z = 1.77 and p ≈ 0.077: a 25% relative lift, yet not significant, because the test was underpowered.","part":3},{"p":"Peeking and stopping at the first p < 0.05 inflates the false-positive rate several-fold; run to the planned sample size.","part":4},{"p":"Three alternatives always compete with 'X causes Y': reverse causation, a confounder, and selection.","part":5}]},"p22m5":{"big":"A pattern in old data becomes a trustworthy prediction only when you prove it on held-out data you never touched until the end.","why":"This workflow is how you prove a model works instead of hoping. The same steps sit under deep learning, SOC anomaly detection and malware classification.","parts":[{"t":"The workflow","gist":"Decide the task first: predict a number (regression), a class (classification), or find structure without labels (unsupervised). Then split: train to fit, validation to choose, test opened once at the end. Split time-ordered data by time.","core":true},{"t":"Feature engineering","gist":"Raw columns become numbers a model can use: categories become 0/1 columns, some models need scaled numbers, gaps get filled. Leakage, using something you wouldn't have at prediction time, is the cardinal sin; Pipelines block it.","core":true},{"t":"The models","gist":"Linear and logistic regression are the simple baselines. Random forests and gradient boosting, built from many decision trees, usually win on tables, and k-means groups rows into clusters. Neural networks win on images, audio and text.","core":true},{"t":"Evaluation metrics","gist":"Choose the metric from what each kind of error costs. With rare fraud, 97% accuracy can hide weakness: precision (flags that were right) is 66.7% and recall (frauds caught) 80%. For numbers, MAE and RMSE measure the misses.","core":true},{"t":"Overfitting and cross-validation","gist":"Overfitting is memorizing the training data: training error keeps falling while validation error starts to rise. Cross-validation measures this honestly by rotating through k folds, and simpler models or regularization help fight it.","core":true},{"t":"Model cards and the bridge to deep learning","gist":"A model card is a one-page document that ships with a model: what it is for, its data, its metrics overall and by subgroup, and its known limits. Deep learning reuses this same workflow and vocabulary.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"The training set fits the model, the validation set chooses between models, and the test set is opened once at the end for the number you report.","part":0},{"p":"Leakage is a feature that would not exist at prediction time; a Pipeline fits every transform on the training fold only.","part":1},{"p":"Precision is how many flags were right; recall is how many real cases were caught. In the fraud example they are 66.7% and 80%.","part":3},{"p":"Overfitting shows as training error falling while validation error rises; k-fold cross-validation trains on k − 1 folds and validates on the held-out one.","part":4},{"p":"A model card records intended use, training data, metrics overall and by subgroup, and known limitations.","part":5}]},"p22m6":{"big":"A trustworthy pipeline runs on schedule, pulls only new rows, validates them and fails loudly, and loads so that a rerun changes nothing.","why":"It gets data from where it is born to where the decision is made, correctly and on schedule, with someone alerted when it breaks. It also covers three of PL-300's four domains.","parts":[{"t":"SQL plus Python pipelines","gist":"Two rules make a pipeline trustworthy: a rerun gives the same result, not duplicates (idempotence), and you pull only rows newer than the last one you saw (a watermark). Never build SQL from strings; use parameters.","core":true},{"t":"APIs, scraping and the ethics of taking data","gist":"Use the official API when there is one. Scraping is a last resort with rules: read robots.txt and the terms, throttle, never bypass a login. hiQ v. LinkedIn covered only the CFAA; other laws still apply.","core":true},{"t":"Scheduling: cron to Airflow","gist":"cron runs a job at set times but has no retries, history or alerts. Once steps depend on each other, an orchestrator like Airflow runs a DAG of tasks, retries failures and records every run.","core":true},{"t":"Data validation","gist":"Check the data at the boundary on every run: columns and types, forbidden nulls, ranges, unique keys, row counts and freshness. When a check fails, stop and alert a person instead of quietly dropping bad rows.","core":true},{"t":"Warehouses, lakes and the star schema","gist":"Analytics reads a few columns from millions of rows, so warehouses store data by column. Lakes hold raw files and lakehouses add tables on top. Inside sits a star schema: a fact table surrounded by dimension tables.","core":true},{"t":"The Power BI data model and DAX","gist":"In Power BI, Power Query prepares the data, relationships link the star schema, and DAX measures calculate under the visual's filters, with CALCULATE changing those filters. Row-level security shows each user only their own rows.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Idempotent means running the same day twice gives the same result, so loads delete-and-replace a partition or upsert on a key.","part":0},{"p":"Never build SQL by string formatting; use parameterized queries, or you have written a SQL injection into your own pipeline.","part":0},{"p":"cron's five fields are minute, hour, day-of-month, month, day-of-week: 0 2 * * * is 02:00 daily. It has no retries, history or alerting.","part":2},{"p":"A star schema has a tall, narrow fact table at a declared grain, linked to short, wide dimension tables, plus a date table.","part":4},{"p":"A measure is computed at query time in the visual's filter context; a calculated column is computed per row and stored.","part":5}]},"p22m7":{"big":"A correct analysis only matters if a decision maker acts on it, and the data behind it must be handled lawfully, fairly and reproducibly.","why":"A correct analysis nobody acts on is wasted, and one that leaks personal data is a liability. The same report skills become the penetration-test report in security work.","parts":[{"t":"Reports and executive summaries","gist":"Put the bottom line up front (BLUF): the answer first, then three numbers with denominators and uncertainty, the recommendation with cost and owner, caveats and the next step. The reader should be able to act after the first paragraph.","core":true},{"t":"Dashboards that get used","gist":"Dashboards that survive have one purpose and audience, the key number top-left, fewer than seven visuals, a 'data as of' stamp, published definitions and a named owner. Measure usage and delete pages nobody uses.","core":true},{"t":"Privacy: GDPR, HIPAA and the analyst's rules","gist":"Laws like GDPR and HIPAA set the rules for personal data. In practice: collect the minimum, keep it the shortest time, replace identifiers with keys, share aggregates, suppress cells under about ten people, and limit who sees it.","core":true},{"t":"Bias and fairness","gist":"Bias enters through the sample, the labels, proxies like ZIP code, and feedback loops. Fairness definitions conflict when groups' base rates differ, as COMPAS showed, so report metrics by subgroup and let a human override decisions about livelihoods.","core":true},{"t":"Reproducibility and notebook hygiene","gist":"Someone else should be able to rerun your work and get the same numbers. Pin package versions, set seeds for random steps, never edit raw data, and make sure 'Restart kernel and run all' passes before you share.","core":true},{"t":"The career ladder","gist":"The roles run roughly from data analyst to analytics engineer, data engineer, data scientist and ML engineer. Hiring managers read portfolios before certificates: three public projects, each with a README, notebook, report and dashboard.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"BLUF: the answer goes in the first sentence, then three key numbers with denominators and uncertainty, then the recommendation with a cost and an owner.","part":0},{"p":"GDPR requires breach notification within 72 hours, with fines up to 20 million euros or 4% of worldwide annual turnover, whichever is higher.","part":2},{"p":"HIPAA's Safe Harbor removes 18 identifier types. Before sharing, suppress cells with fewer than about ten people, because small cells re-identify.","part":2},{"p":"Demographic parity (equal positive rates), equal opportunity (equal recall) and calibration cannot all hold when base rates differ.","part":3},{"p":"Reproducible work has pinned environments, seeds for every random step and untouched raw data, and passes 'Restart kernel and run all' before sharing.","part":4}]},"p23m1":{"big":"Voltage pushes, current flows and resistance opposes, tied together by V = I × R; that arithmetic plus a few firm safety rules lets you reason about any circuit.","why":"Every pin, motor, battery and router-serial-header decision later in this domain is this arithmetic, and the safety rules keep a bench hobby from becoming an injury.","parts":[{"t":"Charge, voltage, current","gist":"Current is how much charge flows per second, in amperes. Voltage is the push between two points, in volts, always measured against a chosen reference called ground. When a circuit fails mysteriously, find the ground first.","core":true},{"t":"Resistance, Ohm's law and power","gist":"Resistance opposes current; Ohm's law links the three: V = I × R. Power, P = V × I, sets how hot a part gets: a 220 Ω resistor is fine on 5 V but smokes on 12 V.","core":true},{"t":"Series and parallel","gist":"In series, parts share one current and resistances add. In parallel, parts share one voltage and the total is less than the smallest branch. A voltage divider, two resistors in series, turns a changing resistance into a readable voltage.","core":true},{"t":"Kirchhoff's laws","gist":"Current flowing into any junction equals current flowing out, and voltages around any closed loop add up to zero. These solve circuits Ohm's law alone can't, and a mismatch points to a path you haven't drawn.","core":true},{"t":"The multimeter","gist":"Measure voltage across two points with the power on, current by breaking the circuit and making the meter part of the path, and resistance only with the power off. CAT ratings say how much transient energy a meter survives.","core":true},{"t":"Electrical safety: what hurts and the rules","gist":"Current through your body does the damage: about 10–16 mA locks your muscles, and 50–100 mA across the chest can stop the heart. Anything above 50 V counts as hazardous, and capacitors stay charged after unplugging.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Ohm's law: V = I × R; power: P = V × I. A 220 Ω resistor on 5 V passes 22.7 mA and dissipates 0.114 W.","part":1},{"p":"Series: one shared current, resistances add. Parallel: one shared voltage, currents add; two 1 kΩ resistors in parallel make 500 Ω.","part":2},{"p":"Kirchhoff: current into a node equals current out, and voltages around any closed loop sum to zero (9 − 3 − 6 = 0).","part":3},{"p":"Measure voltage in parallel, current in series, and resistance or continuity only on an unpowered circuit.","part":4},{"p":"About 10–16 mA is the let-go threshold, codes treat exposed conductors above 50 V as hazardous, and you discharge capacitors through a resistor, never a screwdriver.","part":5}]},"p23m2":{"big":"Every part has two or three numbers that govern its life, voltage, current and heat; read them from its markings and datasheet and design well inside them.","why":"A correctly sized resistor keeps an LED alive for decades, the right transistor switches a motor without a heatsink, and a derated capacitor does not burst on your bench.","parts":[{"t":"Resistors","gist":"Color bands give two digits and a multiplier: red-red-brown is 220 Ω. The odd standard values, the E12 series, are spaced about 20% apart so that, with tolerance, every resistance is covered.","core":true},{"t":"Capacitors and inductors","gist":"Capacitors store charge: 100 nF beside every chip covers sudden current draws, and R × C sets timing. Run electrolytics well under their rated voltage. Switching off a coil makes a voltage spike, so coils get a flyback diode.","core":true},{"t":"Diodes and LEDs","gist":"A diode conducts one way. An LED is a current device: a resistor must limit its current, and from 5 V a red LED at 20 mA needs 150 Ω. Anything bigger than one LED gets a transistor.","core":true},{"t":"Transistors as switches and amplifiers","gist":"A transistor lets a tiny pin current switch a big load. Drive a BJT (current-controlled) hard through a base resistor, or use a logic-level MOSFET (voltage-controlled) that is fully on at 3.3–5 V; an IRF540 isn't.","core":true},{"t":"Voltage regulators","gist":"A linear regulator burns the extra voltage as heat, so 12 V down to 5 V at 0.5 A wastes 3.5 W. A switching regulator does the same job at 85–95% efficiency, losing about 0.3 W.","core":true},{"t":"Reading a datasheet","gist":"Read a datasheet in a fixed order. Absolute maximum ratings are the cliff edge: design nowhere near them. Design to the min and max values, never to 'typical', which describes one lucky part at 25 °C.","core":true},{"t":"Breadboard hygiene","gist":"Each breadboard row of five holes is connected, and the side power rails are often split in the middle. Keep leads short, put 100 nF beside every chip, and keep anything over about 1 A, or mains, off the board.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Four-band resistors read digit, digit, multiplier, tolerance: red-red-brown-gold is 22 × 10^1 = 220 Ω ±5%.","part":0},{"p":"LED resistor: R = (V supply − V f)/I. From 5 V, a 2.0 V red LED at 20 mA needs 150 Ω.","part":2},{"p":"Only logic-level MOSFETs such as the IRLZ44N turn fully on at 3.3–5 V; the IRF540 needs 10 V and overheats half-on.","part":3},{"p":"A linear regulator loses (V in − V out) × I as heat: an LM7805 from 12 V at 0.5 A dissipates 3.5 W.","part":4},{"p":"Stay far from a datasheet's absolute maximum ratings, and design to min and max values, never to typical.","part":5}]},"p23m3":{"big":"Digital electronics is analog with agreed voltage thresholds, so most embedded 'software bugs' are really level, timing or wiring problems you can see on the wire.","why":"Reading signals explains why correct programs fail on healthy chips, and UART, I2C and SPI are the physical doors into every embedded device you will later assess with permission or reverse engineer.","parts":[{"t":"Logic levels","gist":"A logic 1 or 0 is a voltage range: 5 V TTL reads below 0.8 V as LOW and above 2.0 V as HIGH. Feeding 5 V into a 3.3 V-only pin can destroy it, so shift the level first.","core":true},{"t":"Pull-ups, pull-downs and floating inputs","gist":"An input connected to nothing floats and reads random noise. A pull-up or pull-down resistor gives it a defined resting level. Smaller values give cleaner edges but use more current; I2C needs external pull-ups, typically 4.7 kΩ.","core":true},{"t":"Switch debouncing","gist":"A button doesn't close cleanly; it chatters for 1–20 ms and looks like many presses. Fix it in software by ignoring changes for 30–50 ms, or in hardware with an RC filter and a Schmitt trigger.","core":true},{"t":"The 555 timer","gist":"The 555 chip turns two resistors and a capacitor into a repeating signal. Their values set the frequency and the duty cycle, the fraction of each period the signal is high, the same quantity PWM controls later.","core":true},{"t":"Flip-flops and registers","gist":"A D flip-flop copies its input only on a clock edge and holds it otherwise. Eight sharing a clock make an 8-bit register, which is what a CPU register is. A shift register lets three pins drive eight outputs.","core":true},{"t":"Serial buses at the signal level","gist":"UART sends framed bytes with no clock, so both ends must agree on the baud rate. I2C shares two pulled-up wires among addressed devices that acknowledge each byte. SPI adds a clock and a chip-select line per device.","core":true},{"t":"The logic analyzer","gist":"A logic analyzer records several digital lines at once and decodes the bus traffic. A cheap one with PulseView is enough; sample at least four times the bit rate. Waveform shape is the oscilloscope's job.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"5 V TTL reads below 0.8 V as LOW and above 2.0 V as HIGH; 5 V into a 3.3 V-only pin can destroy it.","part":0},{"p":"Every input needs a pull-up, a pull-down or a driver; I2C needs external pull-ups, with 4.7 kΩ the default.","part":1},{"p":"Switches chatter for 1–20 ms; debounce in software by ignoring further changes for 30–50 ms.","part":2},{"p":"A UART frame is a LOW start bit, eight data bits least-significant first, and a HIGH stop bit; 115,200 baud carries at most 11,520 bytes per second.","part":5},{"p":"In I2C the addressed device pulls SDA low on the ninth clock as an ACK; a missing ACK means nobody answered.","part":5}]},"p23m4":{"big":"A microcontroller is a complete computer on one chip, and every pin, timer and converter in it is controlled by writing registers at fixed addresses.","why":"Knowing what is inside the chip lets you read a datasheet instead of copying a tutorial, choose the right board for a job, and later make sense of firmware you pull apart.","parts":[{"t":"Four families","gist":"The 8-bit AVR in the Arduino UNO is the sturdy 5 V teacher, ARM Cortex-M the industry workhorse, the ESP32 the default when you need Wi-Fi or Bluetooth, and the RP2040 the chip in the $4 Pico.","core":true},{"t":"The memory map","gist":"Program, data and hardware all live at addresses. Writing a bit at a peripheral's address flips a pin, and that is what every library does underneath. The vector table at the start of flash tells the chip where to begin.","core":true},{"t":"GPIO, timers and interrupts","gist":"Pins can be inputs, outputs or handed to a peripheral. A timer counts clock ticks through a prescaler (a divider) and can fire on an exact schedule. An interrupt calls your function on an event; keep it short.","core":true},{"t":"ADC and PWM","gist":"The ADC turns a voltage into a number: the UNO maps 0–5 V to 0–1023, 4.88 mV per step. PWM switches a pin at a fixed rate; the fraction of time it's high dims LEDs or sets motor speed.","core":true},{"t":"Clock speed and what it buys","gist":"Megahertz is not work. A 48 MHz Cortex-M4 with hardware math finishes the same calculation about fifty times faster than a 16 MHz AVR, and faster clocks cost power, so many designs run slower on purpose.","core":false},{"t":"Programming: Arduino C/C++, MicroPython, vendor SDKs","gist":"Arduino C/C++ wraps the registers in portable functions and libraries. MicroPython gives you Python on the chip, great for exploring but 10–100 times slower than C. Vendor SDKs expose everything at the cost of more setup.","core":true},{"t":"The toolchain and bootloaders","gist":"The compiler turns your code into an image file and an uploader writes it to flash. A bootloader is a tiny program that accepts new firmware; left unlocked, it is also how firmware gets read out of a product.","core":true},{"t":"Raspberry Pi or microcontroller?","gist":"A Raspberry Pi is a Linux computer: powerful, but slow to boot, power-hungry, with millisecond timing jitter and no ADC. A microcontroller times to the microsecond and sleeps on microamps. The professional pattern uses both together.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Peripherals are memory-mapped registers: writing one bit at an address flips a pin, which is what every library does underneath.","part":1},{"p":"Timer arithmetic: 16,000,000 / 1024 = 15,625 Hz, so counting 0 to 15,624 fires exactly once per second.","part":2},{"p":"In an interrupt handler, set a flag and return, declare shared variables volatile, and never call delay() or Serial.print().","part":2},{"p":"The UNO's 10-bit ADC maps 0–5 V to 0–1023, so one step is 4.88 mV; the ESP32 and RP2040 are 12-bit.","part":3},{"p":"Put real-time sensing, timing and actuation on a microcontroller, and networking, storage and UI on a Raspberry Pi.","part":7}]},"p23m5":{"big":"Here a chip becomes a device: sensors read the world, drivers let a weak pin control motors and relays, and sleeping most of the time makes a battery last.","why":"Every sensor, motor and battery decision in the cyberdeck you planned, and in any field sensor you deploy, is this same power and noise arithmetic.","parts":[{"t":"Sensors","gist":"Sensors give either an analog voltage or a reading over a digital bus. Analog ones depend on the ADC's accuracy, so keep leads short and average readings. Digital ones need the right address, pull-ups and voltage.","core":true},{"t":"Actuators and their drivers","gist":"No actuator connects to a pin directly. Always go pin, driver, actuator: an H-bridge (four switches that set direction) for DC motors, a current-limiting driver for steppers, a transistor and flyback diode for relays, with a shared ground.","core":true},{"t":"Power budgeting","gist":"Add every current at its worst case plus 30% margin. Battery life depends on how long the device sleeps: a node awake 2 s every 10 minutes averages 0.593 mA and lasts about 140 days on an 18650.","core":true},{"t":"Batteries","gist":"Each chemistry has its own voltage range, and energy in watt-hours is volts times amp-hours. Lithium cells pack a lot of energy but need protection, a real charger and a fuse, and must never be shorted or punctured.","core":true},{"t":"Noise and decoupling","gist":"When a motor starts, fast current changes make the supply dip and the chip resets. The fixes: 100 nF capacitors at every power pin, a separate supply for motors, and grounds joined at one star point.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Never run a servo or motor from the Arduino's 5 V pin; give actuators their own supply with a common ground.","part":1},{"p":"Battery life is set by duty cycling: awake 2 s at 160 mA and asleep 598 s at 60 µA averages 0.593 mA.","part":2},{"p":"Deep sleep's 10 µA is the chip alone; a board's regulator and power LED can add 5 mA, cutting about 140 days of battery to 15.","part":2},{"p":"Energy in watt-hours is volts times amp-hours: an 18650 at 3.7 V × 2.5 Ah holds 9.25 Wh.","part":3},{"p":"Stop motor resets with 100 nF decoupling at every power pin, a separate motor supply, and grounds joined at a single star point.","part":4}]},"p23m6":{"big":"You build a board you can trust with good joints, a schematic and KiCad, and you find faults by measuring: power first, ground second, signals third, code last.","why":"A soldered, enclosed board you designed is proof that you can build hardware, and the fault-finding method makes you fast when a device in the field stops working.","parts":[{"t":"Soldering","gist":"Heat the pad and the lead together, then feed solder into the joint. A good joint is bright and concave; a dull, grainy one is cold, and a blob joining two pads is a bridge. Extract flux fumes.","core":true},{"t":"Oscilloscope fundamentals","gist":"A scope draws voltage over time, so you see a signal's shape. Choose bandwidth at least five times your highest frequency and use 10× probes. The ground clip is tied to mains earth: never clip it to a live node.","core":true},{"t":"Logic analyzer, revisited","gist":"The logic analyzer decodes what many lines say over a long capture; the scope shows whether a signal is electrically healthy. A tricky problem, like a bus that fails only when a relay clicks, needs both at once.","core":false},{"t":"Reading schematics","gist":"A schematic is the circuit written in symbols. Reference designators name parts, like U2 for an IC or Q1 for a transistor, and points with the same net label are connected, even on different pages.","core":true},{"t":"PCB design in KiCad","gist":"KiCad is free and used professionally. The flow is schematic, electrical rules check (ERC), board layout, design rules check (DRC), then Gerber and drill files that a fabricator turns into five boards for a few dollars.","core":true},{"t":"Enclosures","gist":"A device without an enclosure is a prototype. Mount the board on standoffs, strain-relieve every cable, ventilate anything over a watt, keep the antenna away from metal, and label every connector.","core":false},{"t":"Common failures and the method","gist":"Find faults by method: reproduce the failure, check power at the chip, then ground, then signals, and code last, changing one thing at a time. Most failures are power, ground, levels or wiring, not code.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Set the iron to 300–330 °C for leaded or 320–350 °C for lead-free; a good joint is bright and concave, a cold one dull and grainy.","part":0},{"p":"The scope's ground clip is bonded to mains earth, so clipping it to a node not at earth potential shorts that node through the scope.","part":1},{"p":"Two points with the same net label are connected even on different pages; U marks an IC, Q a transistor, J a connector.","part":3},{"p":"KiCad's flow: schematic, ERC, PCB layout, DRC, then Gerber and drill files uploaded to a fabricator.","part":4},{"p":"Debug in order: power first (measure every rail at the chip under load), ground second, signals third, code last, one change at a time.","part":6}]},"p23m7":{"big":"Firmware that runs for months is a non-blocking state machine, and a connected device needs unique credentials, TLS, signed updates and locked debug ports to stay out of a botnet.","why":"IoT devices are a leading target of the authorized testing you are training for. Building one correctly, with every defense in place, teaches the attack surface better than any slide, and legally.","parts":[{"t":"State machines","gist":"Good firmware is a small set of named states, like sampling, connecting and sleeping, with explicit rules for moving between them. Nothing blocks, timeouts are planned transitions, and the diagram you draw first becomes the documentation.","core":true},{"t":"Timing without delay()","gist":"delay() freezes everything else. Instead, check whether enough time has passed using millis() subtraction, which keeps working through the 49.7-day rollover. A hardware watchdog resets the chip if your loop ever stops.","core":true},{"t":"RTOS basics","gist":"FreeRTOS schedules several tasks, each with its own stack and priority. Queues pass data safely between tasks and from interrupts; mutexes guard shared things like the I2C bus. For one sensor loop, a state machine is simpler.","core":false},{"t":"Low-power modes","gist":"ESP32 deep sleep keeps only the RTC and 8 KB of RTC memory alive at about 10 µA, then restarts from reset, so save state first. Turn off unused peripherals, and measure the current rather than guessing.","core":true},{"t":"Connectivity","gist":"Store Wi-Fi credentials in NVS, never in source code. With MQTT, devices publish and subscribe to topics through a broker, with QoS levels, retained messages and a Last Will. Use port 8883 with TLS for anything real.","core":true},{"t":"Over-the-air updates","gist":"An OTA update downloads the new image into a spare partition and rolls back if it fails to confirm itself. Always fetch it over TLS with the certificate verified, and run only signed images.","core":true},{"t":"IoT security, defensively","gist":"Mirai took over hundreds of thousands of devices with about sixty default passwords. The baseline: unique credentials, TLS everywhere, signed firmware, locked debug ports, an isolated IoT network, and testing only devices you own or have written permission to test.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Time with if (millis() - last >= 500): the unsigned subtraction survives the 49.7-day rollover, while millis() > last + 500 does not.","part":1},{"p":"MQTT QoS 0 is fire-and-forget, 1 is at-least-once, 2 is exactly-once; the wildcard + matches one level and # everything below.","part":4},{"p":"MQTT port 1883 is plaintext, only for a lab network you own; port 8883 is MQTT over TLS and is the one you deploy.","part":4},{"p":"OTA writes to the idle partition and rolls back on failure; fetch only over verified TLS and run only signed images.","part":5},{"p":"Mirai spread using about sixty factory default passwords; weak or hardcoded passwords are still number one on the OWASP IoT Top 10.","part":6}]},"p24m1":{"big":"Light stays trapped in a fiber's core by total internal reflection, and every number you measure about how much of it survives is decibel arithmetic.","why":"Every number on your power meter, every 'why does it fail at 1550 but not 1310', and every loss budget later comes straight from these facts, and the CFOT exam asks them directly.","parts":[{"t":"Refraction and total internal reflection","gist":"Light slows and bends in glass. The core slows light slightly more than the cladding around it, so rays meeting the boundary beyond the critical angle, about 85.3°, reflect completely and stay trapped. The cladding is half the mirror.","core":true},{"t":"Numerical aperture and modes","gist":"Numerical aperture measures how wide a cone of light a fiber accepts. Large multimode cores gather more light but allow many paths that arrive at different times. A 9 µm single-mode core at 1310 nm allows exactly one path.","core":true},{"t":"Attenuation: where the light goes","gist":"Fiber loses power with distance through scattering, absorption and bending. Scattering is much worse at short wavelengths, so loss is about 0.35 dB/km at 1310 nm and 0.2 dB/km at 1550 nm. Bend loss grows with wavelength.","core":true},{"t":"Dispersion: why pulses smear","gist":"Dispersion spreads pulses out in time, limiting speed rather than power. In multimode, different paths arrive at different times; in single-mode, different wavelengths travel at slightly different speeds. Each kilometer also adds about 4.9 µs of delay.","core":true},{"t":"Decibels: the arithmetic of the trade","gist":"Decibels turn multiplying into adding: 3 dB is half the power, 10 dB a tenth. dBm is absolute power, with 0 dBm meaning 1 mW. Launch power in dBm minus loss in dB gives received power in dBm.","core":true},{"t":"Glass versus copper, and the exam","gist":"Copper's losses grow with frequency, so Cat6A stops at 100 m for 10 Gb/s. Single-mode fiber loses 0.2 dB/km at 1550 nm and is immune to electromagnetic interference. The CFOT exam tests this whole module.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Critical angle: sin θc = n2 ÷ n1. For a 1.468 core and 1.463 cladding it is about 85.3° from the normal.","part":0},{"p":"OS2 single-mode has a core of about 9 µm and runs at 1310 and 1550 nm; multimode cores are 50 or 62.5 µm.","part":1},{"p":"Attenuation is about 0.35 dB/km at 1310 nm and 0.2 dB/km at 1550 nm, the minimum.","part":2},{"p":"A spot that loses more at 1550 nm than at 1310 nm is a bend or stress, not a bad splice.","part":2},{"p":"3 dB is half the power and 10 dB a tenth; 0 dBm is 1 mW. Never add two dBm figures together.","part":4}]},"p24m2":{"big":"Outside-plant cable protects glass that must never be stretched, the handling rules exist to keep it that way, and the footage marks let you prove your as-built adds up.","why":"This is your job turned into engineering: when you can explain why each rule exists and hand in a sheet that reconciles, you are the tech the office trusts with the next segment.","parts":[{"t":"Inside the cable","gist":"Glass must never be stretched, so the cable takes the strain. Fibers float in buffer tubes, typically 12 per tube, around a strength member, inside water blocking, aramid yarn and a jacket. The jacket print gives type, count and footage.","core":true},{"t":"The color code","gist":"Twelve colors in a fixed order, starting blue, orange, green, brown, number both the fibers in each tube and the tubes themselves. So fiber 137 of an 864 is the fifth fiber, slate, in tube 12, aqua.","core":true},{"t":"Rules of handling","gist":"Pull on the strength member, never the fiber, and stay under the rated tension. Keep the bend radius at least 20 times the cable diameter under load and 10 times at rest. Leave slack loops for future splices and repairs.","core":true},{"t":"How the plant is built","gist":"Cable is lashed to poles (aerial), pulled through ducts (underground), plowed into the ground (direct-buried) or blown through microducts. Handholes are buried boxes where it is coiled or spliced. STA 12+50 means 1,250 feet from the segment's zero.","core":true},{"t":"Writing the as-built","gist":"The as-built records what was actually placed, using the footage marks. Slack at a point is IN minus OUT; running line between points is the previous OUT minus this IN. The total must equal first IN minus last OUT.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"TIA-598 color order: blue, orange, green, brown, slate, white, red, black, yellow, violet, rose, aqua.","part":1},{"p":"Fiber number = (tube − 1) × 12 + color position, so fiber 137 is tube 12, position 5: slate in aqua.","part":1},{"p":"Bend radius: at least 20 times the cable diameter under tension and 10 times at rest.","part":2},{"p":"Slack = Sequential IN − Sequential Out at a point; running line = previous row's Sequential Out − this row's Sequential IN.","part":4},{"p":"TOTAL INSTALLED must equal the first Sequential IN minus the last Sequential Out; if the two disagree, a digit is wrong.","part":4}]},"p24m3":{"big":"A good joint, spliced or connectorized, is judged in tenths of a decibel, and it depends on a clean cleave, the right polish and a clean end face.","why":"Splicing and testing are the first big pay jump on the ladder, and the inspect–clean–inspect habit is the cheapest, most visible mark of a professional.","parts":[{"t":"Fusion splicing","gist":"A fusion splice melts two fiber ends into one continuous piece of glass with an electric arc. The order is strip, clean, cleave, align, arc, protect, and the cleave matters most: a flat end face within 0.5°.","core":true},{"t":"What good looks like","gist":"A good single-mode fusion splice loses 0.02–0.05 dB; TIA allows up to 0.3 dB per splice and 0.75 dB per mated connector pair. Mechanical splices are quick and need no power, but lose more and reflect some light.","core":true},{"t":"Connectors and polish","gist":"Connectors hold the fiber in a ceramic ferrule: SC for FTTH, LC for transceivers, MPO for many fibers. UPC (blue) is polished flat; APC (green) is angled at 8° so reflections leave the core. Never mate green to blue.","core":true},{"t":"Pigtails, field connectors and panels","gist":"The usual way to terminate outside-plant cable is to fusion-splice a factory-made pigtail, a short fiber with a connector, onto each field fiber. Field-installable and splice-on connectors are for when you must terminate on site.","core":true},{"t":"Inspect, clean, inspect","gist":"Dirty end faces are the number-one cause of fiber link failures, and dust is about the size of a single-mode core. Inspect with a video probe before every connection, clean with a one-click cleaner, then inspect again.","core":true},{"t":"On the exam","gist":"The CFOT and CFOS/S exams ask for these numbers as plain multiple choice: splice and connector limits, connector types, APC's 8° angle and green color, pigtail splicing and inspect–clean–inspect.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The fusion splice sequence is strip, clean, cleave, align, arc, protect; a single-mode cleave must be flat within 0.5°.","part":0},{"p":"The splicer's loss figure is only an estimate; only an OTDR or OLTS measures a splice.","part":0},{"p":"TIA maximums: 0.3 dB per splice and 0.75 dB per mated connector pair. Good single-mode fusion splices lose 0.02–0.05 dB.","part":1},{"p":"APC is polished at 8° and green; UPC is flat and blue. PON requires APC, and APC never mates to UPC.","part":2},{"p":"Inspect before you connect, every time: dry-clean with a one-click cleaner, re-inspect, and only if still dirty do a wet-dry pass.","part":4}]},"p24m4":{"big":"An OLTS loss test, judged against a loss budget, proves a link will carry the light, and an OTDR trace finds the exact spot where it won't.","why":"Testing turns craftsmanship into evidence. The person who can read a trace and defend a budget is the one engineers call, and it moves you from placing cable to accepting it.","parts":[{"t":"The four instruments","gist":"A red-laser VFL makes breaks and bends glow. A probe checks end faces. An OLTS (light source plus power meter) measures end-to-end loss for certification. An OTDR, from one end, locates every event along the fiber.","core":true},{"t":"Insertion loss with an OLTS: the reference is the test","gist":"Loss is the reference reading minus the measured reading, so what you include in the reference decides what you measure. The one-cord reference, which TIA-568 requires, includes both of the link's end connectors.","core":true},{"t":"The loss budget, end to end","gist":"Add TIA maximums for fiber, splices and connector pairs to get the most loss the link may show. Then check that the optic's power budget, minimum launch minus receiver sensitivity, covers that loss plus a 3 dB margin.","core":true},{"t":"The OTDR: radar for glass","gist":"An OTDR fires pulses and plots the faint light scattered back against distance. Splices and bends show as steps, connectors and cracks as spikes. Watch for ghosts and gainers, and use a launch cable to get past the dead zone.","core":true},{"t":"Documentation and standards","gist":"Acceptance is a package, not a feeling: per-fiber loss at each wavelength against the budget, saved OTDR traces, lengths, splice sheets, the as-built and TIA-606 labels, so every record maps to a physical port.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Certification needs an OLTS insertion-loss test with a one-cord reference; the OTDR locates events but does not certify the link.","part":1},{"p":"TIA-568.3 maximums: 0.5 dB/km for outside-plant single-mode, 0.3 dB per splice, 0.75 dB per mated connector pair.","part":2},{"p":"Power budget = transmitter minimum minus receiver sensitivity (6.2 dB for 10GBASE-LR); it must cover link loss plus a 3 dB margin.","part":2},{"p":"On a trace, a step with no spike is a fusion splice or bend; a spike followed by a step is a connector, mechanical splice or crack.","part":3},{"p":"A gainer is a backscatter artifact from dissimilar fibers, never added power: average both directions.","part":3}]},"p24m5":{"big":"The glass you place carries switch optics, home PON service and many wavelengths, and the product the ISP sells is the sum of your bend radii, splice losses and as-builts.","why":"It connects your handhole to the product the ISP sells and to the switches and GPU clusters of other domains, and it is the vocabulary Network+ and the CFOT both test.","parts":[{"t":"Ethernet over fiber: optics and reach","gist":"Switches take pluggable transceivers. The name gives the medium: SR is 850 nm multimode, LR 1310 nm single-mode to 10 km, ER 40 km, ZR 80 km. The form factor, such as SFP+ or QSFP28, gives the speed.","core":true},{"t":"FTTH and the passive optical network","gist":"A PON carries light from one OLT through an unpowered glass splitter to 32 or 64 homes, sending up and down on different wavelengths of one fiber. The splitter is the biggest loss: about 17.5 dB at 1:32.","core":true},{"t":"WDM: many colors, one fiber","gist":"Wavelength-division multiplexing sends separate signals on different colors down one fiber. CWDM uses 18 widely spaced channels with cheap lasers and no amplifiers; DWDM packs 40 to 96 tight channels with amplifiers. 400ZR puts DWDM in a router pluggable.","core":true},{"t":"Data centers: MPO trunks and polarity","gist":"Data centers need density: MPO trunks carry 12 or 24 fibers per connector, and polarity methods A, B and C make each transmit land on a receive. Mix methods and links light up but pass no traffic.","core":false},{"t":"From your segment to the product","gist":"Follow one subscriber from the OLT through your feeder cable and splices, the splitter, a terminal and a drop to the ONT in the house. The service the operator sells rests on your bend radii, splice losses and as-builts.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"10GBASE-SR is 850 nm multimode, 300 m on OM3 and 400 m on OM4; 10GBASE-LR is 1310 nm single-mode to 10 km.","part":0},{"p":"GPON sends 2.488 Gb/s down at 1490 nm and 1.244 Gb/s up at 1310 nm; XGS-PON sends 9.953 Gb/s each way at 1577/1270 nm.","part":1},{"p":"A 1:32 splitter costs about 17.5 dB, the biggest single loss in a PON budget, and it is passive glass with no power.","part":1},{"p":"CWDM: 18 channels 20 nm apart, uncooled lasers, no amplifiers. DWDM: 40 to 96 channels 0.8 or 0.4 nm apart, with EDFAs.","part":2},{"p":"MPO polarity follows TIA-568.3 Methods A, B and C; mixing methods gives links that light up and never pass traffic.","part":3}]},"p24m6":{"big":"Fiber's hazards are quiet, invisible light, invisible glass, gas in a manhole and a driver who doesn't see you, and each simple rule exists because someone was hurt.","why":"Safety is on every FOA exam and in every employer's orientation, and the same habits, dust caps, the sharps bottle, the checklist and the 811 number on the sheet, are what get a technician promoted.","parts":[{"t":"Light you cannot see","gist":"Telecom light is infrared, so a live fiber looks dark and your blink reflex never fires. Never look into a fiber or port. Check for light with a power meter, and inspect end faces only with a video probe.","core":true},{"t":"Glass you cannot see","gist":"A fiber scrap is a glass needle that passes through skin. Every scrap goes straight into a sharps container; wear safety glasses, work on a dark mat, and never eat or drink at the splice trailer. Isopropyl alcohol is flammable.","core":true},{"t":"Ground, height and traffic","gist":"Call 811 before digging and hand-dig near marks. Trenches need protection at five feet. Test a manhole's air before and during entry. On poles keep the required clearances from power, and on roads set up traffic control and wear hi-vis.","core":true},{"t":"Codes and standards that govern the plant","gist":"NEC Article 770 governs fiber cable indoors, the NESC governs poles, TIA-606 sets labeling and TIA-607 bonding. Metal armor and strand must be bonded, and a wrong label or as-built can send someone to cut the wrong fiber.","core":false},{"t":"Habits that promote technicians","gist":"Supervisors promote the person they stop worrying about: every end face inspected, slack racked, closures photographed, the as-built filled in the truck, the 811 ticket on the sheet, and 'let me check the print' instead of guessing.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Never look into a fiber, connector or port, live or 'dead': telecom light is infrared and invisible.","part":0},{"p":"Prove a fiber is dark with a power meter; any reading above its floor, around −60 dBm, means a source is on somewhere.","part":0},{"p":"Every cleaved scrap goes immediately into a labeled sharps container, never the trash, and no food or drink at the splice trailer.","part":1},{"p":"Call 811 before any digging. Trenches five feet or deeper need a protective system; four feet or deeper need a ladder within 25 feet.","part":2},{"p":"Test a manhole's air for oxygen, combustible gas, hydrogen sulfide and carbon monoxide before entry and continuously after.","part":2}]},"p24m7":{"big":"From the phone network to 5G, submarine cables and AI data centers, everything runs on light in glass, and there is a credentialed ladder from the handhole to engineering.","why":"It gives you the map and the price list: which credential buys which rung, and why your as-builts and test records are the portfolio the FOA and the next employer actually count.","parts":[{"t":"From PSTN to IP","gist":"The old phone network reserved a fixed time slot for every call. The internet shares capacity in packets instead. What rides the glass keeps changing, but attenuation, the loss budget and 4.9 µs per kilometer stay constant.","core":true},{"t":"The carrier network in three tiers","gist":"Carrier networks have three tiers: access (the last miles to homes and cell sites, your daily work), aggregation or metro rings across a city or region, and the long-haul core. A cut ring stays up; a cut spur does not.","core":true},{"t":"5G: the wireless network that is mostly fiber","gist":"A cell site is a fiber site. Fronthaul links the radio unit to the distributed unit within about 100 µs, which limits it to roughly 20 km of fiber. Every small cell also needs its own power and fiber.","core":true},{"t":"Cable's coax versus fiber","gist":"Cable's HFC runs fiber to a neighborhood node, then shared coax to a few hundred homes. Operators push fiber deeper and build FTTH, because XGS-PON is symmetric, cheaper to operate and has an upgrade path.","core":true},{"t":"Under the sea","gist":"Nearly all intercontinental data crosses on submarine cables, more than 600 systems. Repeaters every 60–100 km are powered by thousands of volts of DC from shore, and the most common fault is a trawler or anchor in shallow water.","core":false},{"t":"The ladder, and the stack","gist":"The climb runs technician, splicer, lead or QA, OSP designer, transport engineer, and each rung has a credential: CFOT, the CFOS specialties, BICSI, Network+, and Security+ for a security pivot. It is climbable without a degree.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Carrier networks have three tiers: access, aggregation/metro rings, and the long-haul core with amplifier huts every 80–100 km.","part":1},{"p":"In 5G, fronthaul runs from the radio unit (RU) to the distributed unit (DU); the DU then reaches the centralized unit (CU) over midhaul.","part":2},{"p":"5G fronthaul has a one-way budget near 100 µs; at 4.9 µs per km that allows roughly 20 km of fiber.","part":2},{"p":"Nearly all intercontinental traffic rides submarine cables; satellites carry under one percent of U.S. international capacity.","part":4},{"p":"The ladder: technician (CFOT) → splicer/tester (CFOS/S, CFOS/T) → lead/QA (CFOS/O) → OSP designer (CFOS/D, BICSI) → transport engineer (Network+, CCNA).","part":5}]},"p25m1":{"big":"Modern cryptography is public machinery plus private randomness: the algorithm is known to everyone, and all the secrecy sits in a truly random key.","why":"Every protocol you will test or defend rests on these assumptions, and the breaks you will actually find come from bad randomness and bad key handling, not from AES being too small.","parts":[{"t":"Kerckhoffs's principle: the enemy knows the system","gist":"A system must stay secure even when everything but the key is public. Secret algorithms always leak and can't be reviewed, while AES has been public since 1998 and still stands. All the secrecy belongs in the key.","core":true},{"t":"Threat models and adversaries","gist":"Nothing is secure in the abstract, only against a stated adversary. Name who they are (eavesdropper or active attacker), what they can see and what resources they have. Design against the strongest, and the weaker ones come free.","core":true},{"t":"Randomness and entropy","gist":"A key is only as unpredictable as the process that made it. Take keys from the operating system's secure generator, such as Python's secrets, never from rand() or Python's random, whose output attackers can predict.","core":true},{"t":"The one-time pad and perfect secrecy","gist":"Combine a message bit by bit (XOR) with a truly random key of equal length, used once, and the ciphertext reveals nothing. The cost: the key is as long as the message and can never be reused.","core":true},{"t":"Computational security and key sizes","gist":"Practical ciphers can be broken in principle but not in practice. Each extra key bit doubles the work: a 128-bit key would take the largest brute-force engine on Earth about ten billion years. 112 bits is the floor through 2030.","core":true},{"t":"From Caesar to Enigma to now","gist":"Old ciphers fell to structure an attacker could exploit, operator mistakes or faster hardware. A big key space alone doesn't save a weak design. AES remains unbroken; attacks target everything around it.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Kerckhoffs's principle: a system must remain secure even if everything about it except the key is public.","part":0},{"p":"Eve, the passive eavesdropper, only reads the wire; Mallory, the active attacker, can delete, replay, reorder and forge messages.","part":1},{"p":"Make keys with a CSPRNG such as secrets.token_bytes(32) or /dev/urandom, never with rand() or Python's random module.","part":2},{"p":"The one-time pad is perfectly secret only with a truly random key as long as the message, never reused: reuse gives c1 ⊕ c2 = m1 ⊕ m2.","part":3},{"p":"128 bits of security is the standard (AES-128, RSA-3072, P-256), and 112 bits is the floor through 2030.","part":4}]},"p25m2":{"big":"AES itself is not the weak point; safe encryption depends on the mode, never reusing a nonce, and authenticating before you decrypt.","why":"Almost every symmetric-encryption disaster of the last thirty years was a mode or nonce mistake; recognizing them is what you will be paid for in code reviews and pentests.","parts":[{"t":"AES in one page","gist":"AES scrambles 128-bit blocks with a 128-, 192- or 256-bit key over 10, 12 or 14 rounds of substitution and mixing. Modern CPUs run it in hardware, fast and in constant time. Nobody has broken it.","core":true},{"t":"Modes of operation","gist":"A mode decides how blocks are chained. ECB encrypts equal blocks identically, so patterns show (the penguin). CBC needs an unpredictable IV and padding; CTR makes a keystream. Neither proves integrity; GCM adds a tag and is the default.","core":true},{"t":"The nonce and the catastrophe","gist":"A nonce must never repeat under one key. In CTR and GCM, reuse repeats the keystream, exposing m1 ⊕ m2, and GCM tags become forgeable. Use counter nonces, stay under 2^32 random nonces per key, or a misuse-resistant design.","core":true},{"t":"Authenticated encryption: why unauthenticated CBC fails","gist":"AEAD encrypts and also refuses anything that was modified. Without it, a server that reveals whether the padding was bad becomes an oracle that leaks plaintext byte by byte. Check the tag before decrypting and the oracle disappears.","core":true},{"t":"ChaCha20-Poly1305: the other AEAD","gist":"ChaCha20-Poly1305 is an AEAD with the same interface as AES-GCM, but it needs no special CPU instructions. Use AES-GCM on hardware with AES instructions and ChaCha20-Poly1305 everywhere else; either is fine.","core":true},{"t":"Limits per key","gist":"Every key has a budget of messages and bytes. A 64-bit block cipher starts repeating blocks after about 32 GiB, which Sweet32 exploited; AES's 128-bit block pushes that limit far away. Protocols must rotate keys before the budget runs out.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Never use ECB for data: identical plaintext blocks give identical ciphertext blocks, so the structure leaks.","part":1},{"p":"Reusing a nonce under one key in CTR or GCM exposes m1 ⊕ m2; in GCM it also lets an attacker forge tags.","part":2},{"p":"Random 96-bit GCM nonces are safe only up to about 2^32 messages per key.","part":2},{"p":"Never act on a plaintext you have not authenticated: checking the tag before any decryption removes the padding oracle.","part":3},{"p":"Use AES-GCM where the CPU has AES instructions and ChaCha20-Poly1305 everywhere else.","part":4}]},"p25m3":{"big":"A cryptographic hash is a fingerprint that can't be reversed or forged; use HMAC to authenticate with it, and store passwords with a salt and a slow hash.","why":"Integrity and authentication are the I and A of the security triad made concrete, and password storage is the most common cryptographic decision in any application you will build or audit.","parts":[{"t":"Three properties and their price","gist":"A good hash resists three attacks: finding an input for a given digest, a second input matching a known one, or any matching pair. The last costs only about 2^n/2. MD5 and SHA-1 have both fallen to collisions.","core":true},{"t":"The birthday bound, with arithmetic","gist":"Repeats show up sooner than you'd expect: 23 people give a 50.7% chance of a shared birthday. For an n-bit hash, collisions appear around 2^n/2 tries, so 128-bit hashes are unacceptable and 256 bits is the minimum.","core":true},{"t":"SHA-2, SHA-3 and friends","gist":"SHA-2 is today's main family; SHA-3 is a sponge with a different structure, kept as insurance. SHA-2 allows length extension: knowing H(m), anyone can append to m and compute the new hash, so H(key‖message) is broken as authentication.","core":true},{"t":"HMAC and MACs","gist":"A MAC is a tag only key holders can make or check. HMAC nests two hashes with the key mixed in twice, which defeats length extension. Compare tags in constant time, and prefer an AEAD when you are also encrypting.","core":true},{"t":"Merkle trees: hashing a forest","gist":"A Merkle tree hashes data in pairs up to one root that commits to everything below. Proving one item belongs needs only about log2(n) sibling hashes. Git, Certificate Transparency and Bitcoin all rely on this.","core":true},{"t":"Passwords: the threat is the leaked database","gist":"Assume the user table will leak. Give each user a random salt so every guess must be redone per user, and use a slow, memory-hard hash like Argon2id tuned to about 100–500 ms. A pepper adds a server-side secret.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"By the birthday bound, collisions on an n-bit hash cost about 2^n/2 work, so SHA-256 gives 2^128 and MD5 only 2^64.","part":1},{"p":"H(key‖message) is a broken MAC on SHA-256 because of length extension; use HMAC or an AEAD instead.","part":2},{"p":"Verify MAC tags with a constant-time comparison such as hmac.compare_digest, never a byte-by-byte early-exit compare.","part":3},{"p":"A salt is public and unique per user (16 random bytes); a pepper is a secret kept outside the database, in an HSM or vault.","part":5},{"p":"Hash passwords with Argon2id, at least 19 MiB, two iterations and parallelism one by OWASP's minimum, never with plain SHA-256.","part":5}]},"p25m4":{"big":"Public-key cryptography rests on math that is easy one way and infeasible to reverse, like multiplying primes versus factoring, and real systems pair it with fast symmetric encryption.","why":"Every certificate, SSH key, VPN handshake and blockchain signature you will ever inspect is one of these ideas, and the key-size table is a standard interview question.","parts":[{"t":"Modular arithmetic in one page","gist":"Modular arithmetic is clock arithmetic: 17 mod 12 = 5. Division becomes multiplying by an inverse, which exists only when gcd(a, n) = 1. Square-and-multiply makes huge powers cheap, while recovering primes from their product stays hard.","core":true},{"t":"RSA with a tiny worked example","gist":"RSA multiplies two primes into n, picks a public exponent e, and derives a private exponent d from the primes. Encrypt with e; decrypt or sign with d. Whoever factors n can compute d, so RSA rests on factoring.","core":true},{"t":"Diffie–Hellman and the discrete logarithm","gist":"Two parties each keep a secret exponent, exchange public values, and arrive at the same shared secret; an eavesdropper would have to solve the discrete logarithm. Fresh keys each session give forward secrecy, but plain DH authenticates nobody.","core":true},{"t":"Elliptic curves, and why 256 bits match 3072","gist":"On good elliptic curves only a generic attack is known, needing about √n steps, while RSA and classic DH have shortcuts. So a 256-bit curve matches RSA-3072, with 32-byte keys and roughly ten times the speed.","core":true},{"t":"Signatures: ECDSA, Ed25519 and the nonce that killed a console","gist":"A signature proves the private-key holder endorsed exactly this message. ECDSA needs a fresh random value per signature; reusing it exposed Sony's PS3 key. Ed25519 derives that value deterministically and is the right choice for anything new.","core":true},{"t":"Hybrid encryption","gist":"Public-key operations are slow and carry little data, so real systems use them only to set up a fresh symmetric key, then protect the data with a fast AEAD. HPKE standardizes exactly this pattern.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Toy RSA: p = 61, q = 53 gives n = 3233, φ(n) = 3120, e = 17, d = 2753, and 65 encrypts to 2790.","part":1},{"p":"Knowing p and q gives φ(n) and therefore d, which is why RSA's security reduces to the difficulty of factoring n.","part":1},{"p":"Plain Diffie–Hellman binds no identity; an active attacker can run two exchanges, so signatures or certificates must authenticate it.","part":2},{"p":"128-bit security (NIST SP 800-57) means RSA or DH at 3072 bits, a 256-bit curve such as P-256 or X25519, or AES-128.","part":3},{"p":"Reusing ECDSA's per-signature nonce k exposes the private key; Ed25519 derives its nonce deterministically.","part":4}]},"p25m5":{"big":"Protocols combine primitives into private, authenticated connections, and they usually fail through replayed messages, unchecked identities or old cipher suites left enabled, not broken ciphers.","why":"These five protocols carry nearly all authenticated traffic you will defend or test, and reading a handshake in Wireshark or openssl s_client is a daily skill for a security engineer.","parts":[{"t":"TLS 1.3, step by step","gist":"TLS 1.3 finishes its handshake in one round trip: both sides send fresh key shares, compute a shared secret, and encrypt everything after ServerHello. The server signs the whole handshake to prove it holds the certificate's key.","core":true},{"t":"Certificates and PKI","gist":"A certificate, signed by a CA, binds a public key to a name and chains through an intermediate to a trusted root. ACME automates issuance, lifetimes fall to 47 days by 2029, and Certificate Transparency catches rogue certificates.","core":true},{"t":"HTTPS pitfalls","gist":"The common HTTPS mistakes are self-inflicted: verification turned off in code, no HSTS, TLS 1.0 and 1.1 still enabled, and self-signed internal certificates. Test your own servers. A padlock proves encryption to that name, not that the site is honest.","core":true},{"t":"SSH and WireGuard: trust without certificates","gist":"SSH trusts a server's key the first time and stores its fingerprint; a 'host identification has changed' warning is the man-in-the-middle alarm, never to bypass. Users log in with Ed25519 or FIDO2 keys, and passwords are disabled.","core":true},{"t":"Signal: the double ratchet and its post-quantum additions","gist":"Signal changes keys with every message, so a phone seized tomorrow can't read today's messages, and a stolen state heals after a reply. WireGuard makes one fixed choice of modern ciphers in about 4,000 lines, so nothing can be downgraded.","core":false},{"t":"Kerberos","gist":"Kerberos logs you in through a Key Distribution Center, which issues a ticket-granting ticket and then service tickets. Weak service-account passwords can be guessed offline from tickets, and whoever holds the krbtgt key can mint any ticket.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"A full TLS 1.3 handshake is one round trip, and everything after ServerHello, including the certificate, is encrypted.","part":0},{"p":"Certificate lifetimes are capped at 200 days from March 2026, 100 days from March 2027 and 47 days from March 2029, so automate with ACME.","part":1},{"p":"Never ship verify=False or curl -k to production: disabled verification is a trivial man-in-the-middle.","part":2},{"p":"Never bypass SSH's 'REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED' warning; verify the fingerprint through another channel.","part":3},{"p":"Kerberoasting guesses weak service-account passwords offline from service tickets, so service accounts need AES-only settings and long random passwords.","part":5}]},"p25m6":{"big":"A quantum computer would break today's public-key cryptography but only weaken symmetric ciphers, and because traffic recorded now can be decrypted later, key exchange must migrate first.","why":"The post-quantum migration is the largest cryptographic transition in history and will run through your entire career; being the person who can plan it is a durable advantage.","parts":[{"t":"Shor's algorithm: what breaks","gist":"Shor's algorithm solves factoring and discrete logarithms efficiently, so RSA, Diffie–Hellman and every elliptic-curve scheme fall completely, along with the TLS, SSH and code signing built on them. Estimates of the machine needed keep shrinking.","core":true},{"t":"Grover's algorithm: what bends","gist":"Grover's algorithm only speeds brute-force search to about √N steps, halving the exponent: AES-128 drops to 2^64 serial steps and AES-256 to 2^128. Symmetric ciphers and hashes survive; prefer 256-bit keys for long-lived data.","core":true},{"t":"NIST's standards: FIPS 203, 204, 205 and what is still coming","gist":"NIST's 2024 standards are ML-KEM for key exchange and ML-DSA for signatures, both lattice-based with kilobyte-sized keys, plus SLH-DSA, a hash-based signature kept as a conservative backup. FN-DSA and HQC are still unfinished.","core":true},{"t":"Hybrids: where post-quantum already runs","gist":"A hybrid key exchange combines a classical and a post-quantum secret, so it is safe if either survives. Hybrids are already the default in Chrome and OpenSSH 10.0 and run in Signal, costing roughly 2 KB per handshake.","core":true},{"t":"Harvest now, decrypt later","gist":"Adversaries can record encrypted traffic now and decrypt it once a quantum computer exists. Mosca's inequality: if the years your data must stay secret plus the years migration takes exceed the years until that machine, today's data will be read.","core":true},{"t":"Migration planning and the deadlines","gist":"Inventory every protocol, key, certificate and firmware signature; prioritize by how long the data must stay secret; and build crypto agility so the next change is a configuration change. NIST disallows quantum-vulnerable public-key algorithms after 2035.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Shor breaks RSA, Diffie–Hellman, ECDH/X25519, ECDSA and Ed25519; Grover only halves the exponent, so AES-256 and SHA-256 survive.","part":0},{"p":"FIPS 203 ML-KEM replaces key exchange; FIPS 204 ML-DSA and FIPS 205 SLH-DSA replace signatures. All three were published 13 August 2024.","part":2},{"p":"Mosca's inequality: if X (years data must stay secret) + Y (years to migrate) > Z (years to a quantum computer), today's data will be read.","part":4},{"p":"Migrate key exchange first, signatures second; long-lived firmware roots of trust are hardest of all.","part":4},{"p":"NIST IR 8547 deprecates 112-bit RSA and elliptic-curve algorithms after 2030 and disallows quantum-vulnerable public-key algorithms after 2035.","part":5}]},"p25m7":{"big":"Cryptography is usually bypassed, not broken: systems leak through keys stored beside the data, secrets in git, repeated nonces and vendor claims weaker than they sound.","why":"This is the module that turns knowledge into a job: key management, secrets hygiene and honest evaluation of crypto claims are what security teams hire for and what the CC certifies.","parts":[{"t":"Key management, rotation and hardware roots of trust","gist":"Keys have a lifecycle: generate, store, use, rotate, destroy. A root key wraps key-encryption keys, which wrap a data key per object, so rotation re-wraps small keys, not terabytes. An HSM stops key theft, not key misuse.","core":true},{"t":"Secrets in applications and CI","gist":"A secret in source code is public, because git history is permanent and scanners find it within minutes. Inject secrets from a secret manager, prefer short-lived credentials, use OIDC so CI holds no cloud keys, and rotate anything exposed.","core":true},{"t":"At rest, in transit, end to end","gist":"Each encryption layer stops a different attacker. Disk encryption stops a thief with the drive, TLS stops network eavesdroppers, and end-to-end stops the provider itself. None protects a compromised endpoint, so ask which threat each layer defeats.","core":true},{"t":"The classic mistakes","gist":"The same mistakes recur every year: rolling your own crypto, ECB, weak randomness, nonce reuse, timing leaks, hardcoded keys, and dead algorithms like MD5 and RC4. Use vetted libraries such as libsodium, Tink or age.","core":true},{"t":"Cryptography in blockchains, conceptually","gist":"Bitcoin is hash chains, Merkle trees and signatures assembled into a ledger. The cryptography guarantees the integrity of history and who authorized each transfer; it says nothing about price, legality, smart-contract bugs or an exchange's honesty.","core":false},{"t":"Evaluating a product's cryptographic claims","gist":"Look for named algorithms, a published protocol, dated third-party audits and a clear statement of who holds the keys. Be wary of 'military-grade', 'proprietary' or 'quantum-safe' with no algorithm named. The ISC2 CC exam mapping closes the domain.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Envelope encryption: a root key wraps KEKs and KEKs wrap per-object DEKs, so rotating a KEK re-wraps 32-byte keys, not the data.","part":0},{"p":"An HSM prevents key extraction, not key use: a compromised server with HSM access can still sign and decrypt.","part":0},{"p":"Use OIDC workload identity so CI holds no long-lived cloud keys; a secret that appears in a log or ticket must be rotated.","part":1},{"p":"'Encrypted at rest' with provider-held keys stops a thief with the disk, not SQL injection or an insider with database access.","part":2},{"p":"Ask any vendor: which algorithms and modes, who holds the keys, who audited it and when, what happens on loss, and the plan for 2030.","part":5}]},"p26m1":{"big":"IT runs as a machine of tiers, tickets, agreements and records, where priority comes from impact and urgency and every record type means something precise.","why":"You will walk into your first IT job already speaking its language, and interviewers test exactly these definitions.","parts":[{"t":"The service desk and its tiers","gist":"The service desk is the users' single point of contact, layered from self-service (Tier 0) through first contact (L1) and desktop support (L2) to engineers and vendors (L3). Escalation moves a ticket up for more skill or a management decision.","core":true},{"t":"Tickets, priority and SLAs","gist":"Priority is not the user's mood: it comes from impact times urgency in a matrix. The SLA promises customers response and resolution times per priority; an OLA is the same kind of promise made between IT teams.","core":true},{"t":"Four words that are not synonyms","gist":"An incident is an interruption you restore fast, even by workaround. A problem is its underlying cause; a diagnosed one is a known error. A change alters something that could affect services. A service request is a normal ask.","core":true},{"t":"ITIL: the vocabulary of the industry","gist":"ITIL is the most widely used IT service management framework, built on seven guiding principles, four dimensions and a six-activity value chain. ITIL Version 5 keeps the principles and 34 practices; ITIL 4 exams end 31 December 2027.","core":true},{"t":"CMDB, assets and on-call","gist":"A CMDB stores every configuration item, from servers to certificates, and how they connect, so you can tell which services die if one fails. Asset management tracks purchase, warranty and owner. On-call rotations answer P1s after hours.","core":true},{"t":"Where this lands on the exams","gist":"ITIL Foundation is this module, with many questions on definitions alone. Google IT Support and CompTIA A+ Core 2 also test the service desk, ticketing, documentation and change management.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Functional escalation sends a ticket to someone with more skill; hierarchical escalation tells a manager because a decision is needed.","part":0},{"p":"Priority = impact × urgency from the matrix; a typical P1 means a 15-minute response and a 4-hour resolution.","part":1},{"p":"An incident is restored, by workaround if needed; a problem is its cause, and a diagnosed problem awaiting a fix is a known error.","part":2},{"p":"Changes are standard (pre-approved, low risk), normal (assessed and authorized) or emergency (expedited, documented after).","part":2},{"p":"ITIL's four dimensions: organizations and people; information and technology; partners and suppliers; value streams and processes.","part":3}]},"p26m2":{"big":"Troubleshooting is controlled experimentation in six fixed steps, identify, theorize, test, fix, verify and document, changing one thing at a time.","why":"Troubleshooting is the skill every support interview tests live, and the same method is how you will later trace a vulnerability or a malware sample.","parts":[{"t":"The six steps","gist":"Always the same order: identify the problem, form a theory, test it, plan and implement the fix, verify and prevent, then document. Beginners usually fail at step one, fixing before they know the problem's scope.","core":true},{"t":"Isolating variables and reproducing","gist":"Change one thing at a time. Test the midpoint of a long path, compare with a known-good setup, swap suspect parts, and reproduce the fault before fixing it. Asking 'what changed?' solves more tickets than any tool.","core":true},{"t":"Reading logs","gist":"Logs tell you what happened; your job is to find the few lines that matter. Filter Windows Event Viewer by level, time and Event ID, use journalctl on Linux, and match timestamps to when the user says it broke.","core":true},{"t":"Escalation etiquette, documentation, and the human on the other end","gist":"Escalate within a 20–30 minute time-box, with symptom, scope, tests, evidence and your best hypothesis. Document fixes as cause, fix, verification. Give a stressed user a time and a name for the next update.","core":true},{"t":"Worked ticket 1 — \"The internet is down\"","gist":"A 169.254.x.x address means no DHCP lease. A new cable changed nothing, but the neighbor's jack worked, so the fault sat between jack and switch: a switch port shut down (err-disabled) after someone looped a cable.","core":true},{"t":"Worked ticket 2 — \"The printer won't print\"","gist":"Jobs stuck in the queue while the printer says Ready point to the print server's spooler. Stop the spooler, delete the stuck files, start it again. If that fails, check the printer's IP address, its port and the driver.","core":true},{"t":"Worked ticket 3 — \"I'm locked out again\"","gist":"Lockouts that return after a reset mean something keeps replaying an old password. Event ID 4740 on the domain controller names the caller computer; here a phone's mail profile still held last month's password.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"The six steps open A+ Core 1's troubleshooting domain and reappear on Network+; Google IT Support teaches the same approach as 'isolate the problem'.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"CompTIA's six steps, in order: identify the problem, establish a theory, test it, plan and implement, verify and prevent, document.","part":0},{"p":"Half-splitting a path of eight links takes three tests, not eight.","part":1},{"p":"Windows Event ID 4740 means an account was locked out and names the caller computer; 4625 is a failed logon.","part":2},{"p":"A good escalation gives the symptom, scope, what you tested and the results, evidence, impact and your best hypothesis.","part":3},{"p":"A 169.254.x.x (APIPA) address means the PC got no DHCP lease.","part":4}]}});
